// Animal Kingdom, more articles: pangolins, dugongs, sloths, otters, hyenas and leopards.
ADDART({id:"pangolins",t:"Pangolins",c:"Mammals",i:"🦔",w:"Pangolin",r:["ants","termite","biodivloss","conservation","palawan"],
p:[["Scaly mammals","Pangolins are the only mammals covered in hard scales."],["Ant eaters","They eat ants and termites with long, sticky tongues."],["Most trafficked","They are among the most illegally traded mammals in the world."]],
l:"Pangolins are shy, nocturnal mammals covered in overlapping scales made of keratin, the same material as human fingernails. There are eight species, four in Asia and four in Africa, including the Palawan pangolin, found only in the Philippines. Pangolins have no teeth; they use strong claws to break open ant and termite nests and catch insects with a sticky tongue that can be longer than their body. When threatened, they roll into a tight, armored ball. Pangolins are thought to be among the most trafficked mammals in the world, hunted for their meat and for their scales, which are used in some traditional medicines despite having no proven healing properties. All species are threatened, and international trade in them is banned.",
s:[["Armor of scales","A pangolin’s scales cover most of its body and make up a large share of its weight. When danger comes, it curls into a ball, protecting its soft belly. Its tough scales can defend it even from lions, which may roll it around but cannot bite through."],["Tongue and claws","Pangolins have no teeth. Their tongues can stretch up to about 40 centimeters, longer than their bodies in some species, and are anchored near the pelvis. They swallow small stones that help grind food in their stomachs. A pangolin may eat tens of millions of insects a year."],["Eight species","Asia has the Chinese, Sunda, Indian and Philippine, or Palawan, pangolins; Africa has the giant, ground, white-bellied and black-bellied pangolins. Some live mainly on the ground, digging burrows, while others climb trees using their prehensile tails."],["Life of a pangolin","Pangolins are solitary and mostly active at night. A mother usually has a single baby, which rides on the base of her tail and curls up with her when she rolls into a ball. Pangolins are hard to keep in captivity because of their specialized diet."]],
k:[["Species","Eight"],["Scales made of","Keratin"],["Diet","Ants and termites"],["Philippine species","Palawan pangolin"],["International trade","Banned since 2017"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the pangolin genus"],["1998","The Palawan pangolin is described as a separate species"],["2014","All pangolin species are listed as threatened"],["2016","Countries agree to ban international trade in pangolins"],["2019","The Palawan pangolin is listed as critically endangered"],["2020","China removes pangolin scales from its official list of traditional medicines"]],
s2:[["The Palawan pangolin","The Palawan pangolin, called balintong locally, lives only in Palawan and nearby islands. It is critically endangered because of poaching and habitat loss. Philippine authorities have seized large shipments of pangolins and scales, and conservation groups work with communities to protect the animals."],["Illegal trade","Pangolins are hunted for meat considered a delicacy in some places and for scales used in traditional remedies. Scientists say the scales have no medical benefit. Huge seizures, sometimes of several tonnes of scales, have been made in Asia and Africa."],["Protecting pangolins","In 2016 countries agreed under the CITES treaty to ban all commercial international trade in pangolins, which took effect in 2017. Conservation efforts include stronger law enforcement, campaigns to reduce demand, rescue and rehabilitation centers and community patrols."],["Pangolins and ecosystems","By eating huge numbers of ants and termites, pangolins help control insect populations, and their digging aerates soil. Their burrows can also provide shelter for other animals."],["Pangolins and disease","During the COVID-19 pandemic, scientists investigated whether pangolins might have played a role in spreading the virus, because related coronaviruses were found in smuggled pangolins. The evidence did not show that pangolins were the source, but the research highlighted risks from wildlife trade."],["World Pangolin Day","World Pangolin Day, held on the third Saturday of February, raises awareness about these little-known animals. Many people first learn on that day that pangolins exist and that they are in danger."]],
sp:["The armored ball","When a pangolin senses danger, it does something remarkable: it tucks its head into its belly, wraps its scaly tail around its body and becomes a tight, armored ball. Its scales have sharp edges, and the muscles holding it closed are so strong that a person can hardly pry it open. This defense works well against lions and leopards. Sadly, it is useless against human poachers, who can simply pick the curled-up animal off the ground. This is one reason pangolins are so vulnerable to illegal hunting."],
g:[["Pangolin","A scaly mammal that eats ants and termites"],["Keratin","A tough protein that forms scales, hair and nails"],["Nocturnal","Active at night"],["Trafficking","Illegal trade, for example in wildlife"]],
f:["Pangolins are the only scaly mammals.","They have no teeth.","The Palawan pangolin lives only in the Philippines."],
fun:"A pangolin’s tongue is attached near its hip bones and can be longer than its body.",
tl:"Ang pangolin o balintong ay mamalyang nababalutan ng kaliskis. Kumakain ito ng langgam at anay gamit ang mahabang dila. Isa ito sa pinakamaraming ipinupuslit na hayop, at ang Palawan pangolin ay nanganganib nang maubos."});

ADDART({id:"dugongs",t:"Dugongs and Manatees",c:"Sea Life",i:"🐋",w:"Dugong",r:["coral","whale","seaturtle","conservation","palawan"],
p:[["Gentle sea cows","Dugongs and manatees are large, slow marine mammals that graze on seagrass and water plants."],["Related to elephants","Their closest living relatives on land are elephants."],["Threatened","Habitat loss, fishing nets and boat strikes threaten them."]],
l:"Dugongs and manatees are large, plant-eating marine mammals known together as sirenians or sea cows. The dugong lives in warm coastal waters of the Indian and western Pacific oceans, including the Philippines, and feeds almost entirely on seagrass. Three species of manatees live in the Atlantic region, in coastal waters and rivers of the Americas and West Africa. Sirenians are slow, gentle and long-lived, and their closest relatives on land are elephants. They are thought to have inspired some old sailors’ tales of mermaids. Today all sirenians are vulnerable, threatened by the loss of seagrass beds, accidental capture in fishing nets, boat strikes and hunting. In the Philippines, dugongs are protected by law.",
s:[["Dugongs","Dugongs can grow about three meters long and weigh around 400 kilograms. They have a fluked tail like a whale and a downturned snout suited to grazing on the seafloor. They spend most of their time eating seagrass, pulling up whole plants and leaving feeding trails across seagrass meadows."],["Manatees","Manatees have rounded, paddle-shaped tails and live in the Caribbean, Florida, the Amazon River and West Africa. Some move between salt water and fresh water. They are sometimes seen in warm springs and near power plants in winter, where water stays warm."],["Life of a sea cow","Sirenians breathe air and surface every few minutes. They live long lives, sometimes 70 years for dugongs, but reproduce slowly, with females giving birth to one calf every few years. Calves stay with their mothers for a year or more."],["Seagrass gardeners","By grazing, dugongs help keep seagrass meadows healthy and spread their seeds. Seagrass beds are nurseries for fish, store carbon and protect coasts. Losing seagrass to pollution, dredging and coastal development is a major threat to dugongs."]],
k:[["Group name","Sirenians or sea cows"],["Diet","Seagrass and water plants"],["Closest land relatives","Elephants"],["Dugong length","About 3 meters"],["Philippine name","Dugong or baboy-dagat"]],
d:[["1741","Steller’s sea cow is discovered in the Bering Sea"],["1768","Steller’s sea cow is hunted to extinction"],["1973","Manatees are protected under the US Endangered Species Act"],["1982","Dugongs are listed as vulnerable"],["2001","The Philippines passes the Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act"],["2020s","Communities in Palawan monitor dugongs and protect seagrass beds"]],
s2:[["Dugongs in the Philippines","Dugongs live in scattered populations in the Philippines, with important areas around northern Palawan, including Busuanga and Coron, and parts of Mindanao. Local fishers and conservation groups monitor them and protect seagrass, and some communities have become dugong guardians. Dugong watching, done responsibly, supports local tourism."],["Mermaid legends","Sailors long ago may have mistaken dugongs or manatees, glimpsed at a distance holding calves to their chests, for mermaids. The scientific name of the group, Sirenia, comes from the sirens of Greek mythology. In 1493 Christopher Columbus wrote that he had seen mermaids that were not as beautiful as he had imagined, probably manatees."],["Steller’s sea cow","A giant relative, Steller’s sea cow, lived in the cold Bering Sea and could grow about eight meters long. It was discovered by European explorers in 1741 and hunted to extinction for its meat within 27 years, one of the fastest extinctions caused by humans."],["Threats","Dugongs and manatees drown when caught in fishing nets, are injured by boat propellers and lose food when seagrass dies. In some places they are still hunted. Pollution and algae blooms have caused large die-offs of manatees in Florida in recent years."],["Protection","Dugongs are protected under Philippine law, and killing or trading them is illegal. Protected areas, rules on fishing nets, boat speed limits and seagrass restoration help sirenians. International agreements also promote their conservation across their range."],["Elephants of the sea","Fossils show that sirenians evolved from four-legged land mammals about 50 million years ago, sharing ancestors with elephants and hyraxes. Like elephants, they have teeth that are replaced from the back of the jaw, and manatees have nails on their flippers resembling elephant toenails."]],
sp:["The dugong guardians of Busuanga","In the waters of northern Palawan, fishers who once saw dugongs only as accidental catches have become their protectors. Trained by conservation groups and local governments, some volunteer as wardens, monitoring dugongs and reporting threats. Visitors who join guided tours may glimpse a dugong surfacing for air before gliding back down to graze in the seagrass. Strict rules keep boats at a distance and forbid touching or chasing the animals. The program shows how protecting wildlife can also bring income to coastal communities."],
g:[["Sirenian","A member of the group that includes dugongs and manatees"],["Seagrass","A flowering plant that grows underwater in shallow seas"],["Fluke","The flat, horizontal tail of a dugong or whale"],["Vulnerable","At high risk of becoming endangered"]],
f:["Dugongs eat seagrass.","Their closest land relatives are elephants.","Steller’s sea cow went extinct in 1768."],
fun:"Christopher Columbus reported seeing mermaids in 1493 that were not as beautiful as described; they were probably manatees.",
tl:"Ang dugong at manatee ay malalaking mamalyang dagat na kumakain ng seagrass. Ang pinakamalapit nilang kamag-anak sa lupa ay ang elepante. May dugong sa Pilipinas, lalo na sa hilagang Palawan, at protektado ito ng batas."});

ADDART({id:"sloths",t:"Sloths",c:"Mammals",i:"🦥",w:"Sloth",r:["amazon","amazonriver","monkeys","animalbehavior","extinction"],
p:[["Slow movers","Sloths are tree-dwelling mammals famous for moving very slowly."],["Upside-down life","They spend most of their lives hanging upside down in rainforest trees."],["Giant ancestors","Extinct ground sloths were as big as elephants."]],
l:"Sloths are slow-moving mammals that live in the tropical rainforests of Central and South America. There are six living species, divided into two-toed and three-toed sloths, named after the number of claws on their front limbs. Sloths spend almost their entire lives hanging upside down from branches, where they eat, sleep, mate and even give birth. They move slowly because their diet of leaves provides little energy and takes a long time to digest. Algae grow in their fur, giving them a greenish tint that helps camouflage them. Their slow, quiet lifestyle makes them hard for predators to notice. Thousands of years ago, giant ground sloths as large as elephants roamed the Americas.",
s:[["Two-toed and three-toed","Both groups actually have three toes on their back feet; the names refer to the claws on their front limbs. Two-toed sloths are somewhat larger and eat leaves, fruit and sometimes insects, while three-toed sloths eat mostly leaves and have a face that looks like it is smiling."],["Why so slow","Sloths eat leaves, which are low in energy and hard to digest. Their many-chambered stomachs may take days or even weeks to digest a meal. To save energy, sloths have a slow metabolism, little muscle and a low body temperature, and they move only a few meters a minute at most."],["A hanging life","Sloths’ long, curved claws hook over branches, and special tendons let them hang without effort, even while asleep. Some of their organs are attached to their ribs so that they do not press on their lungs while upside down. They can turn their heads almost all the way around."],["A walking ecosystem","Sloth fur grows in the opposite direction to that of most mammals, so rain runs off when they hang upside down. Grooves in their hairs host algae, as well as moths, beetles and fungi. The algae give camouflage and may provide some nutrients."]],
k:[["Living species","Six"],["Home","Central and South American rainforests"],["Diet","Mostly leaves"],["Famous trait","Very slow movement"],["Extinct relatives","Giant ground sloths"]],
d:[["c. 35 million years ago","Early sloths appear in South America"],["c. 10,000 years ago","Giant ground sloths die out in the Americas"],["1796","Georges Cuvier describes the giant ground sloth Megatherium"],["1990s","Sloth sanctuaries begin rescuing injured animals in Costa Rica"],["2014","A study explains how sloths’ fur moths and algae help each other"],["2010s","Sloths become popular symbols of a relaxed lifestyle online"]],
s2:[["Giant ground sloths","Extinct relatives of sloths, such as Megatherium, could weigh several tonnes and reach about six meters when standing on their hind legs. They lived on the ground and browsed on trees. They disappeared about 10,000 years ago, around the time humans spread through the Americas and the climate changed."],["The weekly trip down","Sloths climb down from their trees only about once a week, to relieve themselves at the base of the tree. This is risky, because they are vulnerable on the ground. Scientists have suggested reasons, such as fertilizing their tree or delivering the moths that live in their fur to lay eggs in the dung."],["Surprisingly good swimmers","Although slow on land, sloths are good swimmers. They use their long arms to paddle and can slow their heart rate to hold their breath for a long time underwater. They sometimes swim across rivers to reach new trees."],["Threats","Sloths are threatened by deforestation, power lines, road traffic, dogs and the illegal pet trade. One species, the pygmy three-toed sloth, lives only on a tiny island off Panama and is critically endangered. Rescue centers care for injured and orphaned sloths."],["Sloths and people","Sloths have become popular on social media and in films as symbols of relaxation. Conservationists warn that taking selfies with captive sloths in tourist attractions can stress the animals and encourage their capture from the wild."],["Name and meaning","The word sloth also means laziness, one of the seven deadly sins in Christian tradition. Scientists point out that sloths are not lazy but well adapted to an energy-saving lifestyle that has helped them survive for millions of years."]],
sp:["The slowest trip to the bathroom","For a sloth, the weekly trip to the forest floor is a major event. Slowly, it climbs down its tree, digs a small hole with its tail, relieves itself and covers it up, then climbs back up. A sloth can lose a large share of its body weight in one go. This journey takes precious energy and exposes it to predators such as jaguars. Scientists are still not completely sure why sloths take this risk rather than simply letting their droppings fall from the branches, and the question has inspired several research studies."],
g:[["Sloth","A slow-moving tree mammal of the Americas"],["Metabolism","The chemical processes that use energy in the body"],["Camouflage","Coloring that helps an animal blend into its surroundings"],["Megatherium","An extinct giant ground sloth"]],
f:["There are six living species of sloth.","Algae grow in sloth fur.","Giant ground sloths were as big as elephants."],
fun:"Sloths can hold their breath underwater for a long time by slowing their heart rate.",
tl:"Ang sloth ay mamalyang mabagal kumilos na nakatira sa mga puno sa rainforest ng Amerika. Halos buong buhay nito ay nakabitin nang patiwarik. May algae sa balahibo nito na nagsisilbing pagbabalatkayo."});

ADDART({id:"otters",t:"Otters",c:"Mammals",i:"🦦",w:"Otter",r:["sea","rodents","wetlands","palawan","conservation"],
p:[["Playful swimmers","Otters are playful, fish-eating mammals that live in rivers, lakes and seas."],["Sea otters","Sea otters use stones as tools and hold hands while sleeping."],["In the Philippines","The Asian small-clawed otter lives in Palawan."]],
l:"Otters are carnivorous mammals in the weasel family, adapted for life in and around water. There are 13 species, living in rivers, lakes, wetlands and coastal seas on every continent except Australia and Antarctica. Otters have long, streamlined bodies, webbed feet and thick, waterproof fur, which keeps them warm. They feed mainly on fish, crabs, shellfish and other water animals. Otters are known for their playful behavior, such as sliding down muddy banks. Sea otters, which live in the North Pacific, use stones to crack open shellfish and are a key species that keeps kelp forests healthy. In the Philippines, the Asian small-clawed otter, the smallest otter species, lives in Palawan.",
s:[["Built for water","Otters have streamlined bodies, strong tails for swimming and webbed feet. They can close their ears and nostrils underwater. Their whiskers detect movement in murky water, helping them find prey."],["The thickest fur","Sea otters have the densest fur of any animal, with up to around a million hairs per square centimeter in places. Unlike whales and seals, they have no thick layer of blubber, so they rely on air trapped in their fur to stay warm and spend much time grooming."],["Tool users","Sea otters float on their backs and use stones to crack open clams, sea urchins and mussels on their bellies. They often keep a favorite stone tucked in a pouch of loose skin under their arm. They are among the few mammals besides primates known to use tools."],["Social life","Some otters live alone, while others, such as giant otters and small-clawed otters, live in family groups. Otters communicate with whistles, chirps and squeaks. Sea otters sometimes hold paws or wrap themselves in kelp while sleeping so they do not drift apart."]],
k:[["Species","13"],["Family","Weasel family (mustelids)"],["Densest fur","Sea otter"],["Smallest species","Asian small-clawed otter"],["Largest species","Giant otter of South America"]],
d:[["1741","European explorers encounter sea otters in the North Pacific"],["1911","An international treaty protects sea otters from fur hunting"],["1938","A small group of sea otters is rediscovered off California"],["1970s","Studies show sea otters protect kelp forests by eating sea urchins"],["2010s","Smooth-coated otters thrive in the city waterways of Singapore"],["2015","The Asian small-clawed otter is listed as vulnerable"]],
s2:[["Otters in the Philippines","The Asian small-clawed otter, known locally as dungon, is the only otter found in the Philippines and lives in Palawan’s rivers, mangroves and wetlands. Weighing only a few kilograms, it uses its nimble, almost clawless fingers to feel for crabs and shellfish. It is threatened by habitat loss, pollution and the illegal pet trade."],["Sea otters and kelp forests","Sea otters eat sea urchins, which graze on kelp. Where otters were hunted out, urchin numbers exploded and destroyed kelp forests. Where otters returned, kelp recovered, along with the many fish and animals that live in it. Ecologists call sea otters a keystone species."],["Saved from the fur trade","Sea otters were hunted almost to extinction for their luxurious fur in the 1700s and 1800s. An international treaty in 1911 ended commercial hunting. Small surviving groups slowly recovered, though populations in some areas remain threatened by oil spills and disease."],["Giant otters","Giant otters of the Amazon can grow nearly two meters long, including the tail. They live in noisy family groups, hunt fish together and defend their territories from other groups and even caimans."],["City otters","In Singapore, families of smooth-coated otters have made the city’s rivers and reservoirs their home, becoming local celebrities with their own fan groups. Their return is seen as a sign of cleaner waterways, though residents sometimes complain when otters raid fish ponds."],["Otters and pets","Videos of pet otters have become popular online, but conservationists warn that the demand fuels illegal capture from the wild, especially of small-clawed otters in Asia. Otters need water, space and the company of other otters, and do not make good pets."]],
sp:["Holding hands at sea","Sea otters spend much of their lives floating on their backs in the cold waters of the North Pacific. When they rest, groups called rafts gather together, and some otters hold paws or wrap strands of kelp around their bodies like anchors so they do not drift apart while sleeping. Mothers carry their fluffy pups on their bellies, grooming them and keeping them out of the cold water. Photos of hand-holding sea otters have charmed people around the world and helped draw attention to the need to protect their ocean home."],
g:[["Otter","A water-loving mammal in the weasel family"],["Keystone species","A species that has a large effect on its ecosystem"],["Kelp","Large brown seaweed that forms underwater forests"],["Raft","A group of resting sea otters"]],
f:["There are 13 otter species.","Sea otters use stones as tools.","Palawan is home to the Asian small-clawed otter."],
fun:"Sea otters often keep a favorite stone tucked in a skin pouch under their arm to use for cracking shellfish.",
tl:"Ang otter ay mapaglarong mamalyang nakatira sa ilog, lawa at dagat. Gumagamit ng bato ang sea otter upang buksan ang kabibe. Sa Pilipinas, ang Asian small-clawed otter ay matatagpuan sa Palawan."});

ADDART({id:"hyenas",t:"Hyenas",c:"Mammals",i:"🐾",w:"Hyena",r:["lion","grasslands","animalbehavior","cheetah","wolf"],
p:[["Skilled hunters","Hyenas are powerful carnivores of Africa and parts of Asia."],["Not just scavengers","Spotted hyenas hunt most of their own food."],["Female leaders","In spotted hyena clans, females are in charge."]],
l:"Hyenas are carnivorous mammals found across Africa and parts of the Middle East and South Asia. There are four species: the spotted hyena, the striped hyena, the brown hyena and the aardwolf, which eats mainly termites. Although they look somewhat like dogs, hyenas are more closely related to cats and mongooses. Hyenas are often portrayed as cowardly scavengers, but spotted hyenas are skilled hunters that catch most of their own food, often working in groups to bring down large animals such as wildebeest and zebras. Their powerful jaws can crush bones, which they digest completely. Spotted hyenas live in large clans led by females and communicate with a range of calls, including the famous laugh.",
s:[["Four species","The spotted hyena is the largest and most social. Striped hyenas live in northern and eastern Africa, the Middle East and India and are more solitary. Brown hyenas live in dry southern Africa. The small aardwolf eats termites, licking up hundreds of thousands in a night."],["Bone-crushing jaws","Hyenas have some of the strongest bites of any mammal, able to crack open large bones to reach the marrow. Their digestive systems can process bones, hooves and even teeth, leaving droppings that turn white from the calcium."],["Hunters and scavengers","Spotted hyenas hunt animals ranging from gazelles to wildebeest and zebras, chasing them over long distances. They also scavenge, and both hyenas and lions steal food from each other. Their reputation as mere scavengers is a myth."],["Clan life","Spotted hyenas live in clans that can number dozens of members, with a strict ranking. Females are larger and dominant over males, and cubs inherit their mother’s rank. Clan members greet each other with special rituals and cooperate to defend territory."]],
k:[["Species","Four"],["Largest species","Spotted hyena"],["Closest relatives","Mongooses and cats"],["Termite-eating hyena","Aardwolf"],["Clan leaders","Females"]],
d:[["c. 25 million years ago","Early hyena ancestors appear"],["c. 10,000 years ago","Cave hyenas die out in Europe and Asia"],["1777","The spotted hyena is described scientifically"],["1960s","Field studies reveal spotted hyenas as skilled hunters"],["1994","The Lion King portrays hyenas as villains"],["2010s","Long-term studies in Kenya reveal the complexity of hyena societies"]],
s2:[["The hyena’s laugh","Spotted hyenas make a range of sounds, including whoops that carry for kilometers and a high-pitched giggle often called laughing. The giggle usually happens when hyenas are excited or frustrated, for example when competing for food. Scientists have found that the calls carry information about age and status."],["Rivalry with lions","Lions and spotted hyenas compete for the same prey and frequently steal kills from each other. Large groups of hyenas can drive lions away, while male lions often kill hyenas. This rivalry is one of the most dramatic relationships on the African savanna."],["Cave hyenas","During the last ice age, cave hyenas, larger relatives of the spotted hyena, lived across Europe and Asia. They left behind bones in caves, along with the remains of animals they ate. Ancient people painted them on cave walls."],["Myths and reputation","Hyenas have often been feared or disliked. In some African folklore they are linked with witchcraft, and in films they are cast as villains. Researchers argue that hyenas are intelligent, social animals that play an important role in cleaning up carcasses and keeping ecosystems healthy."],["Intelligence","Spotted hyenas have shown impressive problem-solving and social intelligence, cooperating to solve puzzles and recognizing relationships among clan members. Some scientists compare their social skills to those of primates."],["Threats","Hyenas are killed by people who see them as threats to livestock, and their habitats are shrinking. Striped and brown hyenas are classed as near threatened. Spotted hyenas remain common in protected areas such as the Serengeti but have declined elsewhere."]],
sp:["Queens of the clan","In a spotted hyena clan, even the lowest-ranking adult female outranks the highest-ranking male. Females are larger and more aggressive, and young hyenas take on their mother’s place in the hierarchy, supported by her allies. When a new male joins a clan from outside, he starts at the bottom and must win acceptance. Scientists studying hyenas in Kenya for decades have found that a cub’s social connections, inherited from its mother, strongly influence its chances of survival and success."],
g:[["Hyena","A carnivorous mammal of Africa and Asia"],["Clan","A large group of spotted hyenas"],["Scavenger","An animal that eats dead animals"],["Aardwolf","A small, termite-eating member of the hyena family"]],
f:["There are four hyena species.","Spotted hyenas hunt most of their food.","Female spotted hyenas lead their clans."],
fun:"Hyena droppings can turn white because their bodies digest the calcium from the bones they eat.",
tl:"Ang hyena ay mabangis na mamalya ng Africa at bahagi ng Asya. Mahusay na mangangaso ang spotted hyena at hindi lamang kumakain ng patay na hayop. Pinamumunuan ng mga babae ang kanilang mga grupo."});

ADDART({id:"leopards",t:"Leopards",c:"Mammals",i:"🐆",w:"Leopard",r:["tiger","lion","cheetah","cats","amazon"],
p:[["Spotted big cats","Leopards are powerful big cats with spotted coats called rosettes."],["Masters of adaptation","They live in more kinds of places than any other big cat."],["Tree climbers","Leopards often drag their prey up into trees."]],
l:"The leopard is a large wild cat found across much of Africa and parts of Asia, from deserts and grasslands to rainforests and mountains. It has a golden coat covered with dark spots grouped into rosettes, which provide camouflage in dappled light. Leopards are strong, solitary and secretive hunters that stalk prey at night, catching animals from insects to antelopes. They are excellent climbers and often haul their kills into trees, out of reach of lions and hyenas. Leopards are the most widespread big cat, but they have disappeared from much of their former range because of habitat loss and hunting, and some subspecies, such as the Amur leopard, are critically endangered. Black leopards, often called black panthers, have dark coats due to a genetic variation.",
s:[["Spots and rosettes","A leopard’s spots are arranged in rose-shaped clusters on its body, while its head and legs have solid spots. Each leopard’s pattern is unique. The pattern helps it blend into grass, shadows and leafy branches."],["Hunting","Leopards stalk silently and pounce from close range, killing with a bite to the neck or throat. They eat a wide variety of animals, including antelopes, deer, monkeys, birds, rodents and fish. This flexible diet helps them survive in many habitats."],["Tree larders","Leopards are extremely strong for their size and can carry a kill heavier than themselves up a tree. Storing food in branches keeps it away from lions, hyenas and other competitors. They also rest in trees during the day."],["Black panthers","Some leopards have a genetic variation called melanism that makes their coats very dark, although their rosettes can still be seen in certain light. These black leopards are more common in dense, shady forests, such as in Southeast Asia."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Panthera pardus"],["Range","Africa and Asia"],["Coat pattern","Rosettes"],["Dark form","Black panther"],["Critically endangered subspecies","Amur leopard"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the leopard"],["1990s","Fewer than about 50 Amur leopards are thought to survive"],["2000s","Camera traps begin monitoring Amur leopards in Russia"],["2012","Russia creates Land of the Leopard National Park"],["2016","The leopard is reclassified as vulnerable"],["2019","A rare black leopard is photographed in Kenya"]],
s2:[["The most widespread big cat","Leopards live across sub-Saharan Africa and in scattered populations in North Africa, the Middle East, India, Sri Lanka, China, Southeast Asia and the Russian Far East. They can survive near villages and even on the edges of cities, such as Mumbai in India, where they live in a national park surrounded by the city."],["The Amur leopard","The Amur leopard lives in the cold forests of the Russian Far East and northeastern China. By the 1990s fewer than about 50 remained. Protection, including the creation of Land of the Leopard National Park, has helped numbers rise to around a hundred or more, though it remains critically endangered."],["Leopards and people","Leopards sometimes kill livestock or, rarely, attack people, especially where their habitat has been destroyed. Conflict leads to leopards being killed. Programs that protect livestock, compensate farmers and educate communities help reduce conflict."],["Leopard relatives","The snow leopard of Central Asia’s mountains and the clouded leopard of Southeast Asia’s forests share the name but are different species. The clouded leopard has the longest canine teeth relative to its size of any cat. Neither lives in the Philippines today."],["Leopards and cheetahs","Leopards and cheetahs are often confused. Cheetahs are slimmer, built for speed, with solid round spots and black tear lines on their faces, and hunt by day. Leopards are stockier, with rosettes, and are stealthy night hunters and climbers."],["Symbol and fashion","Leopards appear in heraldry, mythology and art, symbolizing strength and stealth. Leopard skins were once highly prized, and leopard print remains popular in fashion, now usually on synthetic fabrics. International trade in leopard skins is restricted."]],
sp:["A leopard in the city","In Mumbai, one of the world’s largest cities, leopards live in Sanjay Gandhi National Park, a forest surrounded by apartment blocks and roads. At night, leopards sometimes wander into neighborhoods, hunting stray dogs and occasionally appearing on security cameras. Researchers have worked with residents to reduce conflicts, teaching people to keep areas clean, light pathways and supervise children at night. The story shows both the adaptability of leopards and the challenges of sharing space with large predators as cities grow."],
g:[["Leopard","A large spotted wild cat of Africa and Asia"],["Rosette","A rose-shaped cluster of spots"],["Melanism","A genetic variation that causes dark coloring"],["Camera trap","A camera that photographs animals automatically"]],
f:["Leopards are the most widespread big cat.","Black panthers are often leopards with dark coats.","Leopards drag prey up trees."],
fun:"A leopard can carry a carcass heavier than itself several meters up a tree.",
tl:"Ang leopardo ay malaking pusang may batik na tinatawag na rosette. Ito ang pinakamalawak ang nasasakupang malaking pusa sa Africa at Asya. Madalas nitong iakyat sa puno ang nahuling hayop."});

;
/* data/animals13.js */
// Animal Kingdom, more articles: zebras, the carabao, the tamaraw, vultures, pigeons and doves, and rays.
ADDART({id:"zebras",t:"Zebras",c:"Mammals",i:"🦓",w:"Zebra",r:["horses","grasslands","lion","giraffe","palawan"],
p:[["Striped horses","Zebras are African members of the horse family with bold black-and-white stripes."],["Every pattern unique","No two zebras have exactly the same stripes."],["Fly protection","Scientists think the stripes help keep biting flies away."]],
l:"Zebras are wild members of the horse family that live in the grasslands, savannas and mountains of eastern and southern Africa. There are three living species: the plains zebra, the most common; Grévy’s zebra, the largest; and the mountain zebra. Zebras are famous for their black-and-white stripes, which are unique to each animal, like fingerprints. They live in herds and graze mainly on grasses. Plains zebras take part in the great migration across the Serengeti and Masai Mara alongside millions of wildebeest. Scientists have long debated why zebras have stripes; current evidence suggests the stripes help deter biting flies. One type of zebra, the quagga, was hunted to extinction in the 1800s.",
s:[["Three species","Plains zebras have broad stripes and live in large numbers across eastern and southern Africa. Grévy’s zebras, found in Kenya and Ethiopia, have narrow stripes and large rounded ears and are endangered. Mountain zebras live in rugged areas of South Africa, Namibia and Angola."],["Why stripes?","Ideas have included camouflage, confusing predators, keeping cool and helping zebras recognize each other. Experiments in which horses wore striped coats found that biting flies had trouble landing on stripes, and many scientists now think fly protection is the main reason."],["Herd life","Plains zebras live in family groups of a stallion with several mares and their young, which join together into large herds. When a predator attacks, zebras flee together, and their swirling stripes may make it hard to pick out one animal. Zebras also kick and bite fiercely to defend themselves."],["The great migration","Every year, hundreds of thousands of zebras move with wildebeest and gazelles across the Serengeti in Tanzania and the Masai Mara in Kenya, following rains and fresh grass. Zebras often lead, eating tougher grass tops and leaving shorter grass for wildebeest."]],
k:[["Species","Three"],["Family","Horse family"],["Largest species","Grévy’s zebra"],["Extinct form","Quagga"],["Stripes","Unique to each zebra"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus describes the zebra"],["1883","The last known quagga dies in an Amsterdam zoo"],["1977","Zebras and giraffes are brought from Kenya to Calauit Island in Palawan"],["1986","Grévy’s zebra is listed as endangered"],["1987","The Quagga Project begins breeding zebras to resemble the quagga"],["2019","A study with striped horse coats supports the fly-protection theory"]],
s2:[["Black with white stripes?","Scientists studying zebra embryos and skin have found that zebras have dark skin and that their fur color develops from pigment cells; many describe them as dark animals with white stripes. Either way, the pattern is unique to each individual."],["The quagga","The quagga was a zebra of South Africa with stripes only on the front of its body and a brown rear. It was hunted to extinction, and the last known animal died in an Amsterdam zoo in 1883. DNA studies showed it was a type of plains zebra, and a project in South Africa has bred plains zebras to resemble it."],["Zebras in the Philippines","In 1977 zebras, giraffes and other African animals were brought from Kenya to Calauit Island in Palawan, which became the Calauit Safari Park. Their descendants still live there alongside endemic Philippine animals such as the Calamian deer. The park has been both a tourist attraction and the subject of debate over displacement of local residents when it was created."],["Zebras and horses","Zebras have never been widely domesticated, unlike horses and donkeys. They are more nervous, aggressive and unpredictable. A few people in history trained zebras to pull carriages, but it never became common."],["Zebra crossings","The black-and-white striped pedestrian crossings found on roads in many countries, including the Philippines, are called zebra crossings because they resemble the animal’s stripes. They were introduced in Britain in the late 1940s and early 1950s."],["Threats","Plains zebras remain common in protected areas, but their numbers have declined in some places. Grévy’s zebras number only a few thousand, threatened by competition with livestock for water and grass, hunting and drought. Mountain zebras have recovered from very low numbers thanks to protection."]],
sp:["African animals on a Philippine island","On Calauit Island in northern Palawan, visitors are sometimes surprised to see zebras grazing and giraffes browsing among Philippine trees. In 1977 a shipment of African animals arrived from Kenya, and the island was turned into a game reserve. Over the decades, the zebras and giraffes adapted and multiplied, while rangers also cared for endangered native species such as the Calamian deer and the Philippine mouse-deer. Today the park is an unusual blend of African savanna and Philippine island, though its history, including the relocation of residents, remains debated."],
g:[["Zebra","A striped wild member of the horse family"],["Herd","A group of grazing animals"],["Migration","A seasonal journey of animals"],["Quagga","An extinct type of zebra"]],
f:["There are three zebra species.","Each zebra has unique stripes.","Zebras live on Calauit Island in Palawan."],
fun:"Zebra crossings on roads are named after the animal’s black-and-white stripes.",
tl:"Ang zebra ay ligaw na kamag-anak ng kabayo sa Africa na may itim at puting guhit. Natatangi ang guhit ng bawat zebra. May mga zebra rin sa Calauit Island sa Palawan na dinala mula sa Kenya noong 1977."});

ADDART({id:"carabao",t:"The Carabao",c:"Mammals",i:"🐃",w:"Carabao",r:["rice","domestication","horses","tamaraw","phfood"],
p:[["The farmer’s friend","The carabao is a domestic water buffalo that has long worked Philippine rice fields."],["Strong and patient","It pulls plows and carts and loves wallowing in mud."],["More than a work animal","Today it is also raised for milk, cheese and meat."]],
l:"The carabao is a type of domestic swamp buffalo found in the Philippines and other parts of Southeast Asia. For centuries it has been the farmer’s most important helper, pulling plows through flooded rice paddies, hauling carts and carrying loads. Strong, patient and hard-working, the carabao has become a cherished symbol of the Filipino farmer and is widely regarded as the national animal of the Philippines, though this has not been set in law. Carabaos keep cool by wallowing in mud and water, since they have few sweat glands. With the spread of farm machinery, fewer carabaos work the fields, but government programs now breed them for milk, used to make kesong puti and pastillas, as well as for meat.",
s:[["A swamp buffalo","Carabaos belong to the swamp type of water buffalo, adapted to wet, muddy farmland. They have large, backward-curving horns, gray to black skin and sparse hair. Adult carabaos can weigh several hundred kilograms and work for many years."],["Working the fields","For generations, Filipino farmers relied on carabaos to plow and harrow rice fields, pull sledges and carts, and carry harvests. A carabao and farmer working together in a muddy paddy is one of the most familiar images of rural Philippine life."],["Keeping cool","Carabaos have relatively few sweat glands and dark skin that absorbs heat, so they cool off by wallowing in mud and water, sometimes for hours. The mud also protects their skin from insects and the sun."],["Milk and meat","Carabao milk is rich and creamy, with more fat than cow’s milk. It is used to make kesong puti, a soft white cheese, pastillas de leche and other treats. The Philippine Carabao Center breeds carabaos with dairy buffalo breeds to increase milk production and support farmers’ incomes."]],
k:[["Type","Domestic swamp buffalo"],["Main traditional work","Plowing rice fields"],["Products","Milk, cheese, meat, leather"],["Government agency","Philippine Carabao Center (since 1992)"],["Famous festival","Carabao Festival of Pulilan, Bulacan"]],
d:[["c. 5,000 years ago","Water buffalo are domesticated in Asia"],["c. 1,000s","Water buffalo are used in rice farming in the Philippine islands"],["1992","The Philippine Carabao Act creates the Philippine Carabao Center"],["2000s","Crossbreeding programs increase dairy carabao production"],["2010s","Carabao milk products become popular across the Philippines"],["2020s","Government programs promote carabao-based enterprises for farmers"]],
s2:[["Symbol of hard work","The carabao is often used as a symbol of the patient, hard-working Filipino. It appears on old coins, in paintings, in folk songs and in children’s stories. The Philippine national hero José Rizal and many artists depicted rural scenes with carabaos."],["Carabao festivals","In Pulilan, Bulacan, farmers celebrate the feast of San Isidro Labrador, patron of farmers, each May with a carabao festival in which decorated carabaos parade and kneel before the church. In Angono and other towns, similar festivities honor farmers and their animals."],["Machines and change","Since the late 20th century, hand tractors and other machines have replaced many carabaos in the fields, especially in lowland farms. Carabaos remain important in mountainous and remote areas where machines cannot easily go, and for small farmers who cannot afford tractors."],["The Philippine Carabao Center","Created by law in 1992, the Philippine Carabao Center conserves and improves carabaos, runs breeding programs using artificial insemination, and helps farmers start dairy enterprises. It has research centers across the country, including its national headquarters in the Science City of Muñoz, Nueva Ecija."],["The carabao and the tamaraw","The carabao is often confused with the tamaraw, a small wild buffalo found only on Mindoro. The tamaraw is a separate, critically endangered species and has never been domesticated, while carabaos are domestic animals found throughout the country."],["Protecting carabaos","Philippine laws have long restricted the slaughter of working carabaos to protect farmers’ livelihoods. Today, carabaos are also valued as part of the country’s agricultural heritage, and programs aim to keep healthy, productive herds."]],
sp:["Kesong puti from the farm","Early in the morning in towns of Laguna, Bulacan and Cebu, vendors sell small blocks of soft, white cheese wrapped in banana leaves. This is kesong puti, made from fresh carabao milk curdled with vinegar or rennet and salted lightly. It is often eaten for breakfast with warm pandesal. For many dairy farmers, selling milk and cheese has become an important source of income, giving the carabao a new role beyond the rice field. Programs by the Philippine Carabao Center have helped farming cooperatives produce and sell carabao milk products across the country."],
g:[["Carabao","A domestic swamp buffalo of the Philippines"],["Wallowing","Rolling or lying in mud or water to cool off"],["Kesong puti","A soft white cheese made from carabao milk"],["Artificial insemination","A breeding method used to improve livestock"]],
f:["The carabao is widely regarded as the national animal.","Carabaos cool off in mud.","Carabao milk is used to make kesong puti."],
fun:"Carabao milk has more fat than cow’s milk, making it ideal for rich cheeses and sweets like pastillas.",
tl:"Ang kalabaw ay alagang hayop na matagal nang katuwang ng magsasakang Pilipino sa pag-aararo ng palayan. Nagpapalamig ito sa putik dahil kaunti ang glandula nito ng pawis. Ngayon, inaalagaan din ito para sa gatas, keso at karne."});

ADDART({id:"tamaraw",t:"The Tamaraw",c:"Mammals",i:"🐂",w:"Tamaraw",r:["carabao","phbio","conservation","extinction","eagle"],
p:[["Mindoro’s wild buffalo","The tamaraw is a small wild buffalo found only on the island of Mindoro."],["Critically endangered","Only a few hundred remain in the wild."],["A national treasure","It is protected by law and is a symbol of Philippine wildlife."]],
l:"The tamaraw is a small, stocky wild buffalo found only on the island of Mindoro in the Philippines. It is one of the country’s most famous endemic animals and is critically endangered. Smaller than the domestic carabao, the tamaraw has dark brown to grayish-black hair and short, sturdy horns that grow in a V shape. It once roamed much of Mindoro, but hunting, disease brought by cattle and the loss of its grassland and forest habitat reduced it to a few small areas. Most surviving tamaraws live in Mounts Iglit–Baco Natural Park, where rangers conduct an annual count. In 2026 the count there recorded 425 animals. Tamaraws are protected by law, and October is celebrated as Tamaraw Month.",
s:[["Appearance","The tamaraw stands about one meter tall at the shoulder and weighs around 200 to 300 kilograms, much smaller than a carabao. Its horns are short, thick and point upward in a V shape, unlike the carabao’s wide, curving horns. It often has light markings on its legs and face."],["Life in the wild","Tamaraws live in grasslands and forests in Mindoro’s mountains. They are mainly solitary, though females stay with their calves for several years. Once active by day, they became more active at night to avoid people. They graze on grasses and young bamboo shoots."],["Decline","In the early 1900s there may have been around 10,000 tamaraws on Mindoro. A disease outbreak among cattle in the 1930s spread to tamaraws, and hunting, logging, farming and settlement destroyed much of their habitat. By the 1960s and 1970s, their numbers had fallen dramatically."],["Counting tamaraws","Every year, rangers, volunteers and conservation workers carry out a simultaneous count in Mounts Iglit–Baco Natural Park, watching from vantage points across the grasslands. The count in 2026 recorded 425 tamaraws, an increase of about 12 percent from the previous year, a hopeful sign for the species."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Bubalus mindorensis"],["Found only on","Mindoro"],["Status","Critically endangered"],["2026 count in Mts. Iglit–Baco","425"],["Tamaraw Month","October"]],
d:[["1888","The tamaraw is described scientifically"],["1930s","A cattle disease devastates tamaraw populations"],["1954","A law protects the tamaraw from hunting"],["1970","Mounts Iglit–Baco is declared a national park"],["2002","October is declared Tamaraw Month"],["2026","The annual count records 425 tamaraws in Mounts Iglit–Baco"]],
s2:[["Protecting the tamaraw","The tamaraw has been protected by law since the 1950s, and killing one is a serious crime. The Tamaraw Conservation Program of the Department of Environment and Natural Resources works with local communities, Indigenous peoples and partners to patrol habitats, reduce poaching and manage grasslands with controlled burning."],["The Mangyan and the tamaraw","The Indigenous Mangyan peoples of Mindoro live in and around tamaraw habitat. Conservation programs increasingly involve Mangyan communities, recognizing their ancestral domains and traditional knowledge. Balancing conservation with the rights and livelihoods of Indigenous people is an important part of protecting the species."],["Captive breeding","In the 1980s a captive breeding program tried to increase tamaraw numbers, but only one calf, named Kalibasib, was born and survived. The program showed how difficult breeding tamaraws in captivity is, so conservation now focuses on protecting wild populations."],["A national symbol","The tamaraw appeared on the Philippine one-peso coin and has been used as the name and mascot of sports teams and vehicles, such as the Toyota Tamaraw. Its image represents strength, resilience and the uniqueness of Philippine wildlife."],["Other populations","Small numbers of tamaraws also live in other parts of Mindoro, such as Mount Aruyan and Mount Calavite, where conservation groups have been surveying and protecting them. Finding and protecting these scattered groups is important for the species’ genetic health."],["Hope for the future","Thanks to protection, the tamaraw population in Mounts Iglit–Baco has generally increased since the early 2000s, when only a few hundred were counted. Conservationists say continued protection, community support and habitat management are needed for the species to recover."]],
sp:["The count on the mountain","Every April or May, teams of rangers, scientists, students and volunteers hike into the grasslands of Mounts Iglit–Baco Natural Park in Mindoro. They spread out to dozens of vantage points on ridges and hilltops and, at the same time over several days, scan the slopes with binoculars, recording each tamaraw they see, its sex, age and markings, so the same animal is not counted twice. It is hot, tiring work, often in rugged terrain. When the results are announced, conservationists across the country watch for good news. In 2026 the count reached 425, a record that encouraged everyone working to save Mindoro’s wild buffalo."],
g:[["Tamaraw","A small wild buffalo found only on Mindoro"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"],["Critically endangered","Facing an extremely high risk of extinction"],["Natural park","A protected area for wildlife and nature"]],
f:["The tamaraw lives only on Mindoro.","It is critically endangered.","The 2026 count in Mts. Iglit–Baco found 425."],
fun:"The tamaraw’s horns grow upward in a V shape, unlike the wide, curving horns of the carabao.",
tl:"Ang tamaraw ay maliit na ligaw na kalabaw na matatagpuan lamang sa Mindoro. Lubha itong nanganganib maubos. Sa bilang noong 2026, 425 tamaraw ang naitala sa Mounts Iglit–Baco Natural Park."});

ADDART({id:"vultures",t:"Vultures",c:"Birds",i:"🦅",w:"Vulture",r:["eagle","birds","grasslands","extinction","conservation"],
p:[["Nature’s cleaners","Vultures are large birds that feed mainly on the bodies of dead animals."],["Soaring giants","They soar for hours on warm air currents searching for food."],["In danger","Many vulture species have declined sharply because of poisoning."]],
l:"Vultures are large birds of prey that feed mostly on carrion, the bodies of dead animals. By quickly cleaning up carcasses, they help prevent the spread of diseases and play a vital role in ecosystems. There are two groups that evolved separately: Old World vultures of Africa, Asia and Europe, related to eagles and hawks, and New World vultures of the Americas, including condors. Vultures have broad wings for soaring on rising warm air for hours, keen eyesight and, in some species, an excellent sense of smell. Their heads are often bare, which helps keep them clean while feeding. Many vulture species are now endangered, especially in Asia and Africa, where poisoning has caused dramatic declines.",
s:[["Two groups","Old World vultures, such as the griffon and the lappet-faced vulture, belong to the same family as eagles and hawks. New World vultures, such as the turkey vulture, black vulture and Andean condor, form a separate family. Their similar appearance is an example of convergent evolution."],["Built for scavenging","Many vultures have bare heads and necks, which stay cleaner when feeding inside carcasses and help regulate body temperature. Their stomachs are extremely acidic, killing dangerous bacteria, such as those causing anthrax and botulism, that would sicken other animals."],["Finding food","Vultures soar high on thermals, columns of rising warm air, scanning the ground. Old World vultures find food by sight and by watching other vultures descend. Turkey vultures can smell the gases from decaying flesh, even beneath forest canopies."],["The bone eater","The bearded vulture, or lammergeier, of mountains in Europe, Africa and Asia, eats mainly bones. It drops large bones from great heights onto rocks to break them, then swallows the pieces, digesting them with powerful stomach acid."]],
k:[["Diet","Mostly carrion"],["Groups","Old World and New World vultures"],["Largest flying bird by wingspan among land birds","Andean condor"],["Bone eater","Bearded vulture"],["Main threat in Asia","Poisoning by the drug diclofenac"]],
d:[["1980s","California condors are taken into captivity to save the species"],["1990s","Vulture numbers in South Asia collapse"],["2004","Scientists identify the drug diclofenac as the cause"],["2006","India bans diclofenac for use in livestock"],["2009","International Vulture Awareness Day is first held"],["2010s","Poisoning causes large vulture deaths in Africa"]],
s2:[["The Asian vulture crisis","In the 1990s, the populations of several vulture species in India, Pakistan and Nepal fell by more than 95 percent. Scientists found that vultures were dying from kidney failure after eating carcasses of cattle treated with diclofenac, a painkiller. India, Pakistan and Nepal banned veterinary use of the drug, and breeding programs aim to restore vultures."],["Consequences of losing vultures","Without vultures, carcasses rot for longer, and feral dogs and rats increase, spreading diseases such as rabies. Researchers have estimated that the loss of vultures in India led to increases in human disease and deaths. The crisis showed how important scavengers are to human health."],["The California condor","By 1987 only 27 California condors were left, and all were captured for a breeding program. Through captive breeding and release, the population has grown to several hundred birds, many living in the wild. Lead poisoning from bullets in carcasses remains a major threat."],["Vultures in Africa","Many African vultures are endangered. Some die after eating carcasses deliberately poisoned by poachers, who kill vultures because circling birds can alert rangers to illegal kills. Others are poisoned by farmers targeting predators or are killed for use in traditional medicine."],["Vultures and the Philippines","The Philippines has no native vultures; the large birds of prey seen there are eagles, hawks and kites, such as the Philippine eagle and the brahminy kite. Vultures are found in nearby parts of mainland Asia."],["Vultures in culture","In ancient Egypt the vulture symbolized protection and motherhood and was linked with the goddess Nekhbet. In Tibet, sky burials traditionally leave the bodies of the dead for vultures. In some cultures, vultures are seen as symbols of death, but scientists emphasize their role as essential cleaners."]],
sp:["The bone breaker of the mountains","High in the mountains of the Himalayas, the Alps and East Africa lives a remarkable bird: the bearded vulture. With feathers often stained a rusty orange from bathing in iron-rich mud, it looks fierce, yet it lives mainly on bones. When a bone is too big to swallow, the bird carries it high into the air and drops it onto rocks below, breaking it into pieces. Its stomach acid is strong enough to dissolve bone within a day. In the Alps, the species was wiped out in the early 1900s, but a reintroduction program begun in the 1980s has brought bearded vultures back to the skies."],
g:[["Vulture","A large bird that feeds mainly on dead animals"],["Carrion","The flesh of dead animals"],["Thermal","A column of rising warm air"],["Convergent evolution","When unrelated animals evolve similar features"]],
f:["Vultures eat mostly dead animals.","Old and New World vultures evolved separately.","The Philippines has no native vultures."],
fun:"Vultures’ stomach acid is so strong it can kill the bacteria that cause anthrax.",
tl:"Ang buwitre ay malalaking ibong kumakain ng patay na hayop at tumutulong linisin ang kalikasan. Lumilipad ito nang matagal sa mainit na hangin. Maraming uri nito ang nanganganib dahil sa pagkalason."});

ADDART({id:"pigeons",t:"Pigeons and Doves",c:"Birds",i:"🕊️",w:"Columbidae",r:["birds","birdmigr","domestication","extinction","phbio"],
p:[["Familiar birds","Pigeons and doves are a large family of plump birds found worldwide."],["Amazing navigators","Homing pigeons can find their way home from hundreds of kilometers away."],["Philippine species","The Philippines has many beautiful native doves, including the Luzon bleeding-heart."]],
l:"Pigeons and doves make up the bird family Columbidae, with more than 300 species found on every continent except Antarctica. The names are often used interchangeably, though smaller species tend to be called doves. They have plump bodies, small heads and short legs, and feed mainly on seeds and fruit. The rock dove, the wild ancestor of city pigeons, was domesticated thousands of years ago for food, messages and racing. Homing pigeons can find their way home over long distances, and they carried messages in wars and before modern communication. The family also includes colorful forest species, such as the Philippines’ Luzon bleeding-heart, and extinct birds such as the passenger pigeon and the dodo.",
s:[["A worldwide family","Pigeons and doves range from tiny ground doves to the large, crowned pigeons of New Guinea, which are about the size of a chicken. Many tropical fruit doves are brightly colored in green, orange, purple and yellow. They are important seed dispersers in forests."],["Pigeon milk","Unlike most birds, pigeons and doves feed their chicks with a nutritious substance called crop milk, produced in the throat by both parents. This allows them to raise young even when seeds are scarce."],["Homing pigeons","Homing pigeons can return to their home lofts from hundreds of kilometers away. Scientists think they use the Sun’s position, Earth’s magnetic field, smells and landmarks. Pigeon racing remains a popular hobby in many countries, including the Philippines."],["City pigeons","Feral pigeons, descended from domestic birds, live in cities worldwide, nesting on buildings that resemble the cliffs of their wild ancestors. Some people enjoy feeding them, while others see them as pests because of their droppings and the damage they cause."]],
k:[["Family","Columbidae"],["Species","More than 300"],["Wild ancestor of city pigeons","Rock dove"],["Chicks fed","Crop milk"],["Philippine endemic","Luzon bleeding-heart"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Pigeons are kept by people in Mesopotamia and Egypt"],["1681","The dodo, a giant flightless pigeon, is last reliably seen"],["1870–1871","Pigeons carry messages during the Siege of Paris"],["1914","Martha, the last passenger pigeon, dies"],["1918","The pigeon Cher Ami helps save surrounded soldiers in the First World War"],["2010s","Studies reveal how pigeons use magnetic fields to navigate"]],
s2:[["The passenger pigeon","In the 1800s, passenger pigeons may have been the most numerous birds in North America, with flocks of billions darkening the sky for hours. Massive hunting and forest clearing caused their collapse, and the last one, named Martha, died in the Cincinnati Zoo in 1914. Their extinction became a warning about how even abundant species can disappear."],["The dodo","The dodo, a large, flightless bird of the island of Mauritius, was a relative of pigeons. Discovered by Europeans in the late 1500s, it was extinct within about a century, because of hunting and animals such as rats and pigs brought by sailors. The phrase dead as a dodo comes from its fate."],["Pigeons in war","Pigeons carried messages in many wars. During the Siege of Paris in 1870 and 1871, they carried tiny messages on microfilm. In the First World War, a pigeon named Cher Ami delivered a message that helped rescue surrounded American soldiers, despite being wounded."],["Philippine doves","The Philippines has many native pigeons and doves, including the Luzon bleeding-heart, whose white breast has a red patch that looks like a wound, and the Nicobar pigeon, with shimmering green feathers. Several Philippine species, such as the Negros bleeding-heart and the Mindoro bleeding-heart, are critically endangered."],["Symbols of peace","The white dove carrying an olive branch is a symbol of peace in many cultures, linked to the story of Noah’s Ark in the Bible. In 1949 the artist Pablo Picasso’s drawing of a dove was used as the emblem of a peace congress, making it a famous modern symbol."],["Clever birds","Pigeons are more intelligent than they are often given credit for. Experiments have shown that they can recognize faces, learn to tell apart paintings by different artists, count small numbers and even help spot signs of cancer in medical images after training."]],
sp:["Cher Ami’s flight","In October 1918, during the First World War, a group of American soldiers known as the Lost Battalion was trapped behind enemy lines in the Argonne Forest in France. To make matters worse, their own artillery was mistakenly shelling them. Their commander released their last carrier pigeon, Cher Ami, with a note giving their location and pleading for the shelling to stop. Despite being shot, losing an eye and nearly losing a leg, Cher Ami flew about 40 kilometers back to headquarters in less than half an hour. The shelling stopped, and nearly 200 men were rescued. The brave pigeon was awarded a French medal and is preserved in a museum in Washington, DC."],
g:[["Columbidae","The bird family of pigeons and doves"],["Homing pigeon","A pigeon trained to return home from far away"],["Crop milk","A food made in a pigeon’s throat to feed its chicks"],["Feral","Living wild after being domesticated"]],
f:["There are more than 300 species of pigeons and doves.","The dodo was a relative of pigeons.","Martha, the last passenger pigeon, died in 1914."],
fun:"Pigeons have been trained to tell the difference between paintings by Monet and Picasso.",
tl:"Ang kalapati ay pamilya ng ibong matatagpuan sa buong mundo. Kayang umuwi ng homing pigeon mula sa daan-daang kilometro ang layo. May mga katutubong kalapati sa Pilipinas tulad ng Luzon bleeding-heart."});

ADDART({id:"rays",t:"Rays and Mantas",c:"Sea Life",i:"🐟",w:"Batoidea",r:["shark","fishes","wshark","coral","sea"],
p:[["Flat fish","Rays are flat-bodied fish related to sharks."],["Gentle giants","Manta rays are among the largest fish, gliding with wing-like fins."],["Spiny tails","Stingrays have a sharp spine on their tail for defense."]],
l:"Rays are a large group of fish with flattened bodies and wide, wing-like fins joined to their heads. Like sharks, their skeletons are made of cartilage rather than bone, and they are close relatives of sharks. There are hundreds of species, including stingrays, electric rays, skates, guitarfish, sawfish and the giant manta rays. Most rays live on the seafloor, where they hunt crabs, clams and worms, but mantas swim in open water, filtering tiny plankton from the sea. Stingrays have a venomous spine on their tail for self-defense. Rays live in oceans worldwide and in some rivers. Many species are threatened by overfishing, and the Philippines has important manta and mobula ray sites.",
s:[["Built flat","A ray’s mouth and gill slits are on its underside, suited to feeding on the seabed. Many rays breathe through openings called spiracles on top of their heads, so they can rest on the sand without clogging their gills. They swim by flapping or rippling their large pectoral fins."],["Manta rays","Manta rays can have wingspans of up to about seven meters. They feed by swimming with their mouths open, filtering plankton, and use two fleshy flaps on their heads to funnel food in. Mantas have the largest brains for their size of any fish and are known to visit cleaning stations on reefs."],["Stingrays","Stingrays have one or more sharp, barbed spines on their tails, which can deliver painful venom. They use them only in self-defense, usually when stepped on. Beachgoers in shallow water are advised to shuffle their feet so stingrays swim away."],["Electric rays and sawfish","Electric rays can produce electric shocks to stun prey and defend themselves. Sawfish have a long, toothed snout used to slash at fish and sense prey. Sawfish are among the most endangered marine fish."]],
k:[["Skeleton","Cartilage"],["Closest relatives","Sharks"],["Largest ray","Giant oceanic manta"],["Manta wingspan","Up to about 7 meters"],["Defense of stingrays","Venomous tail spine"]],
d:[["c. 150 million years ago","Early rays appear in the fossil record"],["2006","Wildlife presenter Steve Irwin dies after a stingray injury"],["2009","Scientists recognize two species of manta ray"],["2013","Manta rays receive international trade protections"],["2017","Mobula rays are added to international trade protections"],["2020s","Philippine sites protect manta and mobula ray populations"]],
s2:[["Rays in the Philippines","Philippine waters are home to manta rays, mobula rays, stingrays and other species. Sites such as the Ticao Pass between Masbate and Sorsogon, known for manta and mobula rays, attract divers. Mobula rays were once hunted in parts of Bohol for their meat and gill plates, and protections have been introduced."],["The gill plate trade","Manta and mobula rays have been hunted for their gill plates, which are sold in some markets for use in remedies claimed to have health benefits. Because mantas reproduce very slowly, often giving birth to a single pup every few years, this hunting has caused steep declines."],["Manta tourism","Diving and snorkeling with mantas has become a major tourism activity in places such as Indonesia, the Maldives and Hawaii. Studies suggest a living manta can be worth far more through tourism over its lifetime than as fishing catch, encouraging protection."],["Skates","Skates are relatives of rays that lay eggs in tough cases often called mermaid’s purses, which sometimes wash up on beaches. Most rays, by contrast, give birth to live young."],["Mantas and intelligence","Studies of manta rays have suggested that they may recognize themselves in mirrors, a sign of self-awareness seen in few animals. They also show curiosity toward divers. Scientists continue to study their behavior and social lives."],["Protecting rays","Many rays are caught accidentally in fishing nets, and some are targeted. International agreements regulate trade in manta and mobula rays and sawfish, and some countries have banned catching mantas. Marine protected areas help protect their feeding and breeding grounds."]],
sp:["Flying underwater","Divers who see a manta ray for the first time often describe the experience as magical. The huge, dark shape appears out of the blue, glides overhead with slow beats of its wide fins, and turns gracefully to look at the swimmers before disappearing. Sometimes mantas leap entirely out of the water, crashing back with a loud splash, behavior scientists are still trying to explain. Each manta has a unique pattern of spots on its belly, which researchers use to identify individuals in photographs and track their movements over many years."],
g:[["Ray","A flat-bodied fish related to sharks"],["Cartilage","Flexible tissue that forms the skeleton of sharks and rays"],["Spiracle","An opening behind the eyes used for breathing"],["Plankton","Tiny organisms that drift in water"]],
f:["Rays are related to sharks.","Manta rays can be about 7 meters wide.","Stingrays use their spines only for defense."],
fun:"Each manta ray has a unique pattern of spots on its belly, like a fingerprint.",
tl:"Ang pagi ay isdang patag ang katawan at kamag-anak ng pating. Ang manta ray ay isa sa pinakamalalaking isda. May mga pook sa Pilipinas, gaya ng Ticao Pass, na kilala sa manta at mobula ray."});

;
