// Animal Kingdom, more articles: starfish and other echinoderms, eels, geckos and praying mantises.
ADDART({id:"echinoderms",t:"Starfish and Sea Urchins",c:"Sea Life",i:"⭐",w:"Echinoderm",r:["coral","sea","octopus","mollusc","tubbataha"],
p:[["Spiny-skinned animals","Starfish, sea urchins and sea cucumbers belong to a group called echinoderms."],["Five-part bodies","Most echinoderms have bodies arranged in five parts around a center."],["Regrowing parts","Many starfish can regrow lost arms."]],
l:"Echinoderms are a group of marine animals that includes starfish, also called sea stars, as well as sea urchins, sand dollars, sea cucumbers, brittle stars and feather stars. Their name means spiny skin. There are about 7,000 living species, found only in the sea, from rocky shores and coral reefs to the deepest ocean trenches. Most adult echinoderms have bodies arranged in five parts around a central point, a pattern called five-fold symmetry. They move and feed using hundreds of tiny tube feet powered by a unique water vascular system. Many can regenerate lost body parts. Echinoderms play important roles in ocean ecosystems, and some, such as sea urchins and sea cucumbers, are harvested for food, including in the Philippines.",
s:[["Starfish","Starfish usually have five arms, though some species have many more. They crawl slowly using tube feet on their undersides. Many eat clams and mussels by prying the shells open slightly and pushing their stomachs out of their bodies into the shell to digest the animal inside."],["Sea urchins","Sea urchins are round and covered in movable spines, which protect them from predators. They graze on algae with a five-toothed mouth called Aristotle’s lantern. Some urchin spines are venomous, so swimmers should avoid stepping on them."],["Sea cucumbers","Sea cucumbers have soft, sausage-shaped bodies and live on the seafloor, eating sand and mud and digesting the organic material in it, which helps recycle nutrients and clean sediment. When threatened, some can push out sticky threads or even parts of their insides, which they later regrow."],["Water power","Echinoderms have a water vascular system, a network of canals filled with seawater that operates their tube feet by changing water pressure. This allows them to move, cling to rocks, breathe and catch food."]],
k:[["Group name","Echinoderms"],["Name meaning","Spiny skin"],["Living species","About 7,000"],["Body plan","Five-fold symmetry"],["Movement","Tube feet"]],
d:[["c. 500 million years ago","Early echinoderms appear in the oceans"],["1960s","Crown-of-thorns starfish outbreaks damage reefs in the Pacific"],["1983","Disease kills most long-spined sea urchins in the Caribbean"],["2013","Sea star wasting disease strikes starfish along the Pacific coast of North America"],["2020s","Scientists study how starfish regrow their arms"],["2020s","Sea cucumber farming expands in the Philippines and Asia"]],
s2:[["Crown-of-thorns starfish","The crown-of-thorns starfish is covered in venomous spines and eats coral. When its numbers explode in outbreaks, it can destroy large areas of coral reefs, including in the Philippines and Australia’s Great Barrier Reef. Divers and conservation groups sometimes remove or inject them to protect reefs."],["Echinoderms as food","Sea urchin roe, called uni in Japanese, is a delicacy, and in the Philippines sea urchins called salawaki or swaki are eaten fresh in coastal areas. Dried sea cucumbers, known as balatan or trepang, are highly valued in Chinese cuisine. Overharvesting has reduced their numbers in many places, leading to regulations and farming."],["Regeneration","Many starfish can regrow lost arms, and some species can grow a whole new body from a single arm if part of the central disc remains. Scientists study echinoderm regeneration to understand how tissues regrow, which could have medical uses."],["Sea star wasting disease","Beginning in 2013, a disease caused starfish along the Pacific coast of North America to waste away, killing millions. The loss of starfish such as the sunflower sea star allowed sea urchins to multiply and destroy kelp forests, showing how one species can affect an entire ecosystem."],["Related to us?","Surprisingly, echinoderms are more closely related to animals with backbones, including humans, than to most other invertebrates such as insects or snails. Their larvae are two-sided, like ours, and only develop five-fold symmetry as they grow."],["Not really fish","Starfish are not fish at all; they have no backbone, gills or fins. Many scientists prefer the name sea star. Similarly, sea cucumbers are animals, not plants, and sand dollars are flattened relatives of sea urchins."]],
sp:["The stomach that leaves the body","A starfish attacking a mussel is a slow but remarkable sight. It wraps its arms around the shell and grips with hundreds of tube feet, pulling steadily for hours. Eventually the mussel tires and its shell opens by just a fraction of a millimeter. That is enough: the starfish pushes its stomach out through its mouth and into the gap, releasing digestive juices that turn the mussel’s soft body into soup. When the meal is finished, it pulls its stomach back inside. This method allows starfish to eat prey much larger than their mouths."],
g:[["Echinoderm","A spiny-skinned sea animal such as a starfish or sea urchin"],["Tube feet","Small, water-powered feet used for moving and feeding"],["Five-fold symmetry","A body arranged in five parts around a center"],["Regeneration","Regrowing lost body parts"]],
f:["Starfish are not fish.","Most echinoderms have five-part bodies.","Some starfish can regrow lost arms."],
fun:"Starfish have no brain and no blood; seawater pumped through their bodies does much of the work.",
tl:"Ang echinoderm ay grupo ng hayop-dagat tulad ng starfish, sea urchin at balatan. Karamihan ay may katawang nahahati sa limang bahagi. Kaya ng maraming starfish na palakihin muli ang naputol na braso."});

ADDART({id:"eels",t:"Eels",c:"Sea Life",i:"🐍",w:"Eel",r:["fishes","sea","coral","birdmigr","phfood"],
p:[["Snake-like fish","Eels are long, slender fish that look like snakes."],["Mysterious journeys","Freshwater eels travel thousands of kilometers to breed in the open ocean."],["Reef hiders","Moray eels hide in reef crevices with a second set of jaws in their throats."]],
l:"Eels are long, snake-shaped fish belonging to the order Anguilliformes, with more than 800 species. They include moray eels of coral reefs, conger eels, garden eels that live in burrows on the seafloor, and freshwater eels of the genus Anguilla, which spend most of their lives in rivers and lakes but migrate to the open ocean to breed. Most eels have smooth, slimy skin and long fins running along their backs and bellies. The life cycle of freshwater eels was one of biology’s great mysteries for centuries. Eels are an important food in Japan, Europe and parts of Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, but some freshwater eel species are now endangered. The so-called electric eel is not a true eel but a type of knifefish.",
s:[["Freshwater eels","Freshwater eels hatch in the ocean, drift as transparent larvae, and enter rivers as young glass eels. They grow for years in fresh water before returning to the sea to spawn and die. European and American eels breed in the Sargasso Sea in the Atlantic, while Japanese eels breed near the Mariana Islands in the Pacific."],["Moray eels","Moray eels live in holes and crevices on coral and rocky reefs, often with only their heads poking out. They open and close their mouths constantly to breathe, which can look threatening. They have a second set of jaws in their throats that shoots forward to pull prey down, a feature unique among fish."],["Garden eels","Garden eels live in colonies in sandy seafloors, each in its own burrow. They poke their upper bodies out to catch plankton drifting by, swaying like plants in a garden, and quickly retreat into the sand when danger approaches."],["Electric eels","Electric eels of South American rivers can produce shocks of several hundred volts to stun prey and defend themselves. Despite their name, they are more closely related to carp and catfish than to true eels. In 2019 scientists described a species that can produce up to about 860 volts."]],
k:[["Order","Anguilliformes"],["Species","More than 800"],["Atlantic eel breeding area","Sargasso Sea"],["Unique feature of morays","Second set of throat jaws"],["Not a true eel","Electric eel"]],
d:[["c. 350 BC","Aristotle suggests eels arise from mud because no eggs are found"],["1904","Johannes Schmidt begins searching for the eel’s breeding grounds"],["1922","Schmidt identifies the Sargasso Sea as the breeding area of European eels"],["2008","European eels are listed as critically endangered"],["2009","Japanese eel eggs are collected in the wild for the first time"],["2019","A powerful new species of electric eel is described"]],
s2:[["The mystery of the eel","For over 2,000 years, people wondered where eels came from, because no one had ever found eggs or baby eels in rivers. Aristotle suggested they came from mud. In the 1870s a young Sigmund Freud, later famous in psychology, dissected hundreds of eels searching for their reproductive organs without success. In the early 1900s the Danish scientist Johannes Schmidt traced tiny eel larvae across the Atlantic to the Sargasso Sea."],["Eels in the Philippines","In the Philippines, eels called igat or kasili live in rivers and lakes, and are cooked in dishes such as adobo and ginataan. Glass eels caught in Philippine rivers have been exported to East Asian eel farms, which has raised concern about overfishing, and the government has at times restricted this trade."],["Eel farming","Eels cannot yet be bred in large numbers in captivity, so eel farms raise young glass eels caught from the wild. High demand, especially for Japanese grilled eel, called unagi, has led to overfishing and illegal trade in glass eels, worth large sums of money."],["Endangered eels","European, Japanese and American eel populations have declined sharply because of overfishing, dams that block migration, pollution, parasites and changes in ocean currents. The European eel is critically endangered, and trade in it is restricted."],["Eels in culture","Eels have long been eaten in many cultures, from jellied eels in London to smoked eel in the Netherlands and kabayaki in Japan. They also appear in myths and folklore, sometimes as mysterious creatures linked with water spirits."],["Slippery skin","Most eels produce a thick layer of slime that protects their skin from infection and helps them slip through mud and crevices. Some freshwater eels can even wriggle short distances over damp land between ponds."]],
sp:["The long journey to the Sargasso Sea","When a European eel is about 10 or 20 years old, something changes. Its eyes grow larger, its belly turns silver and it stops eating. On dark, rainy autumn nights it slips downstream from rivers across Europe, and some even cross wet fields, heading for the sea. Then it swims thousands of kilometers across the Atlantic to the Sargasso Sea, a calm region near Bermuda, where it is thought to spawn and die. The eggs hatch into leaf-shaped larvae that drift with the Gulf Stream back toward Europe. Even today, no one has ever directly observed eels spawning in the wild."],
g:[["Eel","A long, snake-shaped fish"],["Glass eel","A young, transparent eel entering rivers"],["Sargasso Sea","A region of the Atlantic where European and American eels breed"],["Moray","A reef-dwelling eel with throat jaws"]],
f:["Electric eels are not true eels.","European eels breed in the Sargasso Sea.","Moray eels have a second set of jaws."],
fun:"No one has ever directly observed freshwater eels spawning in the wild ocean.",
tl:"Ang igat ay mahaba at payat na isdang mukhang ahas. Naglalakbay ang ilang igat ng libu-libong kilometro patungo sa dagat upang mangitlog. Kinakain ito sa Pilipinas, gaya ng adobong igat."});

ADDART({id:"geckos",t:"Geckos",c:"Reptiles",i:"🦎",w:"Gecko",r:["lizard","chameleon","insect","phbio","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Sticky-footed lizards","Geckos are small lizards famous for climbing walls and ceilings."],["Noisy lizards","Unlike most lizards, many geckos make calls."],["A famous call","The tokay gecko, or tuko, is known in the Philippines for its loud call."]],
l:"Geckos are small to medium-sized lizards found in warm regions around the world, with more than 1,500 species. Many geckos can climb smooth walls, glass and even ceilings thanks to millions of tiny hairs on their toe pads that cling to surfaces using weak molecular forces. Most geckos are active at night and eat insects, which makes them welcome guests in many homes. Unlike most lizards, many geckos communicate with chirps, clicks and barks. Most species cannot blink; they lick their eyes clean instead. Many can drop their tails to escape predators and grow new ones. In the Philippines, house geckos called butiki are common in homes, and the larger tokay gecko, or tuko, is known for its loud to-kay call.",
s:[["Sticky feet without glue","Gecko toe pads are covered with ridges carrying millions of tiny hairs called setae, each branching into hundreds of even smaller tips. These get so close to a surface that weak attractive forces between molecules, called van der Waals forces, hold the gecko in place. It can peel its toes off easily by changing the angle."],["Night hunters","Most geckos hunt at night, catching insects, spiders and other small animals. House geckos gather around lights where insects swarm. Many geckos have large eyes with excellent night vision, even able to see colors in dim light."],["Voices","The word gecko comes from a Malay word imitating the call of the tokay gecko. Many geckos chirp, click or squeak to defend territories or attract mates. In the Philippines, the house gecko’s chirping is familiar in homes at night."],["Dropping tails","When grabbed by a predator, many geckos can shed their tails, which keep wriggling to distract the attacker while the gecko escapes. A new tail grows back, though it often looks different from the original."]],
k:[["Species","More than 1,500"],["Climbing secret","Tiny hairs called setae"],["Philippine house gecko","Butiki"],["Philippine large gecko","Tokay gecko or tuko"],["Name origin","Malay imitation of the tokay’s call"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus describes the tokay gecko"],["2000","Scientists explain how gecko feet stick using van der Waals forces"],["2008","The world’s smallest known gecko-sized reptiles are described in the Caribbean"],["2010s","Engineers develop gecko-inspired adhesives and climbing robots"],["2011","Rumors drive a surge in illegal trade of tokay geckos in the Philippines"],["2019","Tokay geckos are added to international trade regulations"]],
s2:[["The tuko","The tokay gecko is one of the largest geckos, growing up to about 30 centimeters, with blue-gray skin covered in orange or red spots. Its loud call, to-kay or tu-ko, can be heard at night in many Philippine homes and forests. Some Filipinos believe counting its calls predicts luck. Tokays have a strong bite and can be aggressive when handled."],["The tuko trade frenzy","Around 2011, false rumors spread that tokay geckos could be used to cure diseases such as AIDS, cancer and asthma, and that dealers would pay huge sums for large ones. Many people in the Philippines captured tuko to sell, and authorities warned that the trade was illegal and the claims untrue. The episode showed how rumors can threaten wildlife."],["Inspiring inventions","Scientists and engineers have studied gecko feet to create dry adhesives that stick and unstick easily without leaving residue. Gecko-inspired grippers have been tested on robots that climb walls and on devices for handling objects in space."],["Geckos as pets","Some geckos, such as the leopard gecko and crested gecko, are popular pets because they are small, calm and easy to care for. Experts advise buying captive-bred animals rather than wild-caught ones, and learning about their care before adopting."],["Tiny and giant geckos","Geckos range from tiny species in the Caribbean, which are among the smallest reptiles in the world, to the New Caledonian giant gecko, which can grow longer than 35 centimeters. A giant gecko from New Zealand, known only from a single museum specimen, was even larger."],["House guests","In tropical countries, house geckos are often welcome because they eat mosquitoes, flies and cockroaches. They are harmless to people, though their droppings can be a nuisance."]],
sp:["Counting the tuko’s calls","On a quiet night in a Philippine province, a deep, croaking call rings out: tu-ko, tu-ko, tu-ko. Families may pause and count. In some traditions, an odd or even number of calls is said to bring good or bad luck, or to answer a question. Scientists explain that the tokay gecko is simply calling to defend its territory or attract a mate. Whether or not people believe the old sayings, the tuko’s call is one of the most familiar night sounds in many Filipino homes, and the gecko itself helps control insects around the house."],
g:[["Gecko","A small lizard, often able to climb walls"],["Setae","Tiny hairs on gecko toes that help them stick"],["Van der Waals forces","Weak attractions between molecules"],["Butiki","The Filipino name for small house geckos"]],
f:["Geckos can climb glass and ceilings.","Many geckos lick their eyes clean.","The tuko is named after its call."],
fun:"A gecko can hang from a ceiling by a single toe.",
tl:"Ang butiki at tuko ay mga uri ng gecko na kayang umakyat sa pader at kisame. May milyon-milyong maliliit na buhok sa kanilang paa na tumutulong sa kanilang kumapit. Kilala ang tuko sa malakas nitong huni sa gabi."});

ADDART({id:"mantises",t:"Praying Mantises",c:"Insects",i:"🦗",w:"Mantis",r:["insect","beetle","animalbehavior","flower","butterfly"],
p:[["Praying posture","Mantises hold their front legs folded as if praying."],["Ambush hunters","They wait motionless and strike prey with lightning-fast front legs."],["Masters of disguise","Many mantises look like leaves, sticks or flowers."]],
l:"Praying mantises are predatory insects named for the way they hold their spiny front legs folded together, as if in prayer. There are about 2,400 species, mostly in warm regions, including many in the Philippines and Southeast Asia. Mantises are ambush hunters: they wait motionless, often camouflaged among leaves or flowers, then strike with their front legs in a fraction of a second, grabbing insects and sometimes small animals. They are the only insects that can turn their heads to look over their shoulders, and they have excellent vision with depth perception. Female mantises sometimes eat males during or after mating. Gardeners value mantises because they eat many pest insects.",
s:[["Deadly front legs","A mantis’s front legs are lined with sharp spines that trap prey. Its strike can take less than a tenth of a second, too fast for many insects to escape. Mantises eat flies, crickets, moths and other insects, and large species occasionally catch lizards, frogs or even small birds."],["Seeing in 3D","Mantises have large compound eyes set wide apart on a triangular head that can swivel about 180 degrees. In 2016 scientists fitted mantises with tiny 3D glasses and showed that they use stereo vision to judge distance, the first insects shown to do so."],["Camouflage","Many mantises are green or brown and blend in with leaves and bark. The orchid mantis of Southeast Asia looks like a pink and white flower, attracting insects that come close looking for nectar. Others resemble dead leaves, twigs or even ants when young."],["Life cycle","Female mantises lay eggs in a frothy mass that hardens into a protective case called an ootheca, attached to twigs or walls. Dozens or hundreds of tiny nymphs hatch, resembling small adults. They grow by shedding their skin several times before becoming adults with wings."]],
k:[["Species","About 2,400"],["Hunting style","Ambush"],["Head rotation","About 180 degrees"],["Egg case","Ootheca"],["Famous flower mimic","Orchid mantis"]],
d:[["c. 100 million years ago","Early mantises are preserved in amber"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the European mantis"],["1890s","European mantises are introduced to North America"],["2016","Scientists show that mantises use 3D vision"],["2017","Researchers report mantises catching birds around the world"],["2020s","New mantis species continue to be discovered in Southeast Asian forests"]],
s2:[["Mantises in the Philippines","The Philippines has many mantis species, including leaf-like and flower-like mantises in its forests. Filipinos often call them sasamba or similar names, and children sometimes keep them briefly as pets. Some species are found only in the Philippines, and scientists continue to describe new ones."],["The orchid mantis","For a long time people thought the orchid mantis hid among orchids, but research showed that it usually sits alone on leaves, where its flower-like appearance itself attracts pollinating insects. It is one of the few known animals that mimics a flower to lure prey."],["Cannibal mates","Female mantises sometimes eat males during or after mating, especially in captivity or when hungry. In the wild this happens less often than once thought. By eating the male, the female gains nutrients that may help her produce more eggs."],["Mantises and martial arts","The movements of mantises inspired a style of Chinese kung fu known as praying mantis style, which imitates their quick, grabbing strikes. The insect’s calm stance and sudden attacks have also been admired in art and poetry."],["Helpful in gardens","Gardeners sometimes buy mantis egg cases to release in gardens to control pests. However, mantises eat helpful insects such as bees and butterflies as well as pests, so they are not a perfect form of pest control."],["Close relatives","Mantises are closely related to cockroaches and termites, which may seem surprising. Together they form a group of insects with similar egg cases and wing structures."]],
sp:["Tiny glasses for a mantis","How do you test whether an insect sees in three dimensions? In 2016 scientists at Newcastle University in England glued tiny 3D glasses, with one blue lens and one green lens, onto praying mantises using beeswax. They then showed the mantises 3D films of buzzing prey on a screen, like those in a 3D cinema. When the images were made to appear within striking distance, the mantises lunged at them, showing that they combine views from both eyes to judge depth. It was the first proof of stereo vision in an insect, and the research may help design simple 3D vision systems for robots."],
g:[["Mantis","A predatory insect that holds its front legs as if praying"],["Ambush","Waiting hidden to attack by surprise"],["Ootheca","A hardened egg case laid by mantises"],["Nymph","A young insect that looks like a small adult"]],
f:["There are about 2,400 mantis species.","Mantises can turn their heads to look behind them.","Mantises see in 3D."],
fun:"Scientists once fitted praying mantises with tiny 3D glasses to prove they see in three dimensions.",
tl:"Ang praying mantis o sasamba ay insektong nakatiklop ang harapang mga paa na parang nagdarasal. Naghihintay ito nang tahimik at mabilis na sinusunggaban ang biktima. Maraming uri nito ang mukhang dahon, sanga o bulaklak."});

;
/* data/plants1.js */
// Plants: orchids, rafflesia, fruits, seaweeds, coconut and seeds.
ADDART({id:"orchids",t:"Orchids",c:"Plants",i:"🌸",w:"Orchid",r:["flower","fungi","bee","darwin","conservation"],
p:[["A huge family","Orchids are one of the largest families of flowering plants."],["Clever flowers","Many orchids trick insects into carrying their pollen."],["A Philippine queen","The Philippines is home to the famous waling-waling orchid."]],
l:"Orchids are flowering plants of the family Orchidaceae, one of the two largest plant families on Earth, with roughly 28,000 known species. They grow on every continent except Antarctica, from tropical rainforests to cold mountains, but are most varied in the tropics. Many tropical orchids are epiphytes, growing on tree branches rather than in soil. Orchid flowers are often elaborate and are shaped to attract particular pollinators, sometimes by deception. Their seeds are as fine as dust and need fungi to sprout. Vanilla comes from an orchid. The Philippines has a rich orchid flora, with many species found nowhere else, including the waling-waling of Mindanao, often called the queen of Philippine orchids.",
s:[["Flower design","Orchid flowers usually have three sepals and three petals, one of which forms a special lip called the labellum that serves as a landing platform for insects. Their pollen is packed into sticky bundles called pollinia, which attach to a visiting insect’s body and are carried to the next flower."],["Tricking pollinators","Many orchids offer no nectar at all. Some look and smell like female insects, so males try to mate with them and pick up pollen. Others imitate rotting meat to attract flies, or trap insects briefly so they must squeeze past the pollen to escape."],["Dust-like seeds","A single orchid seed pod can hold huge numbers of tiny seeds with almost no stored food. To grow, a seed must meet a suitable fungus that supplies it with nutrients, a partnership called mycorrhiza. This is one reason many orchids are hard to grow from seed."],["Living on trees","Epiphytic orchids cling to branches with thick roots covered by a spongy layer that soaks up rain and moisture from the air. They are not parasites; they simply use the tree for support and access to light."]],
k:[["Family","Orchidaceae"],["Known species","About 28,000"],["Seed type","Tiny, dust-like"],["Famous food orchid","Vanilla"],["Philippine star","Waling-waling"]],
d:[["c. 15–20 million years ago","A bee preserved in amber carries orchid pollen"],["1862","Charles Darwin publishes a book on orchid pollination"],["1882","The waling-waling is described for Western science"],["1903","The moth predicted by Darwin for a Madagascar orchid is formally named"],["1920s","Scientists learn to sprout orchid seeds in the laboratory"],["2010s","Growing orchids from tissue culture makes many species widely available"]],
s2:[["The waling-waling","The waling-waling, Vanda sanderiana, grows high on trees in the forests of Mindanao. Its large flowers are pink and white with reddish-brown patterns. It is admired by orchid growers worldwide and has been used to breed many hybrids. Some lawmakers have proposed making it a national flower alongside the sampaguita, while others prefer to keep the sampaguita alone; the debate has continued for years."],["Darwin’s prediction","In 1862 Charles Darwin examined a Madagascar orchid whose nectar lies at the bottom of a spur about 30 centimeters long. He predicted that a moth with an equally long tongue must exist to pollinate it. Many scientists doubted him, but a hawk moth with a very long tongue was later found and named in 1903."],["Orchid fever","In the 1800s, wealthy Europeans became obsessed with rare tropical orchids, a craze sometimes called orchidelirium. Collectors were sent into forests in Asia and South America, including the Philippines, and some stripped whole areas of orchids. This history still shapes laws protecting wild orchids today."],["Vanilla","Vanilla flavoring comes from the cured seed pods of vanilla orchids, native to Mexico and Central America. Outside their homeland, the flowers must usually be pollinated by hand, a technique developed in 1841 by Edmond Albius, a young enslaved worker on the island of Réunion. This labor is one reason real vanilla is expensive."],["Threats and protection","Many wild orchids are endangered by forest loss and illegal collection. International trade in wild orchids is regulated, and in the Philippines collecting rare species from the wild is restricted by law. Growing orchids from seed or tissue culture helps reduce pressure on wild plants."],["Orchids at home","Orchids are among the most popular houseplants in the world. Moth orchids are sold widely because they flower for months. In the Philippines, orchid shows and growers’ societies are popular, and many backyard gardens grow vandas, dendrobiums and other types."]],
sp:["A flower that pretends to be a bee","In parts of Europe and Australia, some orchids have flowers that look remarkably like female bees or wasps, complete with furry bodies and shiny wings. They even release scents that mimic the chemicals female insects use to attract mates. Male insects arrive and try to mate with the flower. In doing so, they pick up a bundle of pollen and carry it to the next deceptive flower. The orchid gets pollinated without offering any nectar at all. It is one of nature’s most extraordinary examples of mimicry, and Darwin himself was fascinated by such tricks."],
g:[["Orchid","A member of a large family of flowering plants"],["Epiphyte","A plant that grows on another plant for support"],["Pollinia","Bundles of orchid pollen"],["Labellum","The lip-shaped petal of an orchid"]],
f:["Vanilla comes from an orchid.","Orchid seeds are as fine as dust.","Some orchids imitate insects."],
fun:"Orchid seeds are so tiny and light that they can drift on the wind like dust.",
tl:"Ang orchid ay isa sa pinakamalaking pamilya ng halamang namumulaklak. Marami ang tumutubo sa sanga ng puno. Ang waling-waling ng Mindanao ay tinaguriang reyna ng mga orchid sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"rafflesia",t:"Rafflesia",c:"Plants",i:"🌺",w:"Rafflesia",r:["flower","orchids","conservation","mindanao","deforestation"],
p:[["Giant flowers","Rafflesia produces some of the largest single flowers in the world."],["No leaves","It has no leaves, stems or roots of its own."],["Philippine species","Many rafflesia species grow only in the Philippines."]],
l:"Rafflesia is a genus of parasitic flowering plants found in the rainforests of Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand. Rafflesia arnoldii of Sumatra and Borneo produces the largest single flower known, which can measure about one meter across. Rafflesia has no leaves, stems or true roots; it lives entirely inside the stems and roots of tropical vines called Tetrastigma, drawing food and water from them. Only its flowers appear, bursting out of the vine as cabbage-like buds. The flowers smell like rotting meat, attracting flies that pollinate them, which is why rafflesia is sometimes called a corpse flower. The Philippines has many rafflesia species, several discovered in recent decades.",
s:[["A hidden parasite","For most of its life, rafflesia exists only as thread-like strands inside its host vine, almost like a fungus. It cannot make its own food through photosynthesis. Scientists found that it has lost many of the genes most plants use for photosynthesis, an unusual case in the plant world."],["The bloom","A rafflesia bud may take many months to grow, swelling like a dark cabbage on the vine. When it opens, the flower has five thick, leathery, reddish lobes often covered with pale spots, surrounding a central bowl. Each flower lasts only about a week before turning black and rotting."],["Smell and pollination","Rafflesia flowers give off a smell like decaying flesh and are warm to the touch, which attracts carrion flies looking for a place to lay eggs. The flies pick up pollen and may carry it to another flower. Because male and female flowers are separate and bloom rarely, pollination is difficult."],["Seeds","After pollination, female flowers produce fruits with many tiny seeds. Scientists still do not fully understand how these seeds reach a new host vine, though animals such as tree shrews or squirrels may help spread them."]],
k:[["Genus","Rafflesia"],["Largest species","Rafflesia arnoldii"],["Flower size","Up to about 1 meter"],["Host plant","Tetrastigma vines"],["Pollinators","Carrion flies"]],
d:[["1818","Europeans first record Rafflesia arnoldii in Sumatra"],["1821","Rafflesia is formally named after Stamford Raffles"],["1882","Rafflesia schadenbergiana is described from Mindanao"],["2014","A study finds that a Philippine rafflesia appears to have lost its chloroplast genome"],["2016","Rafflesia consueloae is discovered in Luzon"],["2023","Scientists warn that most rafflesia species are threatened"]],
s2:[["Rafflesia in the Philippines","The Philippines is a center of rafflesia diversity, with species found in Luzon, Mindoro, Panay, Samar, Leyte and Mindanao, many of them found nowhere else. Rafflesia schadenbergiana of Mindanao is the largest Philippine species, with flowers reaching about 80 centimeters. Rafflesia consueloae, discovered in Nueva Ecija in 2016, is one of the smallest."],["Named for Raffles","The genus is named after Sir Stamford Raffles, the British colonial official, and Joseph Arnold, a naturalist, who saw a giant flower in Sumatra in 1818. Local people had known the flower long before. Some modern scientists encourage giving greater recognition to the local guides and communities who know these plants best."],["Endangered flowers","Because rafflesia depends on its host vines and on undisturbed rainforest, it is very vulnerable to logging and land clearing. A 2023 study found that about two-thirds of rafflesia species are threatened. Some species are known from only a few sites."],["Tourism and protection","In some areas of the Philippines, Malaysia and Indonesia, local communities guide visitors to see rafflesia blooms and earn income that encourages protection of the forest. Conservationists warn that visitors must not trample host vines or buds."],["Growing rafflesia","Rafflesia has proven extremely difficult to grow in gardens, because the parasite must be introduced into a living host vine. Only a few botanical gardens, such as Bogor in Indonesia, have managed it. Scientists are experimenting with grafting infected vines to help save rare species."],["Not the titan arum","Rafflesia is often confused with the titan arum of Sumatra, another giant, smelly plant also called a corpse flower. The titan arum has the largest unbranched flowering structure in the world, made of many tiny flowers, while rafflesia has the largest single flower."]],
sp:["Searching for a flower in the forest","Finding a blooming rafflesia takes patience. Botanists and local guides trek through steep rainforest, looking for the twisting Tetrastigma vines that host the parasite. They search the ground and lower stems for small brown buds. A bud may take many months to mature, and then it opens for only a few days. When the team finally finds an open flower, the smell usually reaches them first. In 2016, researchers in Nueva Ecija, Luzon, found a previously unknown species this way and named it Rafflesia consueloae, showing that the Philippine forests still hold botanical surprises."],
g:[["Parasite","A living thing that feeds on another"],["Host","The plant a parasite lives on"],["Carrion","Dead, decaying flesh"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"]],
f:["Rafflesia has no leaves or roots.","Its flowers smell like rotting meat.","Many species are found only in the Philippines."],
fun:"A rafflesia flower can weigh several kilograms but has no leaves at all.",
tl:"Ang rafflesia ay halamang parasito na may isa sa pinakamalalaking bulaklak sa mundo. Wala itong dahon, tangkay o ugat. Maraming uri ng rafflesia ang matatagpuan lamang sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"fruits",t:"Fruits",c:"Plants",i:"🥭",w:"Fruit",r:["flower","seeds","agri","domestication","phfood"],
p:[["Seeds inside","A fruit is the part of a flowering plant that carries seeds."],["Surprising fruits","Tomatoes, cucumbers and peppers are fruits to a botanist."],["Philippine fruits","Mangoes, bananas and durian are famous Philippine fruits."]],
l:"A fruit is the seed-bearing structure that develops from the flower of a flowering plant after pollination. Fruits protect seeds and help spread them, often by tempting animals to eat them and carry the seeds elsewhere. In everyday language, fruits are sweet foods such as mangoes, apples and bananas, but botanists also count tomatoes, eggplants, cucumbers, chili peppers, beans and nuts as fruits. Fruits are an important source of vitamins, fiber and energy in human diets. People domesticated fruit trees thousands of years ago, breeding them to be larger and sweeter. The Philippines grows many tropical fruits, including mangoes, bananas, pineapples, papayas, durian, lanzones, rambutan and calamansi, and is a major exporter of bananas and pineapples.",
s:[["How fruits form","After a flower is pollinated, its ovary swells and develops into a fruit while the ovules inside become seeds. Petals usually fall away. Some fruits, such as strawberries and apples, also include other parts of the flower."],["Types of fruit","Botanists divide fruits into types. Berries, such as grapes, tomatoes and bananas, have fleshy walls and many seeds. Drupes, such as mangoes and coconuts, have a single hard stone. Dry fruits include nuts, grains and pods. Aggregate fruits, such as raspberries, form from many parts of one flower."],["Spreading seeds","Fleshy fruits attract birds, bats and other animals, which eat them and drop the seeds far away. Other fruits are carried by wind, like dandelion parachutes, or by water, like coconuts. Some, such as burrs, hook onto fur."],["Ripening","Many fruits change color, soften and become sweeter as they ripen. A natural gas called ethylene triggers ripening in fruits such as bananas and mangoes. Placing an unripe fruit near a ripe one can speed ripening for this reason."]],
k:[["Develops from","A flower’s ovary"],["Purpose","Protect and spread seeds"],["Ripening gas","Ethylene"],["Botanical fruits","Tomatoes, peppers, cucumbers"],["Major Philippine exports","Bananas and pineapples"]],
d:[["c. 11,000 years ago","Figs are among the earliest cultivated fruit trees"],["c. 4,000 years ago","Mangoes are cultivated in South Asia"],["1500s","Pineapples, papayas and guavas reach Asia from the Americas"],["1893","The US Supreme Court rules tomatoes count as vegetables for tax purposes"],["1960s","Large-scale banana plantations expand in Mindanao"],["2020s","Philippine mangoes and bananas are exported to many countries"]],
s2:[["Philippine mangoes","The Carabao mango, also called the Manila mango, is known for its sweetness and smooth texture and is often called the national fruit of the Philippines. Guimaras and Zambales are famous for their mangoes. Mangoes are eaten ripe as desserts, green with bagoong, dried as snacks and blended into drinks."],["The king of fruits","The durian, grown widely in Davao, has a spiky husk and a strong smell that some people love and others find unbearable. It is banned on some public transport in Southeast Asia because of its odor. Supporters praise its creamy, sweet flesh."],["Is it a fruit or a vegetable?","Botanically, a tomato is a fruit because it contains seeds and develops from a flower. In cooking, however, it is treated as a vegetable because it is savory. In 1893 the United States Supreme Court ruled that tomatoes should be taxed as vegetables, following everyday usage rather than botany."],["Bananas","Banana plants are giant herbs, not trees, and their fruits are botanically berries. Most bananas sold worldwide are seedless Cavendish bananas, which are grown from cuttings. Because they are genetically very similar, diseases such as Panama disease threaten plantations, including in Mindanao."],["Breeding sweeter fruits","Over thousands of years, people selected the biggest, sweetest fruits to plant, gradually transforming wild plants. Wild bananas are full of hard seeds, and wild apples are small and sour. Modern breeding and grafting continue to create new fruit varieties."],["Fruits and health","Health organizations recommend eating fruits and vegetables every day because they provide vitamins, minerals and fiber. Calamansi and guava are rich in vitamin C. Nutritionists generally advise eating whole fruits rather than only drinking juices, which contain less fiber."]],
sp:["Why plants make fruit","To a plant, a juicy fruit is a deal with animals. The plant packs its seeds inside sweet flesh that animals want to eat. In return, the animal carries the seeds away and drops them elsewhere, often in a small pile of fertilizer. Bright red and orange colors signal ripeness to birds, which see color well, while bat-pollinated and bat-dispersed fruits are often dull but strong-smelling. Some seeds even sprout better after passing through an animal’s gut. Over millions of years, this partnership has shaped both fruits and the animals that eat them, including humans."],
g:[["Fruit","The seed-bearing part of a flowering plant"],["Ovary","The part of a flower that becomes the fruit"],["Drupe","A fruit with one hard stone"],["Ethylene","A gas that makes fruits ripen"]],
f:["Tomatoes are botanically fruits.","Bananas are berries.","Ethylene gas makes fruits ripen."],
fun:"Botanically, strawberries are not true berries, but bananas and eggplants are.",
tl:"Ang prutas ay bahagi ng halamang namumulaklak na naglalaman ng buto. Sa agham, prutas din ang kamatis at talong. Kilala ang Pilipinas sa mangga, saging, pinya at durian."});

ADDART({id:"seaweeds",t:"Seaweeds",c:"Plants",i:"🌿",w:"Seaweed",r:["sea","coral","oxygen","fishes","phfood"],
p:[["Ocean algae","Seaweeds are large algae that grow in the sea."],["Underwater forests","Giant kelp forms forests along cold coasts."],["Philippine farming","The Philippines is one of the world’s leading seaweed farmers."]],
l:"Seaweeds are large, multicellular algae that live in the sea, usually attached to rocks or the seafloor. Although they look like plants and make food through photosynthesis, most scientists do not classify them as true plants. Seaweeds are grouped by color into green, red and brown algae. They provide food and shelter for many sea creatures, and kelp forests are among the richest ocean habitats. Seaweeds are eaten in many cultures, especially in East and Southeast Asia, and are used to make thickeners such as agar and carrageenan found in ice cream, toothpaste and medicines. The Philippines is a major producer of farmed seaweed, much of it grown by coastal families in Mindanao and the Visayas.",
s:[["Three main groups","Green seaweeds include sea lettuce and the grape-like lato. Red seaweeds, the most varied group, include nori and the seaweeds used for carrageenan and agar. Brown seaweeds include kelps and sargassum, some of which grow very large."],["Holdfasts, not roots","Seaweeds attach to rocks with a structure called a holdfast, which anchors them but does not absorb nutrients like roots. They take in water and nutrients directly through their surfaces. Many have gas-filled bladders that keep them floating toward sunlight."],["Kelp forests","Giant kelp, a brown seaweed of cool coasts, can grow more than half a meter in a single day and reach over 40 meters long. Kelp forests shelter fish, sea otters, sea urchins and many other animals. They are threatened by warming seas and by overgrazing urchins."],["Making oxygen","Seaweeds and microscopic ocean algae carry out photosynthesis, releasing oxygen and absorbing carbon dioxide. Ocean algae as a whole produce a large share of the oxygen in Earth’s atmosphere."]],
k:[["Type of organism","Marine algae"],["Main groups","Green, red and brown"],["Anchor","Holdfast"],["Thickeners made","Agar, carrageenan, alginate"],["Major Philippine crop","Carrageenan seaweed"]],
d:[["c. 14,000 years ago","Early people in Chile use seaweeds for food and medicine"],["1600s","Agar is discovered in Japan"],["1949","Kathleen Drew-Baker uncovers the life cycle of nori"],["1970s","Commercial seaweed farming begins in the southern Philippines"],["2010s","Scientists test seaweed feed to cut methane from cattle"],["2020s","Seaweed farming is promoted to support coastal livelihoods and capture carbon"]],
s2:[["Seaweed farming in the Philippines","In the early 1970s, Philippine farmers working with scientists began farming red seaweeds such as Kappaphycus and Eucheuma in shallow waters of Mindanao and the Sulu Archipelago. Farmers tie seaweed cuttings to ropes or lines and harvest them after about six to eight weeks. The dried seaweed is processed into carrageenan. The Philippines was long the world’s top producer and remains a leading one, and seaweed supports many coastal families, though farmers face challenges from low prices, disease and typhoons."],["Seaweeds in Filipino food","Filipinos eat many seaweeds. Lato, or sea grapes, is served fresh with tomatoes and vinegar. Guso is used in salads, and gulaman, a jelly made from seaweed extracts, is a popular dessert ingredient. Seaweed is increasingly used in noodles, snacks and other products."],["The woman who saved nori","In Japan, nori farming once relied on luck because no one knew how its spores formed. In 1949 the British scientist Kathleen Drew-Baker discovered that nori spends part of its life growing inside seashells. Japanese farmers used her discovery to seed nori reliably, and a shrine in Japan honors her as the Mother of the Sea."],["Seaweed in everyday products","Carrageenan, agar and alginate extracted from seaweeds thicken and stabilize many foods, such as ice cream, chocolate milk and jelly, as well as toothpaste, cosmetics and medicines. Agar is also used in laboratories to grow bacteria. Some people raise questions about carrageenan’s effects on digestion, while food safety agencies have generally judged it safe at permitted levels."],["Seaweed and climate","Because seaweeds absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, some researchers hope that seaweed farming could help fight climate change. Others caution that much of the carbon may return to the air when seaweed rots or is eaten, so the benefits are still being studied. Seaweed farming also requires no fresh water or fertilizer."],["Sargassum blooms","Floating brown seaweed called sargassum forms huge mats in the Atlantic. In recent years, massive sargassum blooms have washed onto beaches in the Caribbean and West Africa, harming tourism and fisheries. Scientists are studying causes such as nutrient pollution and changes in ocean conditions."]],
sp:["A day on a seaweed farm","Before sunrise, a family in Tawi-Tawi paddles out to their seaweed farm, a grid of nylon lines strung between wooden stakes in clear, shallow water. They check each line, removing weeds and replacing broken cuttings. When the seaweed has grown thick, they pull it up into their boats and spread it out to dry on bamboo platforms in the sun. After a few days, the dried seaweed is packed into sacks and sold to traders, who send it to factories that extract carrageenan. Much of that carrageenan ends up in foods and products sold around the world."],
g:[["Algae","Simple organisms that make food from sunlight"],["Holdfast","The structure that anchors seaweed"],["Kelp","A large brown seaweed"],["Carrageenan","A thickener made from red seaweeds"]],
f:["Seaweeds are algae, not true plants.","Giant kelp grows very fast.","The Philippines is a major seaweed farmer."],
fun:"The gelatin-like agar in laboratory dishes comes from seaweed.",
tl:"Ang damong-dagat ay malalaking algae na tumutubo sa dagat. Kinakain ito ng mga Pilipino, gaya ng lato at gulaman. Isa ang Pilipinas sa pangunahing nagsasaka ng damong-dagat sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"coconut",t:"The Coconut Palm",c:"Plants",i:"🥥",w:"Coconut",r:["trees","phfood","agri","fruits","sea"],
p:[["Tree of life","The coconut palm is called the tree of life because every part is useful."],["Floating seeds","Coconuts can float across oceans and sprout on distant beaches."],["Philippine crop","The Philippines is one of the world’s largest coconut producers."]],
l:"The coconut palm, Cocos nucifera, is a tall palm tree that grows along tropical coasts around the world. Its fruit, the coconut, is not a true nut but a drupe with a hard shell surrounded by a fibrous husk. Coconuts provide water, flesh, milk and oil, while the palm’s leaves, trunk and husks are used for roofing, building, rope, mats and fuel, which is why it is often called the tree of life. Coconuts can float and survive long journeys at sea, helping the palm spread across tropical islands. People in Southeast Asia and the Pacific have grown coconuts for thousands of years. The Philippines is one of the world’s biggest coconut producers, and coconut farming supports millions of rural Filipinos.",
s:[["Parts of a coconut","A coconut has a smooth outer skin, a thick fibrous husk, and a hard inner shell. Inside the shell are white flesh, called meat, and coconut water. The three eye-like pores on the shell include one through which the sprout emerges."],["Young and mature coconuts","Young coconuts, called buko in the Philippines, are full of sweet water and soft, jelly-like flesh. As the coconut matures, the water decreases and the flesh becomes thick and firm. Mature coconut flesh is grated and squeezed to make coconut milk, or dried to make copra, from which oil is pressed."],["Ocean voyagers","The fibrous husk traps air, allowing coconuts to float. Coconuts can drift on ocean currents and still sprout after long periods at sea. Humans also carried coconuts in canoes as they settled islands across the Pacific and Indian Oceans."],["Tall palms","Coconut palms can grow up to about 30 meters tall and live for many decades. They have no branches; a crown of long, feather-like leaves grows from the top of a single trunk. Dwarf varieties have been bred to make harvesting easier."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Cocos nucifera"],["Fruit type","Drupe"],["Dried flesh","Copra"],["Young coconut in Filipino","Buko"],["Major producers","Indonesia, the Philippines, India"]],
d:[["Ancient times","Coconuts are cultivated in Southeast Asia and the Pacific"],["1521","Antonio Pigafetta describes Filipinos’ many uses of the coconut"],["1949","Nata de coco is developed in the Philippines"],["1973","The Philippine Coconut Authority is established"],["2021","A law creates a trust fund from the coconut levy for farmers"],["2020s","The Philippines launches programs to replant aging coconut trees"]],
s2:[["Coconut in the Philippines","Coconut palms grow throughout the Philippines, especially in Quezon, the Bicol region, the Visayas and Mindanao. Coconut oil and copra have been major exports for over a century. Despite the industry’s size, many coconut farmers earn low incomes, and aging trees and typhoon damage have reduced harvests."],["Coconut in Filipino life","Coconut is central to Filipino cooking, from ginataan dishes cooked in coconut milk to desserts such as buko pie and bibingka. Lambanog is a strong drink distilled from coconut sap, and tuba is a fermented palm wine. Nata de coco, a chewy jelly made by fermenting coconut water, was developed in the Philippines and is now popular across Asia."],["The coconut levy","From 1971 to the early 1980s, the Philippine government collected a levy from coconut farmers, intended to develop the industry. How the funds were used became one of the country’s longest-running controversies, with farmers’ groups saying the money was misused and benefited a few people, while others defended some of the investments made. After decades of court cases, a 2021 law created a trust fund meant to return the benefits to coconut farmers."],["Coconut oil and health","Coconut oil is high in saturated fat. Some people promote it as a healthy oil, saying its fats are processed differently by the body. Many health organizations, however, advise limiting saturated fats, including coconut oil, because they can raise cholesterol. Researchers continue to study the question."],["Useful from top to bottom","Coconut leaves are woven into roofs, baskets and the small rice pouches called puso. The husk fiber, called coir, is made into ropes, mats and doormats. Shells become bowls, charcoal and crafts, and coconut lumber is used for building."],["Coconut crabs","The coconut crab is the largest land-dwelling arthropod, found on islands in the Indian and Pacific Oceans, including parts of the Philippines. It can climb palm trees and crack coconuts with its powerful claws. It is threatened by overharvesting in many areas."]],
sp:["Pigafetta and the tree of life","When Ferdinand Magellan’s expedition reached the Philippines in 1521, the chronicler Antonio Pigafetta was amazed by the coconut palm. He wrote that the people obtained bread, wine, oil and vinegar from it, and that a family could live from just a couple of trees. He described how the islanders tapped the palm for sweet sap and made oil and milk from the flesh. Five centuries later, coconut still plays a central role in Philippine life, in kitchens, farms, crafts and celebrations."],
g:[["Drupe","A fruit with a single hard stone"],["Copra","Dried coconut flesh"],["Coir","Fiber from coconut husks"],["Buko","A young coconut"]],
f:["A coconut is not a true nut.","Coconuts can float across oceans.","Nata de coco was developed in the Philippines."],
fun:"A coconut can survive months floating at sea and still sprout on a distant beach.",
tl:"Ang niyog ay tinaguriang puno ng buhay dahil halos lahat ng bahagi nito ay may gamit. Kaya ng niyog na lumutang sa dagat at tumubo sa malayong dalampasigan. Isa ang Pilipinas sa pinakamalaking prodyuser ng niyog."});

ADDART({id:"seeds",t:"Seeds",c:"Plants",i:"🌱",w:"Seed",r:["fruits","flower","agri","rice","mendel"],
p:[["Baby plants","A seed contains a tiny plant and a supply of food."],["Waiting to grow","Some seeds can wait for years before sprouting."],["Seed vaults","People store seeds in vaults to protect crops for the future."]],
l:"A seed is a small, protected package that contains an embryonic plant and a supply of food, wrapped in a seed coat. Seeds allow plants to reproduce and to spread to new places, and they can survive cold, drought and long waits until conditions are right for growth. Flowering plants produce seeds enclosed in fruits, while conifers and other gymnosperms carry seeds exposed on cones. Seeds come in an enormous range of sizes, from dust-like orchid seeds to the giant seed of the coco de mer palm. Humans depend on seeds for much of their food, including rice, corn, wheat, beans and nuts. Seed banks around the world, including in the Philippines, store seeds to protect crop diversity.",
s:[["Inside a seed","A seed has three main parts. The embryo is the baby plant, with a tiny root and shoot. The stored food, in the endosperm or in seed leaves called cotyledons, feeds the embryo when it starts to grow. The seed coat protects it from damage and drying out."],["Germination","When a seed absorbs water and has enough warmth and oxygen, it begins to germinate. The seed coat splits, a root grows downward and a shoot grows upward toward the light. Once the shoot reaches the light and forms leaves, the seedling can make its own food."],["Dormancy","Many seeds remain dormant, or resting, until conditions are right. Some need cold winters, fire, smoke or passing through an animal’s gut before they will sprout. Dormancy prevents seeds from germinating at the wrong time, such as just before a drought."],["Getting around","Seeds travel in many ways. Some float on wind, like maple and dandelion seeds. Others are carried by water, animals or even explode out of their pods. Ants carry some seeds to their nests, helping plant them."]],
k:[["Main parts","Embryo, stored food, seed coat"],["Sprouting","Germination"],["Largest seed","Coco de mer"],["Oldest sprouted seed","About 2,000-year-old date palm"],["Global seed vault","Svalbard, Norway"]],
d:[["c. 365 million years ago","The first seed plants appear"],["c. 10,000 years ago","Farmers begin saving and planting seeds of crops"],["1866","Gregor Mendel publishes his pea seed experiments"],["1960s","The International Rice Research Institute begins collecting rice seeds in Los Baños"],["2005","A 2,000-year-old date palm seed is sprouted in Israel"],["2008","The Svalbard Global Seed Vault opens in Norway"]],
s2:[["Seeds and farming","Farming began when people learned to save seeds from the best plants and sow them. Over generations, farmers selected seeds that did not scatter, grew larger and tasted better, transforming wild grasses into crops such as rice and wheat. Many farmers in the Philippines and elsewhere still save seeds of traditional varieties."],["The rice gene bank","The International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños, Laguna, keeps the world’s largest collection of rice seeds, with well over 100,000 samples of rice varieties and wild relatives from around the world. Scientists and breeders use these seeds to develop rice that resists disease, floods and drought."],["The doomsday vault","The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, dug into a frozen mountain on a Norwegian island near the Arctic, opened in 2008. It stores backup copies of seeds from gene banks worldwide in case of disasters. In 2015 researchers made the first withdrawal, retrieving seeds that had been lost in Syria because of war."],["Ancient seeds","In 2005, scientists in Israel sprouted a date palm seed about 2,000 years old, found at the ancient fortress of Masada. The plant, nicknamed Methuselah, grew into a healthy tree. Sacred lotus seeds over 1,000 years old have also sprouted."],["Giants and specks","The coco de mer palm of the Seychelles produces the largest seed in the world, weighing up to about 25 kilograms. At the other extreme, orchid seeds are as fine as dust. Larger seeds carry more food for the young plant, while tiny seeds can be produced in huge numbers."],["Debates over seeds","Modern agriculture often uses hybrid or genetically modified seeds that farmers buy each season. Supporters say these seeds increase yields and resist pests. Critics worry that they make farmers dependent on seed companies and reduce the diversity of traditional varieties. Many countries, including the Philippines, have rules on seed saving and genetically modified crops."]],
sp:["The tree from Masada","In the 1960s, archaeologists digging at Masada, an ancient hilltop fortress in Israel, found a jar of date palm seeds about 2,000 years old. The seeds sat in a drawer for decades. In 2005, scientists soaked a few in water and fertilizer and planted them. To their surprise, one sprouted. The seedling, nicknamed Methuselah, grew into a tall date palm. Later, more ancient seeds were sprouted, including a female palm that was pollinated with Methuselah’s pollen and produced dates. The ancient Judean date, once famous in the region, is being brought back to life."],
g:[["Seed","A package containing a baby plant and its food"],["Embryo","The tiny plant inside a seed"],["Germination","When a seed begins to sprout"],["Dormancy","A resting state before sprouting"]],
f:["A 2,000-year-old seed has grown into a tree.","The coco de mer has the largest seed.","Seeds can stay dormant for years."],
fun:"The world’s largest seed, from the coco de mer palm, can weigh as much as a small child.",
tl:"Ang buto ay naglalaman ng maliit na halaman at pagkain nito. Kaya ng ilang buto na maghintay ng maraming taon bago tumubo. Nag-iimbak ang IRRI sa Los Baños ng napakaraming uri ng binhi ng palay."});

;
/* data/plants2.js */
// Plants: leaves, roots, medicinal plants, grasses, giant trees and water plants.
ADDART({id:"leaves",t:"Leaves",c:"Plants",i:"🍃",w:"Leaf",r:["photo","trees","oxygen","wcycle","flower"],
p:[["Food factories","Leaves capture sunlight to make food for the plant."],["Tiny pores","Leaves breathe through tiny openings called stomata."],["Changing colors","In cold climates, many leaves turn red and gold before falling."]],
l:"Leaves are the main organs of photosynthesis in most plants. Using sunlight, water and carbon dioxide from the air, the green pigment chlorophyll in their cells makes sugars that feed the plant and releases oxygen. Leaves also release water vapor through tiny pores called stomata, a process called transpiration that pulls water up from the roots. Leaves come in an enormous variety of shapes and sizes, from tiny pine needles to raffia palm leaves longer than a bus, each adapted to its environment. Some leaves have become spines, tendrils or traps for insects. People use leaves for food, medicine, shelter and wrapping. In the Philippines, banana leaves, pandan and anahaw are part of everyday life and culture.",
s:[["Parts of a leaf","A typical leaf has a flat blade and a stalk called a petiole that joins it to the stem. Veins run through the blade, carrying water in and sugars out, and also give the leaf support. The outer surface is coated with a waxy layer that reduces water loss."],["Stomata","Stomata are tiny pores, usually on the underside of leaves, each surrounded by two guard cells that open and close it. When open, they let in carbon dioxide and release oxygen and water vapor. Plants close them in hot, dry weather to save water."],["Leaf shapes","Broad leaves capture lots of light in shady forests. Needle-like leaves of pines lose less water and shed snow. Thick, fleshy leaves of succulents store water in deserts. Floating leaves of water lilies have stomata on their upper surfaces."],["Autumn colors","In places with cold winters, many trees stop making chlorophyll as days shorten. As the green fades, yellow and orange pigments called carotenoids become visible, and some trees produce red pigments called anthocyanins. The trees then drop their leaves to save water and energy during winter."]],
k:[["Main job","Photosynthesis"],["Green pigment","Chlorophyll"],["Pores","Stomata"],["Water loss process","Transpiration"],["Largest leaves","Raffia palm"]],
d:[["c. 400 million years ago","Early plants begin to evolve true leaves"],["1771","Joseph Priestley finds that a mint plant restores air used up by a candle"],["1779","Jan Ingenhousz shows that plants need light to refresh the air"],["1817","Chlorophyll is named by French chemists"],["1950s","Melvin Calvin works out how leaves turn carbon dioxide into sugar"],["2010s","Scientists develop artificial leaves that use sunlight to split water"]],
s2:[["Leaves in Filipino life","Banana leaves are used to wrap and serve food, from suman and bibingka to the shared feasts known as boodle fights. Pandan leaves add fragrance to rice and desserts. Anahaw, a palm with large, fan-shaped leaves, is often regarded as a national leaf and is used for fans, roofing and decorations. Malunggay leaves are prized for their nutrition."],["Leaves that do other jobs","Some leaves have taken on new roles. Cactus spines are modified leaves that protect the plant and reduce water loss. Pea tendrils are leaves that coil around supports. Pitcher plants and Venus flytraps have leaves that trap insects. Onion bulbs are made of swollen leaves that store food."],["Giant leaves","The raffia palm of Africa has the longest leaves of any plant, sometimes over 20 meters. The giant Amazon water lily has round floating leaves up to about 3 meters across, strong enough to support a small child. Banana and taro plants also have very large leaves."],["How leaves cool the planet","Through transpiration, leaves release enormous amounts of water vapor into the air. This cools plants, much like sweating cools people, and adds moisture that forms clouds and rain. Large forests, such as the Amazon, help create their own rainfall in this way."],["Reading leaves","Scientists study fossil leaves to learn about past climates. Leaves with smooth edges are more common in warm climates, while toothed edges are more common in cool ones. The number of stomata on fossil leaves also reveals how much carbon dioxide was in the ancient air."],["Leaf eaters","Many animals eat leaves, including caterpillars, leaf-cutter ants, giraffes, koalas and sloths. Plants defend their leaves with tough textures, hairs, spines and bitter or poisonous chemicals. Some plants even release scents that attract predators of the insects eating them."]],
sp:["Priestley’s mint and the mouse","In 1771 the English scientist Joseph Priestley placed a burning candle in a sealed glass jar. The flame soon went out, and a mouse in such a jar could not survive. Then he put a sprig of mint in a jar where a candle had burned out. Days later, he found that a candle could burn there again. The mint had restored the air. A few years later, the Dutch scientist Jan Ingenhousz showed that plants refresh the air only in light. These experiments helped reveal that leaves produce oxygen, a gas later named and studied by Antoine Lavoisier."],
g:[["Chlorophyll","The green pigment that captures sunlight"],["Stomata","Tiny pores in leaves"],["Transpiration","The loss of water vapor from leaves"],["Petiole","The stalk joining a leaf to a stem"]],
f:["Leaves release oxygen.","Cactus spines are modified leaves.","Raffia palms have the longest leaves."],
fun:"A single large tree can release hundreds of liters of water through its leaves on a hot day.",
tl:"Ang dahon ang gumagawa ng pagkain ng halaman gamit ang sikat ng araw. Humihinga ito sa maliliit na butas na tinatawag na stomata. Ginagamit ng mga Pilipino ang dahon ng saging, pandan at anahaw sa araw-araw na buhay."});

ADDART({id:"roots",t:"Roots",c:"Plants",i:"🥕",w:"Root",r:["soil","trees","fungi","leaves","wetlands"],
p:[["Hidden half","Roots anchor plants and soak up water and nutrients."],["Fungus partners","Most plant roots work together with fungi."],["Roots we eat","Carrots, cassava and sweet potatoes are roots."]],
l:"Roots are the parts of a plant that usually grow underground. They anchor the plant in the soil and absorb water and minerals, which they send up to the stems and leaves. Many roots also store food. Roots grow at their tips, pushing through soil guided by gravity, moisture and touch, and are covered with tiny root hairs that greatly increase their surface. Most plants form partnerships with fungi in their roots, and some, such as beans, host bacteria that capture nitrogen from the air. Roots help hold soil together and prevent erosion. Many important foods are roots, including carrots, cassava, sweet potatoes and the purple yam known in the Philippines as ube.",
s:[["Taproots and fibrous roots","Some plants, such as carrots and many trees, have a main taproot that grows straight down, with smaller roots branching off. Grasses, including rice and corn, have fibrous roots, a mass of thin roots spreading through the soil. Fibrous roots are especially good at holding soil in place."],["Root hairs","Just behind the growing tip, roots produce thousands of microscopic root hairs. These hair-like cells reach into tiny spaces between soil particles, absorbing water and dissolved minerals. They give roots a huge surface area for their size."],["Underground partners","Most land plants have mycorrhizae, partnerships in which fungi grow in or around their roots. The fungal threads reach far into the soil, bringing water and nutrients such as phosphorus to the plant, and the plant gives the fungi sugars in return. Bean and peanut roots form nodules filled with bacteria that turn nitrogen from the air into fertilizer."],["Growing the right way","Root tips sense gravity using tiny grains inside their cells, so they grow downward even if a seed lands upside down. Roots also grow toward water and away from obstacles, constantly probing the soil."]],
k:[["Main jobs","Anchor, absorb, store"],["Types","Taproot and fibrous"],["Tiny absorbing cells","Root hairs"],["Fungal partnership","Mycorrhiza"],["Philippine root crops","Kamote, cassava, ube, gabi"]],
d:[["c. 400 million years ago","The first true roots evolve"],["1880","Charles and Francis Darwin study how root tips sense their surroundings"],["1885","Albert Bernhard Frank names mycorrhizae"],["1888","Scientists show that bacteria in bean root nodules capture nitrogen"],["1937","A study measures the enormous total root length of a single rye plant"],["2010s","Scientists research how plants share resources through underground fungal networks"]],
s2:[["Roots on the table","Root crops are important foods in the Philippines, especially in upland and island areas. Kamote, or sweet potato, and cassava, also called kamoteng kahoy, are boiled, fried or made into desserts. Ube, the purple yam, is used in halaya, ice cream and pastries and has become famous worldwide. Gabi, or taro, is cooked in dishes such as laing, though it is technically a corm."],["Roots above ground","Some roots grow in the air. Mangroves have stilt roots that prop them up in mud and breathing roots that stick up out of the water to take in air. Orchids have aerial roots that absorb moisture. Strangler figs, called balete in the Philippines, send roots down from branches to the ground, eventually surrounding their host trees."],["A hidden giant","In 1937 the scientist Howard Dittmer carefully measured all the roots of a single rye plant grown in a box of soil for four months. He estimated that its roots alone stretched over 600 kilometers, and that its root hairs added thousands of kilometers more. His study showed how vast a plant’s hidden underground system can be."],["The deepest roots","Some plants send roots extremely deep to reach water. The roots of a wild fig tree in a South African cave have been traced to about 120 meters below the surface. Desert plants such as mesquite can also root very deeply."],["Holding the land together","Plant roots bind soil and help prevent erosion and landslides. When forests on steep slopes are cleared, the loss of roots can make landslides more likely during heavy rains. Planting vetiver grass and trees with strong roots is one way communities protect slopes and riverbanks."],["Do plants communicate underground?","Some scientists have suggested that trees share sugars and warning signals through fungal networks connecting their roots, sometimes called the wood wide web. Others argue that the evidence for widespread sharing between trees is weaker than popular accounts suggest. Research on how much plants interact underground is ongoing."]],
sp:["The root tip as a brain","In 1880 Charles Darwin and his son Francis published a book on how plants move. After many experiments, they noticed that the very tip of a root seemed to control its direction, sensing gravity, moisture and obstacles and steering the root around them. They wrote that the root tip acts somewhat like the brain of a lower animal. Most scientists today would not call it a brain, but research has confirmed that root tips process many signals at once. Darwin’s simple experiments with seedlings opened a field of research that continues today."],
g:[["Root hair","A tiny cell that absorbs water"],["Taproot","A main root that grows straight down"],["Mycorrhiza","A partnership between roots and fungi"],["Nodule","A swelling on roots that holds nitrogen-fixing bacteria"]],
f:["Most plant roots partner with fungi.","Ube and kamote are roots.","Roots grow downward by sensing gravity."],
fun:"A single rye plant was found to have over 600 kilometers of roots, not counting its root hairs.",
tl:"Ang ugat ng halaman ay humahawak dito sa lupa at sumisipsip ng tubig at sustansiya. Karamihan sa ugat ay may katuwang na fungi. Ang kamote, kamoteng kahoy at ube ay mga ugat na kinakain natin."});

ADDART({id:"medicinalplants",t:"Medicinal Plants",c:"Plants",i:"🌿",w:"Medicinal plants",r:["malaria","antibiotics","flower","trees","conservation"],
p:[["Medicine from nature","Many modern medicines were first found in plants."],["Traditional healing","People have used plants to treat illness for thousands of years."],["Philippine herbs","Lagundi and sambong are well-known Philippine herbal medicines."]],
l:"Medicinal plants are plants used to prevent or treat illness. People in every culture have used herbs, roots, leaves and bark as medicine for thousands of years, and traditional medicine systems in China, India, Africa, the Americas and the Philippines rely heavily on plants. Many modern drugs were first discovered in plants, including aspirin from willow bark, morphine from the opium poppy, quinine from cinchona bark and the malaria drug artemisinin from sweet wormwood. Scientists continue to search plants for new medicines. At the same time, not all herbal remedies are proven to work, and some can be harmful or interact with other medicines, so health experts encourage testing remedies scientifically and using them carefully.",
s:[["From plant to pill","Plants produce many chemicals to defend themselves against insects, germs and grazing animals. Some of these chemicals affect the human body. Scientists isolate the active substance, test it for safety and effectiveness, and then may produce it in pure form or make similar compounds in laboratories."],["Famous plant medicines","Aspirin is based on salicin, found in willow bark, which people chewed for pain for centuries. Morphine, a powerful painkiller, comes from the opium poppy. Digoxin, a heart medicine, comes from foxglove. Quinine, once the main malaria treatment, comes from the bark of the South American cinchona tree."],["Cancer drugs from plants","Vincristine and vinblastine, used to treat leukemia and other cancers, come from the Madagascar periwinkle. Paclitaxel, also called Taxol, was first found in the bark of the Pacific yew tree. These discoveries encouraged scientists to search rainforests for new medicines."],["Traditional medicine","Traditional Chinese medicine, Ayurveda in India and many indigenous healing traditions use complex mixtures of plants. Supporters value their long history and holistic approach, while critics point out that many traditional remedies have not been tested in rigorous studies. Some researchers study traditional remedies to find promising compounds."]],
k:[["Aspirin source","Willow bark"],["Quinine source","Cinchona tree"],["Artemisinin source","Sweet wormwood"],["Taxol source","Pacific yew"],["Philippine herbal law","Traditional and Alternative Medicine Act of 1997"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Medicinal plants are recorded on Sumerian clay tablets"],["1804","Morphine is isolated from the opium poppy"],["1820","Quinine is isolated from cinchona bark"],["1899","Aspirin is sold as a medicine"],["1972","Tu Youyou’s team isolates artemisinin"],["2015","Tu Youyou receives the Nobel Prize for discovering artemisinin"]],
s2:[["Herbal medicine in the Philippines","Filipinos have long used plants such as lagundi for coughs, sambong for kidney problems, ampalaya for diabetes and bayabas leaves for cleaning wounds. In the 1990s, the Department of Health promoted ten herbal plants it considered supported by research. The Traditional and Alternative Medicine Act of 1997 created an institute to study and regulate traditional remedies. Lagundi and sambong tablets developed by Philippine scientists are sold in pharmacies."],["The story of artemisinin","In the 1960s and 1970s, the Chinese scientist Tu Youyou searched ancient Chinese medical texts for malaria remedies. A 4th-century text mentioned sweet wormwood soaked in cold water. Her team found that extracting it at low temperature preserved the active substance, artemisinin. Artemisinin-based drugs have since saved millions of lives, and she shared the 2015 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."],["Natural does not mean safe","Some plants used as remedies can be poisonous in large amounts, and herbal products can interact with prescription medicines. Products sold as supplements may not be tested as strictly as medicines. Health authorities advise people to tell their doctors about any herbal remedies they take and not to replace proven treatments for serious diseases."],["Debates over evidence","Supporters of herbal medicine argue that traditional knowledge built over generations is valuable and that plant remedies can be cheaper and more accessible. Skeptics argue that only remedies proven in controlled trials should be promoted. Many scientists take a middle view, calling for traditional remedies to be studied carefully so that effective ones can be used safely."],["Protecting plants and knowledge","Many medicinal plants are harvested from the wild, and some are becoming rare. Conservationists work to protect them and encourage cultivation. There are also debates about biopiracy, when companies patent medicines based on indigenous knowledge without sharing benefits; international agreements now encourage fair sharing."],["Medicines still waiting","Scientists believe that many plant species have never been tested for medical uses. As forests disappear, potential medicines may be lost before they are discovered. This is one argument conservationists use for protecting biodiversity, including in the Philippine rainforests."]],
sp:["The willow and the aspirin","For thousands of years, people in ancient Egypt, Greece and elsewhere used willow bark and leaves to ease pain and fever. In the 1800s, chemists extracted salicin from willow and turned it into salicylic acid, which worked but irritated the stomach. In 1897 chemists at the German company Bayer produced a gentler form, acetylsalicylic acid. Sold from 1899 as aspirin, it became one of the most widely used medicines in history. Scientists later found that aspirin also helps prevent some heart attacks and strokes. A remedy from a riverside tree had become a modern drug."],
g:[["Medicinal plant","A plant used to treat illness"],["Active ingredient","The chemical in a remedy that has an effect"],["Herbal medicine","Treatment using plants"],["Clinical trial","A careful scientific test of a treatment"]],
f:["Aspirin was first made from a substance in willow bark.","Artemisinin treats malaria.","Lagundi is a Philippine herbal medicine for cough."],
fun:"The malaria drug artemisinin was rediscovered with help from a medical text written about 1,600 years ago.",
tl:"Ang halamang gamot ay ginagamit upang gamutin ang sakit. Maraming makabagong gamot, tulad ng aspirin, ang unang natuklasan sa halaman. Kilala sa Pilipinas ang lagundi at sambong bilang halamang gamot."});

ADDART({id:"grasses",t:"Grasses",c:"Plants",i:"🌾",w:"Poaceae",r:["rice","wheat","maize","bamboo","grasslands"],
p:[["Feeding the world","Rice, wheat and corn are all grasses."],["Growing from the base","Grasses grow from near the ground, so they survive grazing and mowing."],["Covering the land","Grasslands cover a large part of Earth’s land."]],
l:"Grasses are flowering plants of the family Poaceae, with about 12,000 species. They are among the most important plants on Earth. The cereal grains that provide much of humanity’s food, including rice, wheat, corn, barley, oats and sorghum, are all grasses, as are sugarcane and bamboo. Grasslands such as savannas, prairies and steppes cover large areas of the planet and support grazing animals from zebras to cattle. Grasses grow from points near their base rather than from their tips, which allows them to regrow quickly after being grazed, mowed or burned. Their fibrous roots hold soil in place. In the Philippines, rice is the staple food, and cogon grass covers many hillsides.",
s:[["How grasses grow","Most plants grow from their tips, but grasses grow from regions near the base of their leaves and stems. When animals eat the tops or people mow lawns, the grass keeps growing from below. This has made grasses extremely successful in places with grazing animals and frequent fires."],["Grass flowers","Grasses have small, plain flowers without colorful petals, grouped in clusters called spikelets. They are pollinated by wind rather than insects, releasing large amounts of light pollen, which is a major cause of hay fever. Their fruits are grains."],["Hollow stems","Grass stems, called culms, are usually hollow between solid joints called nodes. This structure is light but strong. Bamboo, the giant of the grass family, uses the same design to grow as tall as trees."],["Saving water","Many tropical grasses, including corn, sugarcane and sorghum, use a special type of photosynthesis called C4, which works efficiently in heat and bright sunlight and uses less water. Scientists are researching whether rice could be engineered to use C4 photosynthesis to increase yields."]],
k:[["Family","Poaceae"],["Species","About 12,000"],["Major cereals","Rice, wheat, corn"],["Pollination","Wind"],["Philippine staple","Rice"]],
d:[["c. 66 million years ago","Grasses already exist, as shown by fossil dung of dinosaurs"],["c. 20 million years ago","Grasslands spread across many continents"],["c. 10,000 years ago","Wheat and barley are domesticated in the Middle East"],["c. 9,000 years ago","Corn is domesticated from teosinte in Mexico"],["1966","The International Rice Research Institute releases IR8 rice"],["2020s","Researchers work to develop rice with more efficient photosynthesis"]],
s2:[["Grains that built civilizations","The domestication of grasses changed human history. Wheat and barley supported the first farming villages and cities of the Middle East, rice fed civilizations in Asia, and corn fed the peoples of the Americas. Stored grain allowed populations to grow and supported trade, writing and the rise of states."],["Rice and the Philippines","Rice has been grown in the Philippines for thousands of years, and the Cordillera rice terraces are a UNESCO World Heritage Site. In 1966, the International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños released IR8, a high-yielding variety that helped spark the Green Revolution in Asia. Supporters credit such varieties with preventing famines, while critics point to greater dependence on fertilizers and the loss of traditional varieties."],["Cogon grass","Cogon grass, Imperata cylindrica, covers large areas of Philippine hillsides, often after forests are cleared and burned. It spreads quickly and is hard to remove, which makes it difficult for trees to regrow. Traditionally, cogon has been used for thatched roofs. It is considered an invasive weed in many countries."],["Sugarcane","Sugarcane is a tall grass that stores sugar in its stems. It was domesticated in New Guinea and spread across Asia and later to the Americas, where plantations relied on enslaved labor. In the Philippines, sugarcane is a major crop in Negros and Panay, where the sugar industry has shaped the region’s history and society."],["Lawns and sports","People plant grasses for lawns, parks, golf courses and sports fields. Lawns are popular for recreation, but some environmental groups note that they can use large amounts of water, fertilizer and pesticides. Some cities encourage native plants instead."],["Grasses and grazing animals","Grasses evolved alongside grazing animals. Grass leaves contain tiny particles of silica that wear down teeth, and grazing animals such as horses, cattle and zebras evolved tall, continuously growing or hard-wearing teeth to cope. Grasslands support some of the largest herds of animals on Earth."]],
sp:["The grass that became corn","About 9,000 years ago in southern Mexico, people began gathering a wild grass called teosinte. Its tiny ears held only a handful of hard kernels, each wrapped in a tough casing. Over thousands of years, farmers kept seeds from plants with bigger ears and softer kernels. Slowly, teosinte was transformed into maize, or corn, with large cobs packed with hundreds of kernels. Scientists were long puzzled about corn’s origins because it looks so different from its wild ancestor, but genetic studies have confirmed the link. Corn is now one of the most widely grown crops in the world."],
g:[["Grass","A plant of the family Poaceae"],["Cereal","A grass grown for its grain"],["Culm","The hollow stem of a grass"],["Teosinte","The wild grass ancestor of corn"]],
f:["Rice, wheat and corn are grasses.","Bamboo is a giant grass.","Grasses are pollinated by wind."],
fun:"Fossil dinosaur dung shows that grasses existed when dinosaurs were alive.",
tl:"Ang damo ay pamilya ng halaman na kinabibilangan ng palay, trigo at mais. Lumalaki ito mula sa ibaba kaya muling tumutubo kahit kainin o gapasin. Ang kawayan ay isang napakalaking damo."});

ADDART({id:"giantrees",t:"Giant Trees",c:"Plants",i:"🌲",w:"List of superlative trees",r:["trees","deforestation","conservation","amazon","climate"],
p:[["The tallest","Coast redwoods are the tallest trees on Earth."],["The biggest","Giant sequoias are the largest trees by volume."],["The oldest","Some bristlecone pines are nearly 5,000 years old."]],
l:"Giant trees are among the largest and oldest living things on Earth. Coast redwoods in California grow taller than any other trees, with the tallest known, named Hyperion, measuring about 116 meters. Giant sequoias, which grow in California’s Sierra Nevada mountains, are the largest single trees by volume. Some bristlecone pines in the American West are nearly 5,000 years old, older than the Egyptian pyramids. Tropical forests also hold giants, such as the towering dipterocarp trees of Southeast Asia, including the Philippines. Giant trees store large amounts of carbon and provide homes for many species. Many have been lost to logging, and today they face threats from fire, drought and climate change.",
s:[["Coast redwoods","Coast redwoods grow along the foggy coast of northern California and southern Oregon. Fog provides moisture that helps them grow so tall. Their thick bark resists fire and insects. Hyperion, the tallest known tree, was discovered in 2006, and its exact location is kept secret to protect it."],["Giant sequoias","Giant sequoias do not grow as tall as redwoods, but their massive trunks make them the largest trees by volume. The General Sherman tree in Sequoia National Park is the largest known single tree. Sequoias need fire to open their cones and clear the ground for seedlings."],["Ancient pines","Great Basin bristlecone pines grow in harsh, dry mountains in the western United States. Their slow growth and dense wood help them survive for thousands of years. One tree, named Methuselah, has been dated by counting tree rings at more than 4,800 years old."],["Tropical giants","In the rainforests of Borneo, a yellow meranti tree named Menara was measured at about 100 meters in 2019, the tallest known tropical tree. Dipterocarps, the family it belongs to, dominate the lowland forests of Southeast Asia, including Philippine trees such as apitong, lauan and yakal."]],
k:[["Tallest species","Coast redwood"],["Tallest known tree","Hyperion, about 116 m"],["Largest by volume","General Sherman, giant sequoia"],["Oldest known individual","Methuselah, bristlecone pine"],["Tallest tropical tree","Menara, yellow meranti"]],
d:[["1852","Giant sequoias become widely known to Europeans and Americans"],["1890","Sequoia National Park is created in California"],["1957","Edmund Schulman dates the Methuselah bristlecone pine"],["2006","Hyperion is identified as the world’s tallest known tree"],["2019","Menara is measured as the tallest known tropical tree"],["2020","Wildfires kill many large giant sequoias"]],
s2:[["Giant trees of the Philippines","The Philippines once had vast forests of tall dipterocarp trees, which were heavily logged during the 20th century for timber, much of it exported as Philippine mahogany. Large trees still survive in protected areas. Famous old trees include ancient balete trees, such as the one in Lazi, Siquijor, and large narra and almaciga trees in the forests of Palawan and Mindanao."],["Climbing to measure","Measuring the world’s tallest trees is difficult. Scientists use lasers to estimate heights from the ground and then climb the trees with ropes, dropping a tape from the top to get exact measurements. Climbers also study the life in the canopy, where ferns, lichens, insects and even small animals live high above the ground."],["Fire and giant sequoias","Giant sequoias are adapted to frequent, low-intensity fires, which clear the forest floor and help seeds sprout. But after a century of fire suppression, forests became crowded with fuel. Severe wildfires in 2020 and 2021 killed a large share of the world’s big sequoias, alarming scientists. Forest managers now use controlled burns and thinning to reduce risks."],["Saving the redwoods","Logging removed most of the original coast redwood forests in the 1800s and 1900s. Conservation groups such as the Save the Redwoods League, founded in 1918, campaigned to protect the remaining old-growth forests. Today they are protected in national and state parks, which are a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["The oldest living things","Some trees grow as clones, sending up new trunks from the same roots. Pando, a grove of aspen trees in Utah, is a single organism connected underground and may be many thousands of years old. A Norway spruce in Sweden has roots estimated at nearly 10,000 years old, though its visible trunk is much younger."],["Why giant trees matter","Large old trees store huge amounts of carbon and produce many seeds. They provide nesting holes for birds and bats and shelter for many plants and insects. Scientists warn that big trees are declining worldwide because of logging, drought, heat and insect outbreaks."]],
sp:["The secret location of Hyperion","In 2006, two naturalists exploring a remote valley in Redwood National Park in California found a redwood that seemed taller than any known tree. A scientist climbed it and measured it at about 115.5 meters, making it the tallest known living tree. They named it Hyperion. Park officials kept its location secret to protect it. But after the location leaked online, many visitors hiked off-trail to find it, trampling the soil around its roots. In 2022, the park closed the area and warned of fines for anyone who visited, saying that people were damaging the very tree they came to admire."],
g:[["Redwood","A very tall conifer of California’s coast"],["Sequoia","A massive conifer of the Sierra Nevada"],["Dipterocarp","A family of tall tropical trees"],["Old-growth forest","A forest that has never been logged"]],
f:["The tallest known tree is about 116 meters tall.","Some bristlecone pines are nearly 5,000 years old.","Giant sequoias need fire to reproduce."],
fun:"The tallest known tree, Hyperion, is taller than a 30-story building.",
tl:"Ang redwood ang pinakamatataas na puno sa mundo, at ang giant sequoia ang pinakamalaki. May mga bristlecone pine na halos 5,000 taon na ang edad. Dating may malalawak na kagubatan ng matatayog na dipterocarp sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"aquaticplants",t:"Water Plants",c:"Plants",i:"🪷",w:"Aquatic plant",r:["wetlands","lakes","flower","dugongs","seaweeds"],
p:[["Living in water","Aquatic plants grow in ponds, lakes, rivers and the sea."],["Floating leaves","Water lilies and lotuses float their leaves on the surface."],["A troublesome invader","Water hyacinth clogs many Philippine rivers and lakes."]],
l:"Aquatic plants are plants adapted to live in or on water. Some float freely on the surface, such as duckweed and water hyacinth; some are rooted in mud with floating leaves, such as water lilies and lotuses; others live fully underwater, such as pondweeds and seagrasses. Many have air-filled tissues that help them float and carry oxygen to their roots. Aquatic plants produce oxygen, provide food and shelter for fish, birds and insects, and filter pollutants from water. Some, such as rice, water spinach and lotus, are important foods. Others, especially water hyacinth, have become invasive pests that choke waterways. In the Philippines, kangkong is a favorite vegetable, while water hyacinth is a major problem in the Pasig River and Laguna de Bay.",
s:[["Floating plants","Free-floating plants drift on the water with their roots dangling below. Duckweeds are tiny, and one type, Wolffia, is the smallest flowering plant in the world. Water hyacinth and water lettuce have spongy leaves or stalks filled with air."],["Rooted with floating leaves","Water lilies and lotuses are rooted in the mud of ponds and lakes, with long stalks carrying leaves and flowers to the surface. Their leaves have stomata on top, unlike most plants. Lotus leaves are coated with tiny bumps and wax that make water roll off, carrying away dirt."],["Underwater plants","Submerged plants grow completely underwater. They have thin, flexible leaves that bend with currents and absorb nutrients and gases directly from the water. They provide hiding places for young fish and release oxygen into the water."],["Seagrasses","Seagrasses are flowering plants that live in shallow seawater, forming underwater meadows. They are the main food of dugongs and green sea turtles, and nurseries for many fish. Seagrass meadows store large amounts of carbon in their sediments. The Philippines has extensive seagrass meadows."]],
k:[["Main types","Floating, rooted-floating, submerged"],["Smallest flowering plant","Wolffia duckweed"],["Largest floating leaves","Giant water lily, Victoria"],["Philippine vegetable","Kangkong"],["Major invasive","Water hyacinth"]],
d:[["c. 700 AD","A sacred lotus seed that would sprout in modern times forms in China"],["1837","The giant Amazon water lily is named after Queen Victoria"],["1849","Joseph Paxton first makes the giant water lily bloom in England"],["c. 1912","Water hyacinth is introduced to the Philippines"],["1990s","Scientists study seagrass meadows as carbon stores"],["2022","A new species of giant water lily is identified at Kew Gardens"]],
s2:[["The lotus","The sacred lotus has been revered in Asian cultures for thousands of years, symbolizing purity because its beautiful flowers rise clean from muddy water. It is important in Hinduism and Buddhism and is the national flower of India. Its seeds, roots and leaves are eaten. Lotus seeds can remain alive for over a thousand years."],["Giant water lilies","Giant water lilies of South America have round leaves up to about 3 meters across, with upturned rims and spiny undersides. Ribs beneath the leaves give them great strength. In 1849 the gardener Joseph Paxton made one bloom in England, and the leaf’s structure is said to have inspired his design for the Crystal Palace. In 2022 botanists at Kew Gardens identified a third species, Victoria boliviana."],["The water hyacinth problem","Water hyacinth, native to South America, has beautiful purple flowers and was spread around the world as an ornamental plant. It grows extremely fast and forms thick mats that block boats, clog drains, reduce oxygen in water and worsen flooding. In the Philippines, it is a persistent problem in the Pasig River, Laguna de Bay and other waterways, and is regularly cleared by crews and machines."],["Turning weeds into products","Some Philippine communities harvest water hyacinth and weave its dried stems into baskets, bags, slippers and furniture, earning income while helping clear waterways. It has also been tested for making paper, compost and biogas."],["Food from water plants","Kangkong, or water spinach, grows quickly in wet places and is one of the most popular vegetables in the Philippines, cooked in sinigang, adobong kangkong and many other dishes. Lotus roots, watercress, water chestnuts and taro are eaten in many parts of Asia. Rice itself is grown in flooded fields."],["Cleaning water","Aquatic plants absorb nutrients and some pollutants from water. Engineers build artificial wetlands planted with reeds and other water plants to treat wastewater naturally. Too many nutrients from fertilizers and sewage, however, can cause overgrowth of algae and some water plants."]],
sp:["A leaf that inspired a palace","In 1849 Joseph Paxton, head gardener at Chatsworth House in England, built a special heated tank and became the first person to make the giant Amazon water lily bloom in Europe. To show how strong its leaves were, he placed his young daughter on one. Paxton studied the network of ribs beneath the leaf and said it gave him ideas for a building of iron and glass. Two years later, he designed the Crystal Palace, a huge glass hall for the Great Exhibition of 1851 in London. A water plant from the Amazon had helped inspire one of the most famous buildings of the age."],
g:[["Aquatic plant","A plant that lives in water"],["Seagrass","A flowering plant that grows in shallow seawater"],["Invasive species","A non-native living thing that spreads harmfully"],["Duckweed","Tiny floating plants"]],
f:["The smallest flowering plant is a duckweed.","Kangkong is a water plant.","Water hyacinth is invasive in the Philippines."],
fun:"A giant water lily leaf can support the weight of a small child.",
tl:"Ang halamang-tubig ay tumutubo sa lawa, ilog at dagat. Ang kangkong ay isang halamang-tubig na paboritong gulay ng mga Pilipino. Ang water hyacinth o water lily naman ay nagbabara sa Ilog Pasig at Laguna de Bay."});

;
/* data/science11.js */
// Science: water chemistry, static electricity, electric circuits, Kepler's laws and the discovery of the nucleus.
ADDART({id:"waterchem",t:"Water: The Remarkable Molecule",c:"Chemistry",i:"💧",w:"Properties of water",r:["molecules","states","wcycle","acid","mixtures"],
p:[["H₂O","A water molecule has two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom."],["Strange behavior","Ice floats, which is unusual for a solid."],["Universal solvent","Water dissolves more substances than almost any other liquid."]],
l:"Water is a simple molecule made of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom, written H₂O, yet it has unusual properties that make life on Earth possible. Water molecules are polar, with a slightly negative oxygen end and slightly positive hydrogen ends, so they attract one another through hydrogen bonds. This explains why water has a high boiling point for such a small molecule, why it can absorb lots of heat, why it has strong surface tension and why it dissolves so many substances. Unusually, solid water, or ice, is less dense than liquid water, so ice floats. Water covers about 71 percent of Earth’s surface, makes up most of the human body, and is the only common substance found naturally as a solid, liquid and gas on Earth.",
s:[["A bent molecule","The two hydrogen atoms in a water molecule are attached to the oxygen at an angle of about 104.5 degrees, giving the molecule a bent shape. Oxygen pulls electrons more strongly than hydrogen, so the molecule has a negative end and positive ends. This polarity is the key to many of water’s special properties."],["Hydrogen bonds","The positive hydrogen of one water molecule is attracted to the negative oxygen of another, forming a hydrogen bond. These bonds are weaker than the bonds inside a molecule but are constantly forming and breaking. Together, they hold water molecules close, giving water its high boiling point and surface tension."],["Why ice floats","When water freezes, hydrogen bonds lock the molecules into an open, six-sided crystal pattern with more space than in liquid water. This makes ice about 9 percent less dense than liquid water, so it floats. Water is densest at about 4 degrees Celsius. Floating ice insulates lakes and seas, allowing life to survive below in winter."],["A great dissolver","Because water molecules are polar, they surround and pull apart many substances, such as salt and sugar, dissolving them. This is why water is sometimes called the universal solvent. In living things, water carries nutrients, salts and wastes, and most chemical reactions in cells take place in water."]],
k:[["Formula","H₂O"],["Bond angle","About 104.5°"],["Boiling point at sea level","100 °C"],["Densest at","About 4 °C"],["Key attraction","Hydrogen bonding"]],
d:[["1781","Henry Cavendish makes water by burning hydrogen"],["1783","Antoine Lavoisier concludes that water is a compound of hydrogen and oxygen"],["1800","Water is split into hydrogen and oxygen using electricity"],["1805","Gay-Lussac and Humboldt show water contains two volumes of hydrogen to one of oxygen"],["1920s","Scientists describe hydrogen bonding"],["2020s","Scientists continue to study the unusual behavior of water under extreme conditions"]],
s2:[["Surface tension","Water molecules at the surface are pulled inward and sideways by their neighbors, creating a kind of skin. Surface tension lets water striders walk on ponds, makes water form round droplets and lets you float a needle on water if placed gently. Soap reduces surface tension, helping water wash away dirt."],["Holding heat","Water can absorb a lot of heat without its temperature rising much, a property called high specific heat. Oceans store enormous amounts of heat, moderating climates along coasts. This is why coastal places such as many Philippine islands have smaller differences between day and night temperatures than inland deserts."],["Climbing up","Water molecules stick to each other, called cohesion, and to other surfaces, called adhesion. Together these let water climb up narrow tubes, an effect called capillary action. It helps water rise through soil and through the thin tubes inside plant stems, and it is why a paper towel soaks up spills."],["Acids, bases and water","Pure water contains a tiny number of molecules that split into hydrogen ions and hydroxide ions, giving it a neutral pH of 7. Acids add hydrogen ions to water, and bases add hydroxide ions. Rainwater is slightly acidic because it dissolves carbon dioxide from the air."],["Heavy water","Some water molecules contain deuterium, a heavier form of hydrogen. This heavy water is slightly denser than ordinary water and is used in some nuclear reactors. During World War II, Allied commandos sabotaged a heavy water plant in occupied Norway to hinder German nuclear research."],["Water and life","Scientists searching for life beyond Earth often follow the water, because all known life needs liquid water. Its ability to dissolve substances, carry heat and remain liquid over a wide range of temperatures makes it an ideal medium for life’s chemistry. This is why discoveries of water on Mars and on moons such as Europa and Enceladus generate great excitement."]],
sp:["Why lakes freeze from the top","Imagine a lake in a cold country as winter arrives. As the surface water cools, it becomes denser and sinks, letting warmer water rise. But when the water cools below about 4 degrees Celsius, it starts to expand and become lighter, so the coldest water stays at the top and eventually freezes. The layer of ice then insulates the water below, which stays around 4 degrees. Fish, frogs and plants survive the winter in the liquid water beneath. If ice were denser than water, like most solids, lakes would freeze from the bottom up and many would become solid ice, making life in them nearly impossible."],
g:[["Polar molecule","A molecule with positive and negative ends"],["Hydrogen bond","An attraction between water molecules"],["Surface tension","The skin-like effect at a liquid’s surface"],["Solvent","A substance that dissolves others"]],
f:["Ice floats because it is less dense than water.","Water is densest at about 4 °C.","Water dissolves many substances."],
fun:"Hot water can sometimes freeze faster than cold water, a puzzling effect called the Mpemba effect, though scientists still debate when and why it happens.",
tl:"Ang tubig ay binubuo ng dalawang atom ng hydrogen at isang atom ng oxygen. Lumulutang ang yelo dahil mas magaan ito kaysa sa likidong tubig. Natutunaw sa tubig ang napakaraming sangkap, kaya mahalaga ito sa buhay."});

ADDART({id:"staticelec",t:"Static Electricity",c:"Physics",i:"⚡",w:"Static electricity",r:["elec","electron","atom","magnet","storms"],
p:[["Electric charges","Static electricity is a buildup of electric charge on a surface."],["Sparks and shocks","It causes small shocks when you touch a doorknob."],["Nature’s biggest spark","Lightning is a giant spark of static electricity."]],
l:"Static electricity is an imbalance of electric charge on the surface of an object. It happens when certain materials rub together and electrons, the tiny negatively charged particles in atoms, move from one material to another. The object that gains electrons becomes negatively charged, and the one that loses them becomes positively charged. Opposite charges attract and like charges repel, which is why a balloon rubbed on hair can stick to a wall and make hair stand on end. When enough charge builds up, it can jump through the air as a spark, giving a small shock. Lightning is a huge discharge of static electricity in storm clouds. Static electricity is used in photocopiers, air filters and paint sprayers, but can also damage electronics.",
s:[["Rubbing and charges","When two different materials touch and separate, electrons can transfer between them. Scientists rank materials in a list called the triboelectric series, showing which tend to gain or lose electrons. For example, rubbing a balloon on hair usually moves electrons to the balloon, giving it a negative charge."],["Attraction and repulsion","Charged objects push or pull on each other without touching. Like charges repel, while opposite charges attract. A charged object can even attract neutral objects, such as bits of paper, by pulling their charges slightly apart, an effect called induction."],["Sparks","When charge builds up enough, it can break down the air between two objects and jump across as a spark, producing light, heat and a crackling sound. Static shocks happen more often in dry weather, because moist air helps charges leak away gradually."],["Conductors and insulators","Metals are conductors that let charges move freely, so they do not usually hold static charge unless insulated. Materials such as rubber, plastic, glass and wool are insulators, which keep charges in place on their surfaces, making static electricity build up."]],
k:[["Cause","Transfer of electrons"],["Rule","Opposite charges attract"],["Unit of charge","Coulomb"],["Biggest natural example","Lightning"],["More common in","Dry weather"]],
d:[["c. 600 BC","Thales of Miletus is said to observe that rubbed amber attracts light objects"],["1600","William Gilbert coins a Latin word that leads to the term electricity"],["1663","Otto von Guericke builds a machine that produces static charge"],["1745","The Leyden jar is invented to store electric charge"],["1752","Benjamin Franklin’s kite experiment links lightning to electricity"],["1785","Charles-Augustin de Coulomb measures the force between charges"]],
s2:[["Where the word came from","The ancient Greeks noticed that amber, a fossilized tree resin, attracted feathers and straw when rubbed with fur. The Greek word for amber is elektron. In 1600 the English scientist William Gilbert used a related Latin word to describe this attraction, and from it came the words electric and electricity."],["Lightning","Inside thunderclouds, collisions between ice crystals and soft hail separate charges, with negative charge often building near the bottom of the cloud. When the difference becomes great enough, a giant spark leaps between the cloud and the ground or between clouds. Lightning heats the air to temperatures hotter than the surface of the Sun, causing the explosive sound of thunder. The Philippines, with its many thunderstorms, experiences frequent lightning, especially during the rainy season."],["Franklin and the kite","In 1752 Benjamin Franklin is said to have flown a kite in a thunderstorm with a metal key tied to the string, drawing sparks that showed lightning was electrical. The experiment was extremely dangerous, and others who tried similar experiments were killed. Franklin went on to invent the lightning rod, which protects buildings by safely guiding lightning into the ground."],["Useful static","Photocopiers and laser printers use static charges to attract toner powder onto paper in the shape of text and images. Electrostatic air filters and factory smokestack filters remove dust by charging particles and collecting them on plates. Car factories use static charges to make paint spray evenly onto metal."],["Dangers of static","Static sparks can damage delicate electronic parts, so technicians wear grounding straps and use antistatic bags. In places with flammable gases or dust, such as fuel stations, flour mills and mines, a static spark can cause fires or explosions. This is one reason gas stations advise people not to use phones or re-enter cars while refueling."],["Shocks in daily life","People often get static shocks after walking on carpets, sliding across car seats or taking off sweaters, especially in dry, air-conditioned rooms. Touching a metal object with a key or knuckle first can reduce the sting. Fabric softeners and humidifiers help reduce static buildup."]],
sp:["The Leyden jar shock","In 1745 and 1746, experimenters in Germany and the Netherlands discovered they could store static charge in a glass jar lined with metal foil, later called the Leyden jar. When the Dutch scientist Pieter van Musschenbroek touched one, the shock was so strong that he wrote he would not repeat it for the whole kingdom of France. Soon, showmen across Europe used Leyden jars to amaze audiences. In one famous demonstration in France, a long line of monks holding hands or wires jumped together as a shock passed through them all. These experiments led to deeper understanding of electricity and, eventually, to modern capacitors used in electronics."],
g:[["Static electricity","A buildup of electric charge on a surface"],["Electron","A tiny negatively charged particle"],["Conductor","A material that lets charge flow"],["Insulator","A material that blocks the flow of charge"]],
f:["Static shocks are more common in dry weather.","Lightning is a giant electric spark.","The word electricity comes from the Greek word for amber."],
fun:"A lightning bolt can heat the air to around five times hotter than the Sun’s surface.",
tl:"Ang static electricity ay pag-ipon ng kargang elektrikal sa ibabaw ng isang bagay. Ito ang dahilan kung bakit dumidikit ang lobo sa pader pagkatapos ikiskis sa buhok. Ang kidlat ay napakalaking siklab ng static electricity."});

ADDART({id:"circuits",t:"Electric Circuits",c:"Physics",i:"🔌",w:"Electrical network",r:["elec","battery","lightbulb","chip","currents"],
p:[["A complete loop","Electricity flows only around a complete path called a circuit."],["Voltage, current, resistance","Three key ideas describe how circuits work."],["Everywhere","Circuits power everything from flashlights to computers."]],
l:"An electric circuit is a closed path through which electric current flows. A simple circuit has a source of energy, such as a battery, wires to carry the current, and a device, called a load, such as a bulb or motor, that uses the energy. When the circuit is complete, electrons flow through the wires and the device works; when a switch opens a gap in the circuit, the flow stops. Circuits are described using three main quantities: voltage, the push that drives the current; current, the rate of flow of charge; and resistance, which opposes the flow. Ohm’s law links them. Circuits can be connected in series or in parallel. Every electrical device, from a flashlight to a smartphone, depends on circuits.",
s:[["Voltage, current and resistance","Voltage, measured in volts, is like the pressure that pushes water through a pipe. Current, measured in amperes or amps, is how much charge flows per second. Resistance, measured in ohms, is how much a material opposes the flow, like a narrow pipe. Ohm’s law states that voltage equals current multiplied by resistance."],["Series circuits","In a series circuit, components are connected one after another in a single loop. The same current flows through each, and if one bulb breaks, the whole circuit stops. Old-style holiday lights were often wired in series, so a single dead bulb could make the whole string go dark."],["Parallel circuits","In a parallel circuit, components are connected on separate branches, each with its own path to the power source. If one branch breaks, the others keep working. Homes are wired in parallel so that each appliance can be switched on or off independently."],["Switches and safety","Switches open or close a circuit to control the flow of electricity. Fuses and circuit breakers protect circuits by cutting the current if it becomes too large, which could overheat wires and cause fires. Grounding provides a safe path for stray current."]],
k:[["Voltage unit","Volt"],["Current unit","Ampere"],["Resistance unit","Ohm"],["Key law","Ohm’s law, V = IR"],["Two basic types","Series and parallel"]],
d:[["1800","Alessandro Volta invents the battery, providing steady current"],["1827","Georg Ohm publishes the law linking voltage, current and resistance"],["1845","Gustav Kirchhoff states his laws for complex circuits"],["1879","Thomas Edison demonstrates a practical light bulb circuit"],["1947","The transistor is invented, transforming electronic circuits"],["1958","Jack Kilby builds the first integrated circuit"]],
s2:[["Direct and alternating current","Batteries provide direct current, which flows in one direction. Power plants supply homes with alternating current, which reverses direction many times a second. In the Philippines, household electricity is supplied at about 220 volts and 60 hertz. The rivalry between supporters of direct and alternating current in the late 1800s is known as the war of the currents."],["Circuit diagrams","Engineers draw circuits using standard symbols for batteries, wires, resistors, switches, bulbs and other components. Circuit diagrams make it easy to understand and build circuits, from simple school experiments to complex electronic devices."],["Electronic circuits","Electronic circuits use components such as resistors, capacitors, diodes and transistors to control electricity precisely. Transistors act as tiny switches and amplifiers. An integrated circuit, or microchip, packs billions of transistors onto a small piece of silicon, making computers and phones possible."],["Short circuits","A short circuit happens when current finds an unintended low-resistance path, such as when damaged wires touch. The current can become very large, heating wires and causing sparks or fires. Faulty wiring and overloaded outlets are a common cause of house fires, including in the Philippines, where fire officials urge people to avoid octopus connections that plug many devices into one outlet."],["Electricity and the body","The human body can conduct electricity, and currents passing through the heart can be deadly. Wet skin lowers the body’s resistance, which is why electrical appliances should never be used near water. Interestingly, the body itself uses tiny electrical signals in nerves and heart muscle."],["Superconducting circuits","Some materials lose all electrical resistance when cooled to very low temperatures, becoming superconductors. Superconducting circuits are used in MRI scanners, particle accelerators and some quantum computers. Scientists are searching for materials that superconduct at higher temperatures."]],
sp:["Ohm’s doubted discovery","In the 1820s, Georg Ohm, a German schoolteacher, carried out careful experiments with wires of different lengths and thicknesses. He found a simple relationship: the current through a wire is proportional to the voltage across it and inversely proportional to its resistance. When he published his work in 1827, many scientists in Germany dismissed it, and Ohm struggled to find a university post. Recognition came years later, when the Royal Society in London awarded him its Copley Medal in 1841. Today the unit of resistance, the ohm, is named after him, and Ohm’s law is taught to students around the world."],
g:[["Circuit","A closed path for electric current"],["Voltage","The push that drives current"],["Current","The flow of electric charge"],["Resistance","Opposition to the flow of current"]],
f:["A circuit must be complete for current to flow.","Homes are wired in parallel.","Ohm’s law links voltage, current and resistance."],
fun:"A modern microchip can hold billions of tiny circuit switches called transistors.",
tl:"Ang electric circuit ay saradong daanan ng kuryente. Kailangang buo ito upang dumaloy ang kuryente. Inilalarawan ito ng voltage, current at resistance, na pinag-uugnay ng batas ni Ohm."});

ADDART({id:"keplerlaws",t:"Kepler’s Laws of Planetary Motion",c:"Astronomy",i:"🪐",w:"Kepler's laws of planetary motion",r:["copern","galileo","grav","solar","newton"],
p:[["Ellipses","Planets move in oval-shaped paths called ellipses."],["Faster when closer","Planets speed up when they are nearer the Sun."],["A mathematical pattern","Kepler found a rule linking a planet’s distance and its year."]],
l:"Kepler’s laws of planetary motion are three rules describing how planets move around the Sun, discovered by the German astronomer Johannes Kepler in the early 1600s. Using precise observations of Mars made by the Danish astronomer Tycho Brahe, Kepler found that planets travel in ellipses rather than perfect circles, that they move faster when closer to the Sun, and that there is a precise mathematical relationship between a planet’s distance from the Sun and the time it takes to complete an orbit. His laws supported the idea that the Sun, not Earth, is at the center of the solar system, and they later helped Isaac Newton develop his law of universal gravitation. They also apply to moons, comets, satellites and exoplanets.",
s:[["The first law","Each planet moves in an ellipse with the Sun at one of its two focus points. An ellipse is a stretched circle. Most planetary orbits are nearly circular, but Mars’s orbit is noticeably elongated, which is why Kepler’s study of Mars revealed the pattern."],["The second law","A line joining a planet to the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times. This means a planet moves faster when it is closer to the Sun and slower when it is farther away. Earth moves fastest in early January, when it is nearest the Sun."],["The third law","The square of a planet’s orbital period is proportional to the cube of its average distance from the Sun. Planets farther from the Sun take much longer to orbit. For example, Jupiter is about five times farther from the Sun than Earth and takes about 12 years to complete an orbit."],["Brahe’s data","Tycho Brahe made the most accurate naked-eye observations of the planets ever recorded, from his observatory in Denmark. After Brahe’s death in 1601, Kepler, his assistant, used these records. Fitting the data for Mars took him years of calculations."]],
k:[["Discoverer","Johannes Kepler"],["Data source","Tycho Brahe’s observations"],["First and second laws published","1609"],["Third law published","1619"],["Explained later by","Newton’s law of gravitation"]],
d:[["1543","Copernicus proposes a Sun-centered system"],["1576","Tycho Brahe begins observations at his island observatory"],["1600","Kepler joins Brahe in Prague"],["1609","Kepler publishes his first two laws in Astronomia Nova"],["1619","Kepler publishes his third law in Harmonices Mundi"],["1687","Newton explains Kepler’s laws using gravity in the Principia"]],
s2:[["Breaking with circles","For about 2,000 years, astronomers assumed that heavenly bodies moved in perfect circles, which seemed fitting for the heavens. Even Copernicus used circles. Kepler’s decision to abandon circles for ellipses, after his calculations for Mars disagreed with Brahe’s data by a small amount, was a major break with tradition and showed his commitment to evidence."],["Kepler’s life","Johannes Kepler was born in 1571 in Germany and suffered from poor eyesight after childhood smallpox. He was a devout Christian who believed that God had created the universe according to mathematical harmony. He faced religious persecution as a Lutheran, and his mother was put on trial for witchcraft; Kepler defended her, and she was eventually released."],["Newton’s explanation","Kepler described how planets move but could not fully explain why. In 1687 Isaac Newton showed that all three of Kepler’s laws follow mathematically from his laws of motion and his law of universal gravitation. This was a triumph that united the physics of Earth and the heavens."],["Using the laws today","Kepler’s laws are used to plan the paths of spacecraft and satellites and to predict the return of comets. Astronomers use his third law to calculate the masses of stars and planets from the orbits of objects around them, and to study exoplanets discovered around other stars."],["The Kepler telescope","NASA’s Kepler space telescope, launched in 2009 and named in his honor, watched a patch of sky for tiny dips in starlight caused by planets passing in front of their stars. It discovered thousands of exoplanets before retiring in 2018, showing that planets are common in our galaxy."],["Kepler and science fiction","Kepler also wrote Somnium, a story published after his death in 1634, about a journey to the Moon and what Earth would look like from there. Some consider it one of the earliest works of science fiction. It used real astronomy to imagine life on another world."]],
sp:["The war with Mars","When Kepler began studying the orbit of Mars, he expected to solve it in eight days. It took him about six years. He tried circle after circle, adjusting their positions, but his best model still disagreed with Brahe’s observations by about 8 minutes of arc, a tiny fraction of a degree. Kepler trusted Brahe’s data and refused to ignore the difference. He later wrote that those 8 minutes led to a reform of all astronomy. Finally, he tried an ellipse, and it fit perfectly. Kepler called his long struggle his war with Mars, and his victory changed how humanity understood the solar system."],
g:[["Ellipse","An oval-shaped curve"],["Orbit","The path of an object around another"],["Orbital period","The time to complete one orbit"],["Focus","One of two special points inside an ellipse"]],
f:["Planets move in ellipses.","Planets move faster when closer to the Sun.","Kepler used Tycho Brahe’s observations."],
fun:"Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, during the Northern Hemisphere’s winter.",
tl:"Ang mga batas ni Kepler ay naglalarawan kung paano umiikot ang mga planeta sa Araw. Gumagalaw ang mga planeta sa hugis-itlog na landas na tinatawag na ellipse. Mas mabilis sila kapag malapit sa Araw."});

ADDART({id:"nucleusdisc",t:"Discovering the Atomic Nucleus",c:"History of Science",i:"⚛️",w:"Geiger–Marsden experiments",r:["atom","electron","radioact","quarks","curie"],
p:[["A tiny center","Almost all of an atom’s mass is packed into a tiny nucleus."],["The gold foil experiment","Rutherford’s team fired particles at thin gold foil."],["A big surprise","A few particles bounced back, revealing the nucleus."]],
l:"The atomic nucleus was discovered in 1911 by the New Zealand-born physicist Ernest Rutherford, based on experiments carried out by Hans Geiger and Ernest Marsden at the University of Manchester. They fired tiny, positively charged alpha particles at a very thin sheet of gold foil. Most passed straight through, but a few bounced off at large angles, and some came almost straight back. Rutherford concluded that an atom’s positive charge and nearly all its mass are concentrated in a tiny, dense center, the nucleus, with electrons moving around it through mostly empty space. This overturned the earlier plum pudding model and laid the foundation for nuclear physics. Later discoveries showed that the nucleus is made of protons and neutrons.",
s:[["The plum pudding model","In 1904 J. J. Thomson, who had discovered the electron, proposed that atoms were like a pudding of positive charge with negative electrons scattered through it like plums. In this model, the positive charge was spread out, so fast particles should pass through with only small deflections."],["The experiment","Between 1909 and 1913, Geiger and Marsden directed a beam of alpha particles from a radioactive source at gold foil only a few hundred atoms thick. In a darkened room, they counted tiny flashes of light on a screen, each marking where an alpha particle landed. Most went straight through, but about 1 in 8,000 bounced back at large angles."],["Rutherford’s explanation","Rutherford realized that large deflections could only happen if the alpha particles encountered a small, very concentrated positive charge. In 1911 he proposed the nuclear model: a tiny nucleus surrounded by electrons. If an atom were the size of a stadium, its nucleus would be about the size of a small marble at the center."],["Protons and neutrons","In 1917 Rutherford showed that the hydrogen nucleus, later called the proton, is found in other nuclei. In 1932 James Chadwick, his former student, discovered the neutron, a neutral particle in the nucleus. These discoveries explained the masses of atoms and the existence of isotopes."]],
k:[["Year proposed","1911"],["Scientist","Ernest Rutherford"],["Experimenters","Hans Geiger and Ernest Marsden"],["Target","Thin gold foil"],["Model replaced","Plum pudding model"]],
d:[["1897","J. J. Thomson discovers the electron"],["1904","Thomson proposes the plum pudding model"],["1909","Geiger and Marsden observe alpha particles bouncing back from gold foil"],["1911","Rutherford proposes the nuclear model of the atom"],["1913","Niels Bohr places electrons in fixed orbits around the nucleus"],["1932","James Chadwick discovers the neutron"]],
s2:[["Rutherford the experimenter","Ernest Rutherford, born on a farm in New Zealand in 1871, became one of the greatest experimental physicists. He had already won the 1908 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for his work on radioactivity, before discovering the nucleus. Later he led the Cavendish Laboratory in Cambridge, where his team split the atom. The element rutherfordium is named after him."],["Bohr’s improvement","Rutherford’s model had a problem: according to classical physics, orbiting electrons should lose energy and spiral into the nucleus. In 1913 the Danish physicist Niels Bohr, who had worked with Rutherford, suggested that electrons occupy fixed energy levels and jump between them by absorbing or releasing light. This step helped launch quantum physics."],["Mostly empty space","Rutherford’s discovery showed that atoms, and therefore everything around us, are mostly empty space. The nucleus is around 100,000 times smaller than the whole atom. Objects feel solid because the electrons of atoms repel those of other atoms."],["What holds the nucleus together","Protons in the nucleus all have positive charge and repel each other strongly. Scientists later discovered the strong nuclear force, which acts only at very short distances and binds protons and neutrons together. Releasing some of this binding energy powers nuclear reactors, atomic bombs and the Sun."],["Splitting the atom","In 1932, at Rutherford’s Cavendish Laboratory, John Cockcroft and Ernest Walton used a particle accelerator to split lithium nuclei, the first artificial splitting of an atom using accelerated particles. This work, and later discoveries of nuclear fission, led to both nuclear power and nuclear weapons."],["Inside protons","Scientists later found that protons and neutrons themselves are made of smaller particles called quarks, held together by gluons. Experiments similar in spirit to Rutherford’s, firing electrons at protons at the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center in the late 1960s, provided the first evidence for these particles."]],
sp:["Like a shell bouncing off tissue paper","Rutherford later described his amazement at the results of the gold foil experiment. He said it was almost as incredible as if you fired a large artillery shell at a piece of tissue paper and it came back and hit you. According to the plum pudding model, alpha particles should have passed through the foil with only slight nudges. Yet some were bounced right back. Rutherford spent months working out the mathematics before announcing in 1911 that the atom must have a tiny, massive nucleus. A careful look at a few unexpected flashes of light had revealed the heart of the atom."],
g:[["Nucleus","The tiny, dense center of an atom"],["Alpha particle","A positively charged particle given off by some radioactive materials"],["Plum pudding model","An early model of the atom"],["Neutron","A neutral particle in the nucleus"]],
f:["Most alpha particles passed straight through the gold foil.","The nucleus holds nearly all of an atom’s mass.","The neutron was discovered in 1932."],
fun:"If an atom were the size of a sports stadium, its nucleus would be about the size of a marble.",
tl:"Natuklasan ni Ernest Rutherford noong 1911 ang nucleus ng atom. Pinatamaan ng kanyang koponan ng maliliit na partikulo ang manipis na gintong foil. Ipinakita nito na halos lahat ng masa ng atom ay nasa maliit na gitna nito."});

;
/* data/science12.js */
// Science: microscopes, fractions, topology, radiocarbon dating and the nitrogen cycle.
ADDART({id:"microscopes",t:"The Microscope",c:"History of Science",i:"🔬",w:"Microscope",r:["cell","microbe","pasteur","light","scope"],
p:[["Seeing the invisible","Microscopes reveal things too small for the naked eye."],["A hidden world","Early microscopes revealed cells and microbes."],["Down to atoms","Modern microscopes can show individual atoms."]],
l:"A microscope is an instrument that makes very small objects appear larger, revealing details invisible to the naked eye. Light microscopes use glass lenses to magnify objects up to about 1,000 or 2,000 times, enough to see cells and bacteria. Electron microscopes use beams of electrons instead of light and can magnify millions of times, showing viruses, molecules and even individual atoms. The invention of the microscope in the late 1500s and 1600s opened up a hidden world: Robert Hooke described cells in 1665, and Antonie van Leeuwenhoek was the first to observe bacteria and other microorganisms. Microscopes are essential tools in biology, medicine, materials science and many other fields, from diagnosing diseases to designing computer chips.",
s:[["How a light microscope works","A compound light microscope has two sets of lenses. The objective lens, close to the specimen, forms an enlarged image, which the eyepiece lens magnifies further. Light shines through a thin sample on a glass slide. Stains are often added to make cells and their parts easier to see."],["Limits of light","Light microscopes cannot clearly show objects smaller than about 200 nanometers, roughly half the wavelength of visible light, because light waves blur such tiny details. This limit, described by Ernst Abbe in 1873, means most viruses cannot be seen with ordinary light microscopes."],["Electron microscopes","Electron microscopes, first built in the 1930s, use electrons, which have much shorter wavelengths than light. Transmission electron microscopes send electrons through ultra-thin samples, while scanning electron microscopes bounce electrons off surfaces to create detailed three-dimensional-looking images. Samples usually must be placed in a vacuum."],["Beyond the limit","In recent decades, scientists developed super-resolution fluorescence microscopes that get around the light limit, using glowing molecules switched on and off. This won the 2014 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Cryo-electron microscopy, which freezes samples to study molecules in detail, won the 2017 prize."]],
k:[["Light microscope magnification","Up to about 2,000×"],["Electron microscope magnification","Millions of times"],["First described cells","Robert Hooke, 1665"],["First saw bacteria","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek"],["First electron microscope","1931"]],
d:[["c. 1590","Dutch spectacle makers build early compound microscopes"],["1665","Robert Hooke publishes Micrographia and names cells"],["1676","Van Leeuwenhoek reports tiny living creatures in water"],["1873","Ernst Abbe explains the resolution limit of light microscopes"],["1931","Ernst Ruska and Max Knoll build the first electron microscope"],["1981","The scanning tunneling microscope is invented, imaging individual atoms"]],
s2:[["Hooke’s Micrographia","In 1665 the English scientist Robert Hooke published Micrographia, a book of detailed drawings of things seen through his microscope, including a flea, a louse, the eye of a fly and thin slices of cork. The cork was made of tiny compartments that reminded him of monks’ cells, so he called them cells. The book became a bestseller and amazed readers."],["Van Leeuwenhoek’s animalcules","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, a Dutch cloth merchant with no university training, ground tiny, powerful lenses and built simple microscopes that magnified over 200 times. From the 1670s he sent letters to the Royal Society in London describing tiny moving creatures, which he called animalcules, in pond water, rainwater and scrapings from his teeth. These were bacteria and protists, seen for the first time."],["Microscopes and medicine","Microscopes allowed scientists such as Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch to identify the germs that cause diseases like tuberculosis, cholera and anthrax in the late 1800s. Today, doctors and laboratory technicians use microscopes to examine blood, tissue samples and bacteria, diagnosing illnesses such as malaria, tuberculosis and cancer."],["Seeing atoms","In 1981 Gerd Binnig and Heinrich Rohrer at IBM invented the scanning tunneling microscope, which uses a very sharp tip passing extremely close to a surface to map individual atoms. In 1989 IBM scientists used a similar instrument to arrange 35 xenon atoms to spell the company’s name, showing that atoms could be moved one by one."],["Foldscope","In 2014 researchers at Stanford University introduced the Foldscope, a paper microscope that can be folded from a flat sheet and costs about one US dollar. It was designed to bring microscopy to students and health workers in places with limited resources, and has been distributed in many countries."],["Microscopes in Philippine science","Philippine scientists use microscopes in research at institutions such as the University of the Philippines and the Research Institute for Tropical Medicine, studying tropical diseases, marine life and new materials. Electron microscopes are used to study the structure of materials and microorganisms in local laboratories."]],
sp:["Leeuwenhoek’s teeth","In 1683 Antonie van Leeuwenhoek scraped some white material from between his teeth, mixed it with clean rainwater and looked at it through one of his tiny handmade microscopes. He was astonished to see many very small living creatures moving about, some darting like fish, others spinning in circles. He wrote that there were more animals in his mouth than there were people in the whole Netherlands. He had observed bacteria, though it would take nearly two centuries before scientists understood their role in disease. Van Leeuwenhoek kept his lens-making methods secret, and for many years no one could match his microscopes."],
g:[["Microscope","An instrument that magnifies tiny objects"],["Lens","A curved piece of glass that bends light"],["Magnification","How much larger an object appears"],["Resolution","The ability to show fine detail"]],
f:["Robert Hooke named cells.","Van Leeuwenhoek first saw bacteria.","Electron microscopes can show atoms."],
fun:"A Foldscope paper microscope can be made from a flat sheet and costs about the price of a candy bar.",
tl:"Ang mikroskopyo ay instrumento na nagpapalaki sa maliliit na bagay. Sa pamamagitan nito, natuklasan ang mga cell at bakterya. Kaya ng makabagong electron microscope na ipakita ang mga atom."});

ADDART({id:"fractions",t:"Fractions and Decimals",c:"Mathematics",i:"½",w:"Fraction",r:["percent","zero","alg","measure","negativenumbers"],
p:[["Parts of a whole","A fraction shows a part of a whole, like one half."],["Numerator and denominator","The top and bottom numbers have special names."],["Another way to write them","Decimals are another way of writing fractions."]],
l:"Fractions are numbers that represent parts of a whole or ratios between quantities. A fraction is written with a numerator on top, showing how many parts are taken, and a denominator below, showing how many equal parts the whole is divided into; for example, three quarters is written 3/4. Decimals express fractions using powers of ten, so one half equals 0.5 and three quarters equals 0.75. Fractions and decimals are used every day in cooking, shopping, measuring, money, sports statistics and science. Ancient Egyptians, Babylonians, Indians and Chinese all developed ways of writing fractions, and decimal notation spread widely in Europe after the 1500s. Percentages are fractions out of one hundred.",
s:[["Types of fractions","A proper fraction, such as 2/3, has a numerator smaller than its denominator and is less than one. An improper fraction, such as 7/4, is greater than one and can also be written as a mixed number, 1¾. Equivalent fractions, such as 1/2, 2/4 and 4/8, have the same value."],["Adding and subtracting","To add or subtract fractions, they must have the same denominator. For example, to add 1/2 and 1/3, both are rewritten in sixths, as 3/6 and 2/6, which add up to 5/6. Finding the least common denominator makes calculations simpler."],["Multiplying and dividing","To multiply fractions, multiply the numerators and the denominators: 2/3 times 3/4 equals 6/12, or 1/2. To divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal, the fraction turned upside down. So dividing by 1/2 is the same as multiplying by 2."],["Decimals","In a decimal number, digits to the right of the decimal point represent tenths, hundredths, thousandths and so on. Some fractions, such as 1/4, become decimals that end, 0.25. Others, such as 1/3, become repeating decimals, 0.333 and so on forever."]],
k:[["Top number","Numerator"],["Bottom number","Denominator"],["One half as decimal","0.5"],["Percent meaning","Out of one hundred"],["Decimal point in the Philippines","A period, as in 2.5"]],
d:[["c. 1800 BC","Babylonians write fractions using a base-60 system"],["c. 1650 BC","The Egyptian Rhind Papyrus shows calculations with unit fractions"],["c. 200 BC","Chinese mathematicians use decimal fractions in calculations"],["c. 1200","Fibonacci helps spread fractions written with a horizontal bar in Europe"],["1585","Simon Stevin publishes a booklet promoting decimal fractions"],["1795","France adopts the decimal metric system"]],
s2:[["Egyptian fractions","Ancient Egyptians wrote most fractions as sums of unit fractions, which have a numerator of one, such as 1/2, 1/4 and 1/7. For example, they would write 3/4 as 1/2 plus 1/4. The Rhind Mathematical Papyrus, copied about 1650 BC, contains tables for converting fractions in this way. Mathematicians still study puzzles about Egyptian fractions today."],["The decimal revolution","In 1585 the Flemish mathematician Simon Stevin published a short booklet called De Thiende, or The Tenth, arguing that decimal fractions would make calculations easier for merchants, surveyors and astronomers. He predicted that coins, weights and measures would one day be decimal. The metric system adopted in France in 1795 and later worldwide fulfilled his vision."],["Fractions in daily life","Recipes use fractions such as half a cup of sugar. Shops advertise discounts as percentages. Basketball statistics use decimals and percentages for shooting accuracy. Money in the Philippines is decimal: one peso equals 100 centavos, so 2.50 pesos means two pesos and 50 centavos."],["Ratios and rates","Fractions also describe ratios, such as 1 part concentrate to 4 parts water, and rates, such as kilometers per hour. Scale drawings and maps use ratios like 1:50,000. Probability is often expressed as a fraction, such as the 1/6 chance of rolling a particular number on a die."],["Rational and irrational numbers","Numbers that can be written as fractions of whole numbers are called rational numbers. Some numbers, such as the square root of 2 and pi, cannot be written as fractions and are called irrational numbers; their decimals go on forever without repeating. The discovery of irrational numbers by ancient Greek mathematicians is said to have shocked them."],["Learning fractions","Many students find fractions difficult, and research suggests that understanding fractions is an important predictor of later success in algebra. Teachers use pizzas, chocolate bars, number lines and measuring cups to help students picture fractions. Some educators debate how early and in what order fraction topics should be taught."]],
sp:["The third-pound burger","In the 1980s, the A&W restaurant chain in the United States introduced a third-pound burger to compete with McDonald’s quarter-pounder. It offered more meat for the same price and did well in taste tests. Yet it did not sell well. According to the company’s owner, research found that many customers thought a third of a pound was smaller than a quarter of a pound, because three is smaller than four. The story is often told to show why understanding fractions matters, since a larger denominator actually means smaller pieces."],
g:[["Fraction","A number showing part of a whole"],["Numerator","The top number of a fraction"],["Denominator","The bottom number of a fraction"],["Decimal","A number written using tenths, hundredths and so on"]],
f:["One half equals 0.5.","Percent means out of one hundred.","Egyptians wrote fractions as sums of unit fractions."],
fun:"The fraction 1/7 as a decimal repeats the digits 142857 forever.",
tl:"Ang praksyon ay bilang na nagpapakita ng bahagi ng kabuuan, tulad ng kalahati. Ang numerator ay nasa itaas at ang denominator ay nasa ibaba. Ang decimal ay isa pang paraan ng pagsulat ng praksyon."});

ADDART({id:"topology",t:"Topology",c:"Mathematics",i:"🍩",w:"Topology",r:["shapes","euclid","graphs","infin","maps"],
p:[["Rubber-sheet geometry","Topology studies shapes that can be stretched without tearing."],["Donut and mug","To a topologist, a donut and a coffee mug are the same shape."],["Bridges and knots","Topology began with a puzzle about seven bridges."]],
l:"Topology is a branch of mathematics that studies the properties of shapes that stay the same when they are stretched, twisted, bent or squeezed, but not torn or glued. It is sometimes called rubber-sheet geometry. In topology, distances and angles do not matter; what matters is how a shape is connected, such as how many holes it has. A doughnut and a coffee mug with one handle are considered the same shape because each has one hole and one could be smoothly deformed into the other. Topology grew from Leonhard Euler’s 1736 solution to the Seven Bridges of Königsberg puzzle. Today it is used in physics, biology, computer science, data analysis and the study of the shape of the universe.",
s:[["Holes matter","In topology, a sphere, a cube and a bowl are all equivalent because none has a hole through it. A doughnut, a ring and a mug have one hole. A pretzel shape may have two or three. Mathematicians call the number of holes in a surface its genus."],["The Möbius strip","A Möbius strip is made by giving a strip of paper a half twist and joining its ends. It has only one side and one edge: an ant walking along it could cover both faces without crossing an edge. If you cut it down the middle, it does not fall into two pieces but forms one longer loop."],["Knots","Knot theory, a part of topology, studies closed loops in three-dimensional space and when one knot can be untangled into another without cutting. It has applications in understanding how DNA strands tangle and untangle, and in physics and chemistry."],["Networks","Topology also studies how points are connected, regardless of distances. A subway map is a topological map: it shows the order of stations and connections correctly, even though distances and directions are distorted. Computer networks are also described by their topology."]],
k:[["Also called","Rubber-sheet geometry"],["Founding problem","Seven Bridges of Königsberg"],["Founder","Leonhard Euler, 1736"],["One-sided surface","Möbius strip"],["Famous solved problem","Poincaré conjecture"]],
d:[["1736","Leonhard Euler solves the Seven Bridges of Königsberg problem"],["1847","Johann Listing uses the word topology in print"],["1858","August Möbius and Johann Listing describe the one-sided strip"],["1904","Henri Poincaré poses his famous conjecture"],["2003","Grigori Perelman posts proofs of the Poincaré conjecture"],["2016","The Nobel Prize in Physics honors topological discoveries in materials"]],
s2:[["The seven bridges","The city of Königsberg, now Kaliningrad in Russia, had seven bridges connecting two islands and the riverbanks. People wondered whether one could walk through the city crossing each bridge exactly once. In 1736 Leonhard Euler proved it was impossible by representing land areas as points and bridges as lines, showing that the answer depends only on how they are connected. This founded both graph theory and topology."],["The Poincaré conjecture","In 1904 the French mathematician Henri Poincaré proposed a statement about the shapes of three-dimensional spaces, roughly that any such space without holes is equivalent to a sphere. It became one of the most famous unsolved problems in mathematics. In 2002 and 2003, the Russian mathematician Grigori Perelman posted proofs online. He declined both the Fields Medal in 2006 and a one-million-dollar prize offered in 2010."],["Topology in physics","In 2016 the Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded to David Thouless, Duncan Haldane and Michael Kosterlitz for using topology to explain unusual states of matter. Topological materials, whose electrical properties stay stable even when they are bent or imperfect, are being explored for future electronics and quantum computers."],["The shape of the universe","Topology helps cosmologists ask questions about the overall shape of space. Could the universe be finite but without edges, like the surface of a sphere or a doughnut extended to three dimensions? Observations of the cosmic microwave background have so far found no evidence of such a pattern, but the question remains open."],["Hairy ball theorem","A playful result in topology, the hairy ball theorem, states that you cannot comb all the hair on a hairy ball flat without creating at least one tuft or bald spot. One consequence is that at any moment, there must be at least one place on Earth where the horizontal wind speed is zero."],["Topology and data","Topological data analysis is a newer field that uses topology to find shapes and patterns in large, complex data sets, such as medical records or images. It looks for loops, clusters and holes in data that may reveal hidden structure."]],
sp:["Perelman’s refusals","For nearly a century, mathematicians tried and failed to prove the Poincaré conjecture. Then, in late 2002, a reclusive Russian mathematician named Grigori Perelman quietly posted a series of papers on the internet. Over the next few years, experts checked his work and confirmed that he had solved the problem. In 2006 he was awarded the Fields Medal, often called the Nobel Prize of mathematics, but he declined it. In 2010 he was offered a one-million-dollar Millennium Prize and declined that too, reportedly saying that he felt the contributions of another mathematician, Richard Hamilton, were equally important. Perelman then withdrew from mathematics and public life."],
g:[["Topology","The study of shapes under stretching and bending"],["Genus","The number of holes in a surface"],["Möbius strip","A surface with only one side"],["Conjecture","A mathematical statement not yet proven"]],
f:["To a topologist, a donut and a mug are the same.","A Möbius strip has only one side.","Euler founded topology with a bridge puzzle."],
fun:"Topology proves that somewhere on Earth, the horizontal wind is always perfectly still.",
tl:"Ang topology ay sangay ng matematika na nag-aaral ng mga hugis na maaaring banatin o baluktutin nang hindi pinupunit. Para sa topology, magkapareho ang donut at tasa dahil parehong may isang butas. Nagsimula ito sa palaisipan ni Euler tungkol sa pitong tulay."});

ADDART({id:"carbondating",t:"Radiocarbon Dating",c:"Science",i:"🦴",w:"Radiocarbon dating",r:["isotopes","radioact","archaeology","fossils","carbonchem"],
p:[["A natural clock","Radiocarbon dating measures the age of once-living things."],["Carbon-14","It uses a radioactive form of carbon that decays at a known rate."],["Up to 50,000 years","It works for remains up to about 50,000 years old."]],
l:"Radiocarbon dating is a method of finding the age of materials that were once alive, such as wood, bone, charcoal, shells and cloth, by measuring how much carbon-14 they contain. Carbon-14 is a radioactive isotope of carbon formed in the atmosphere. Living things take in carbon-14 while they are alive, but after death the carbon-14 slowly decays, halving about every 5,730 years. By comparing how much carbon-14 remains with how much a living thing contains, scientists can calculate how long ago the organism died. Developed by the American chemist Willard Libby in the late 1940s, radiocarbon dating transformed archaeology, geology and climate science. It works for remains up to about 50,000 years old.",
s:[["How carbon-14 forms","Cosmic rays from space strike atoms in the upper atmosphere, producing neutrons that turn nitrogen atoms into carbon-14. This carbon-14 combines with oxygen to form carbon dioxide, which plants absorb through photosynthesis. Animals take it in by eating plants, so all living things contain a small, steady proportion of carbon-14."],["Half-life","After an organism dies, it stops taking in carbon, and its carbon-14 decays back into nitrogen. Half of it disappears in about 5,730 years, half of what remains in another 5,730 years, and so on. After about 50,000 years, too little remains to measure reliably."],["Measuring the carbon","Early methods counted the radiation given off by decaying carbon-14 atoms, requiring large samples. Since the late 1970s, accelerator mass spectrometry has allowed scientists to count carbon-14 atoms directly, using samples as small as a few milligrams, such as a single seed or a tiny piece of cloth."],["Calibration","The amount of carbon-14 in the atmosphere has varied over time, so raw radiocarbon ages must be adjusted, or calibrated. Scientists use tree rings, cave formations, corals and lake sediments with known ages to build calibration curves that convert radiocarbon years into calendar years."]],
k:[["Isotope used","Carbon-14"],["Half-life","About 5,730 years"],["Useful range","Up to about 50,000 years"],["Inventor","Willard Libby"],["Nobel Prize","Chemistry, 1960"]],
d:[["1940","Martin Kamen and Sam Ruben discover carbon-14"],["1949","Willard Libby’s team publishes the first radiocarbon dates"],["1960","Libby wins the Nobel Prize in Chemistry"],["1977","Accelerator mass spectrometry is first used for radiocarbon dating"],["1988","Radiocarbon tests date the Shroud of Turin to the Middle Ages"],["2020","A new international calibration curve is published"]],
s2:[["Testing the method","To prove that his method worked, Willard Libby tested samples of known age, such as wood from ancient Egyptian tombs dated by historical records. The radiocarbon dates matched closely. Archaeologists soon realized they could date sites anywhere in the world without written records."],["Radiocarbon in the Philippines","Radiocarbon dating has helped Philippine archaeologists date sites such as the Tabon Caves in Palawan, where human remains and artifacts tens of thousands of years old have been found, and the jar burials and shell middens of other regions. It has also been used to study the age of the Cordillera rice terraces. Some studies have suggested the terraces are younger than long believed, a question still debated among researchers."],["The bomb pulse","Nuclear weapons tests in the 1950s and early 1960s nearly doubled the amount of carbon-14 in the atmosphere. This bomb pulse has since been declining. Scientists use it to date things from recent decades, such as the age of human cells, ivory from poached elephants and fine wines, to detect fakes."],["The Shroud of Turin","In 1988, three laboratories radiocarbon-dated small samples of the Shroud of Turin, a cloth that some believe wrapped the body of Jesus. They dated the linen to between about 1260 and 1390. Many scientists accept this result, while some supporters of the shroud’s authenticity argue that the samples came from a medieval repair or were contaminated. The debate continues."],["Limits and errors","Radiocarbon dating can only be used on materials that contain carbon from living things, so it cannot date stone tools or rocks directly, though it can date charcoal or bones found with them. Contamination by younger or older carbon can produce misleading results, so samples must be carefully cleaned. Results are given with a margin of error."],["Other dating methods","For older materials, scientists use other radioactive clocks. Potassium-argon and uranium-lead dating can measure ages of millions or billions of years, and are used to date volcanic rocks and the age of Earth. Tree-ring counting, called dendrochronology, gives exact calendar years for wood."]],
sp:["Libby’s atomic clock","In the 1940s, Willard Libby, a chemist who had worked on the Manhattan Project, had an idea: if cosmic rays steadily produced carbon-14, then every living thing should contain the same proportion, and that amount should decline at a known rate after death. Many colleagues doubted that such tiny amounts could be measured. Libby and his students at the University of Chicago built sensitive detectors shielded from background radiation. In 1949 they published dates for samples of known age, and the results matched. Libby called it an atomic clock, and in 1960 he received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."],
g:[["Isotope","A form of an element with a different number of neutrons"],["Half-life","The time for half of a radioactive substance to decay"],["Calibration","Adjusting measurements to match known standards"],["Carbon-14","A radioactive form of carbon"]],
f:["Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years.","Radiocarbon dating works on once-living things.","Willard Libby invented the method."],
fun:"Nuclear bomb tests left a carbon-14 signal that scientists now use to date wines and catch ivory smugglers.",
tl:"Ang radiocarbon dating ay paraan upang malaman ang edad ng mga bagay na dating buhay. Ginagamit nito ang carbon-14, na nabubulok sa kilalang bilis. Nakatulong ito sa pagtukoy ng edad ng mga labi sa Tabon Caves sa Palawan."});

ADDART({id:"nitrogencycle",t:"The Nitrogen Cycle",c:"Science",i:"🔄",w:"Nitrogen cycle",r:["soil","eco","microbe","agri","wcycle"],
p:[["Air full of nitrogen","Nitrogen makes up about 78 percent of the air."],["Needed by life","All living things need nitrogen to build proteins and DNA."],["Bacteria at work","Bacteria turn nitrogen gas into forms plants can use."]],
l:"The nitrogen cycle is the natural process by which nitrogen moves between the air, soil, water and living things. Nitrogen gas makes up about 78 percent of Earth’s atmosphere, but most organisms cannot use it directly because its two atoms are tightly bonded. Certain bacteria, some living in the roots of beans and other legumes, convert nitrogen gas into ammonia, a process called nitrogen fixation. Other bacteria turn ammonia into nitrates, which plants absorb to build proteins and DNA. Animals get nitrogen by eating plants. When living things die or produce waste, decomposers return nitrogen to the soil, and still other bacteria release it back into the air. Since the early 1900s, humans have greatly altered this cycle by making artificial fertilizers.",
s:[["Nitrogen fixation","Nitrogen-fixing bacteria break the strong bond in nitrogen gas and combine nitrogen with hydrogen to form ammonia. Some, such as Rhizobium, live in nodules on the roots of legumes like beans, peanuts and mung beans, exchanging nitrogen for sugars. Lightning also fixes small amounts of nitrogen in the air."],["Nitrification","Other soil bacteria convert ammonia first into nitrites and then into nitrates, a process called nitrification. Nitrates dissolve easily in water and are absorbed by plant roots. Plants use the nitrogen to make amino acids, proteins, chlorophyll and DNA."],["Decomposition","When plants and animals die, or when animals produce urine and dung, fungi and bacteria break down the nitrogen-containing compounds, releasing ammonia back into the soil. This step, called ammonification, recycles nitrogen so it can be used again."],["Denitrification","In waterlogged soils and other places with little oxygen, denitrifying bacteria convert nitrates back into nitrogen gas, which returns to the atmosphere. This completes the cycle. Flooded rice fields are important places for denitrification."]],
k:[["Nitrogen in air","About 78 percent"],["Key bacteria","Rhizobium and others"],["Plant-usable forms","Ammonium and nitrate"],["Industrial process","Haber–Bosch"],["Legume examples","Beans, peanuts, mung beans"]],
d:[["1772","Daniel Rutherford discovers nitrogen"],["1888","Hellriegel and Wilfarth show legumes fix nitrogen with bacteria"],["1909","Fritz Haber shows how to make ammonia from nitrogen and hydrogen"],["1913","The first industrial Haber–Bosch ammonia plant opens in Germany"],["1918","Haber receives the Nobel Prize in Chemistry"],["2009","Scientists identify nitrogen pollution as one of the planetary boundaries being exceeded"]],
s2:[["Feeding billions","In 1909 the German chemist Fritz Haber found a way to combine nitrogen from the air with hydrogen to make ammonia, and Carl Bosch scaled it up into an industrial process. The Haber–Bosch process produces the nitrogen fertilizers that grow much of the world’s food. Scientists estimate that roughly half of the nitrogen in people’s bodies today came originally from such factories."],["A complicated legacy","Fritz Haber’s discovery helped feed the world, but he also led Germany’s development of poison gas in World War I, and ammonia from his process was used to make explosives. This has made him one of the most debated figures in the history of science, admired for his contribution to food production and condemned for his role in chemical warfare."],["Too much nitrogen","Excess fertilizer and animal waste can wash into rivers, lakes and seas, causing algae to grow out of control. When the algae die and decay, they use up oxygen, creating dead zones where fish cannot survive. Nitrogen pollution also produces nitrous oxide, a powerful greenhouse gas, and contributes to air pollution."],["Nitrogen in Philippine farming","Filipino rice and corn farmers rely heavily on nitrogen fertilizers such as urea, and fertilizer prices strongly affect farm incomes and food prices. Researchers at the International Rice Research Institute and Philippine universities study ways to use fertilizer more efficiently, including applying it at the right time and amount. Some farmers grow mung beans or other legumes between rice crops to naturally add nitrogen to the soil."],["Organic and natural methods","Organic farming uses compost, manure, legumes and crop rotation instead of synthetic fertilizers to supply nitrogen. Supporters say this is better for soil health and the environment, while others argue that organic methods produce lower yields and could not feed the world’s population alone. Many farmers combine approaches."],["Nitrogen in the ocean","In the ocean, tiny blue-green algae called cyanobacteria fix nitrogen and help support marine food webs. Scientists continue to discover new nitrogen-fixing organisms in the sea. Changes in ocean temperature and oxygen may affect the ocean nitrogen cycle in the future."]],
sp:["Bread from air","At the start of the 20th century, scientists warned that the world was running out of natural fertilizers such as guano, bird droppings mined from islands off Peru, and nitrate deposits from the deserts of Chile. In 1898 the British scientist William Crookes predicted a coming wheat famine unless chemists found a way to capture nitrogen from the air. Within about a decade, Fritz Haber did just that, using high pressure, high temperature and an iron catalyst to make ammonia. The process was soon described as making bread from air. Today it uses about one to two percent of the world’s energy and supports the food supply of billions of people."],
g:[["Nitrogen fixation","Turning nitrogen gas into usable compounds"],["Legume","A plant such as a bean that hosts nitrogen-fixing bacteria"],["Nitrate","A form of nitrogen plants absorb"],["Fertilizer","A substance that adds nutrients to soil"]],
f:["Nitrogen makes up about 78 percent of the air.","Beans host bacteria that fix nitrogen.","The Haber–Bosch process makes ammonia fertilizer."],
fun:"About half the nitrogen atoms in your body may have passed through a fertilizer factory.",
tl:"Ang nitrogen cycle ay ang paggalaw ng nitrogen sa hangin, lupa, tubig at mga buhay na bagay. Ginagawang magagamit ng mga bakterya ang nitrogen mula sa hangin para sa mga halaman. Ang munggo at iba pang legume ay nagdaragdag ng nitrogen sa lupa."});

;
/* data/space11.js */
// Space: Io, Enceladus, Halley's Comet, Betelgeuse and the Magellanic Clouds.
ADDART({id:"io",t:"Io",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌋",w:"Io (moon)",r:["jupiter","europa","volc","moons","galileo"],
p:[["Volcano world","Io is the most volcanically active place in the solar system."],["Jupiter’s grip","Jupiter’s gravity squeezes Io, heating its interior."],["A colorful moon","Sulfur paints Io’s surface yellow, orange and red."]],
l:"Io is the innermost of Jupiter’s four large moons, discovered by Galileo Galilei in 1610. Slightly larger than Earth’s Moon, it is the most volcanically active body known in the solar system, with hundreds of active volcanoes, some shooting plumes of sulfur and gas hundreds of kilometers into space and others pouring out vast lava flows. Io’s heat comes not from radioactivity but from tidal heating: as it orbits Jupiter, gravitational tugs from Jupiter and from the neighboring moons Europa and Ganymede constantly flex its interior, generating heat by friction. Sulfur compounds color its surface in yellows, oranges, reds, whites and blacks, giving it a pizza-like appearance. Io was explored by the Voyager, Galileo and Juno spacecraft.",
s:[["Tidal heating","Io orbits Jupiter every 1.8 days in a slightly oval path, kept that way by regular tugs from Europa and Ganymede. As its distance from Jupiter changes, the giant planet’s gravity squeezes and stretches Io, raising tides in its solid surface of up to about 100 meters. This constant flexing heats Io’s interior, melting rock."],["Volcanoes and lava","Io’s volcanoes erupt lava hotter than most volcanoes on Earth today. Loki Patera is a huge lava lake about 200 kilometers across that brightens and dims as its crust overturns. Pele’s plume rises about 300 kilometers above the surface, raining sulfur in a giant red ring."],["A thin atmosphere","Io has a very thin atmosphere, made mostly of sulfur dioxide from volcanoes. It freezes onto the surface when Io passes into Jupiter’s shadow. Material escaping from Io forms a doughnut-shaped cloud of charged particles, called the Io plasma torus, around Jupiter."],["Electric connection","As Io moves through Jupiter’s powerful magnetic field, it generates an electric current of millions of amperes flowing between the moon and Jupiter. This current creates a glowing spot in Jupiter’s auroras, sometimes called Io’s footprint."]],
k:[["Planet","Jupiter"],["Discovered","1610, by Galileo"],["Diameter","About 3,640 km"],["Orbital period","About 1.8 days"],["Heat source","Tidal heating"]],
d:[["1610","Galileo discovers Io and three other moons of Jupiter"],["1979","Scientists predict Io’s volcanism just before Voyager 1 arrives"],["1979","Linda Morabito discovers a volcanic plume in Voyager 1 images"],["1995","The Galileo spacecraft begins orbiting Jupiter"],["2007","New Horizons photographs a huge plume on Io while passing Jupiter"],["2023","Juno makes close flybys of Io"]],
s2:[["A prediction just in time","In March 1979, three scientists, Stanton Peale, Patrick Cassen and Ray Reynolds, published a paper predicting that tidal heating would make Io volcanically active. Just days later, on March 9, Voyager 1 navigation engineer Linda Morabito noticed a strange crescent shape at the edge of Io in an image. It was a volcanic plume, the first active volcano seen beyond Earth."],["The Galilean moons","Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto were the first moons discovered around another planet, seen by Galileo in January 1610. Their orbits around Jupiter showed that not everything circled Earth, supporting the Copernican view. Io, Europa and Ganymede are linked in an orbital rhythm: for every orbit of Ganymede, Europa orbits twice and Io four times."],["Juno’s close look","NASA’s Juno spacecraft, orbiting Jupiter since 2016, made close flybys of Io in December 2023 and February 2024, passing about 1,500 kilometers above its surface. Its images revealed mountains, lava lakes and shifting volcanic features. Scientists also found evidence that Io does not have a global ocean of magma beneath its crust, as some had suspected."],["Mountains taller than Everest","Despite its volcanism, Io’s tallest mountains are not volcanoes. They are huge blocks of crust pushed up by stresses, some rising higher than Mount Everest. Boösaule Montes reaches about 17 kilometers high."],["Named for a myth","Io is named after a figure in Greek mythology, a priestess loved by Zeus, whom the Romans called Jupiter. Its volcanoes and features are named after fire, volcano, sun and thunder gods from many cultures, as well as people and places from the myth."],["A window on early Earth","Studying Io helps scientists understand how volcanism and tidal heating work on other worlds, including exoplanets that orbit close to their stars. Some scientists think Io’s eruptions resemble those that may have occurred on early Earth, when its interior was hotter."]],
sp:["The day Voyager found a volcano","On March 9, 1979, four days after Voyager 1 flew past Jupiter, Linda Morabito was studying an image of Io taken to help navigate the spacecraft. She deliberately brightened the image to see a faint star behind the moon. Instead, she saw a huge umbrella-shaped cloud rising from the edge of Io. At first she wondered if it was another moon peeking out from behind. But no moon belonged there. It was a volcanic plume, rising about 300 kilometers into space. For the first time, humans had seen an active volcano on another world, confirming a prediction published just days earlier."],
g:[["Tidal heating","Heat produced by a moon being squeezed by gravity"],["Plume","A column of gas and dust rising from a volcano"],["Plasma torus","A ring of charged particles around Jupiter"],["Galilean moons","Jupiter’s four largest moons"]],
f:["Io has hundreds of active volcanoes.","Io is slightly larger than Earth’s Moon.","Io was discovered by Galileo in 1610."],
fun:"Tides on Io raise its solid rock surface by up to about 100 meters.",
tl:"Ang Io ay buwan ng Jupiter na may pinakamaraming aktibong bulkan sa sistemang solar. Pinaiinit ito ng paghila ng grabidad ng Jupiter at ng ibang buwan. Natuklasan ito ni Galileo noong 1610."});

ADDART({id:"enceladus",t:"Enceladus",c:"Astronomy",i:"❄️",w:"Enceladus",r:["saturn","astrobio","europa","titan","cassini"],
p:[["Icy moon","Enceladus is a small, bright moon of Saturn covered in ice."],["Geysers","Jets of water spray from cracks near its south pole."],["A hidden ocean","Beneath its ice lies a global ocean of salty water."]],
l:"Enceladus is a small moon of Saturn, about 500 kilometers across, discovered by William Herschel in 1789. Covered in fresh ice, it is the most reflective body in the solar system. In 2005 NASA’s Cassini spacecraft discovered jets of water vapor and ice grains erupting from long cracks near its south pole, nicknamed tiger stripes. These plumes come from a global ocean of salty liquid water beneath the icy crust, kept warm by tidal heating from Saturn’s gravity. The plumes contain salts, organic molecules, hydrogen and phosphorus, ingredients that could support life. Because of this, many scientists consider Enceladus one of the most promising places to search for life beyond Earth. Its plumes also feed Saturn’s E ring.",
s:[["The tiger stripes","Near Enceladus’s south pole are four long, parallel cracks, each about 130 kilometers long, warmer than the surrounding ice. More than 100 jets of water vapor and ice grains spray from them, forming a huge plume. Scientists named the stripes after cities in a collection of Middle Eastern tales, such as Baghdad and Cairo."],["The ocean below","By measuring how Enceladus wobbles as it orbits Saturn, Cassini scientists showed in 2015 that its icy shell floats on a global ocean. The ocean is thought to lie beneath ice several kilometers thick near the south pole and thicker elsewhere, and it may be around 10 kilometers deep."],["Ingredients for life","Cassini flew through the plumes and sampled them. It detected salty water, simple and complex organic molecules, and molecular hydrogen, which suggests chemical reactions between water and rock on the seafloor, possibly at hydrothermal vents. In 2023 scientists reported phosphates in ice grains, completing the main chemical elements known to be needed by life."],["Feeding a ring","Ice grains from Enceladus’s plumes escape into orbit around Saturn, forming the wide, faint E ring. Some of the ice also falls back to the surface as snow, keeping Enceladus bright and fresh."]],
k:[["Planet","Saturn"],["Discovered","1789, by William Herschel"],["Diameter","About 500 km"],["Plumes discovered","2005, by Cassini"],["Global ocean confirmed","2015"]],
d:[["1789","William Herschel discovers Enceladus"],["1981","Voyager 2 shows that parts of Enceladus are smooth and young"],["2005","Cassini discovers plumes erupting from the south pole"],["2015","Scientists confirm a global ocean beneath the ice"],["2017","Hydrogen is detected in the plumes"],["2023","Phosphates are found in plume particles"]],
s2:[["Could life exist there?","On Earth, hydrothermal vents on the dark seafloor support communities of microbes that live on chemical energy rather than sunlight. If similar vents exist on Enceladus’s seafloor, they could provide energy for microbes. Scientists stress that no life has been detected, but Enceladus offers a rare chance to sample an alien ocean by flying through its plumes."],["Webb sees a giant plume","In 2023 the James Webb Space Telescope observed a plume of water vapor from Enceladus stretching more than 9,000 kilometers into space, many times wider than the moon itself. The observations showed how Enceladus supplies water to a doughnut-shaped cloud around Saturn."],["Why is it warm?","Enceladus is too small to have kept much heat from its formation. Instead, tidal heating keeps its ocean liquid. Its orbit around Saturn is slightly oval because of a rhythm with the larger moon Dione, so Saturn’s gravity flexes Enceladus as it moves, generating heat, especially at the south pole."],["Future missions","Scientists have proposed missions to orbit Enceladus or land near its tiger stripes to search for signs of life. The European Space Agency has considered an Enceladus mission as a future priority, and NASA scientists have proposed concepts such as an orbiter and lander. Such missions would take many years to plan and fly."],["Named after a giant","Enceladus is named after a giant in Greek mythology who was buried under Mount Etna by the goddess Athena. The name was suggested by John Herschel, William Herschel’s son. Its surface features are named after characters and places from The Thousand and One Nights."],["A moon of contrasts","Some regions of Enceladus are old and heavily cratered, while others are smooth, cracked and almost crater-free, showing they have been recently resurfaced. This variety on such a small body surprised scientists when Voyager 2 first photographed it in 1981."]],
sp:["Cassini’s surprise","When Cassini first flew past Enceladus in early 2005, its magnetometer detected something strange: Saturn’s magnetic field seemed to be bending around the moon, as if Enceladus had an atmosphere. That should have been impossible for such a small moon, whose weak gravity cannot hold onto gas. Mission planners decided to send Cassini much closer. In July 2005 the spacecraft passed just 175 kilometers above the surface and discovered the source: water vapor erupting from warm cracks at the south pole. Over the following years, Cassini flew through the plumes many times, even tasting them. A small, overlooked moon had become one of the most exciting places in the solar system."],
g:[["Plume","A spray of gas and particles"],["Tiger stripes","Warm cracks at Enceladus’s south pole"],["Hydrothermal vent","A hot spring on a seafloor"],["Tidal heating","Heat from a moon being flexed by gravity"]],
f:["Enceladus has a global ocean under its ice.","Its plumes feed Saturn’s E ring.","Enceladus is the most reflective body in the solar system."],
fun:"Enceladus reflects almost all the sunlight that hits it, making it brighter than fresh snow.",
tl:"Ang Enceladus ay maliit na buwan ng Saturn na nababalutan ng yelo. Bumubuga ito ng tubig mula sa mga bitak sa timog na polo nito. Sa ilalim ng yelo ay may karagatan ng maalat na tubig, kaya iniisip ng ilang siyentipiko na maaaring may buhay doon."});

ADDART({id:"halley",t:"Halley’s Comet",c:"Astronomy",i:"☄️",w:"Halley's Comet",r:["comet","kuiper","meteors","newton","rosetta"],
p:[["Once a lifetime","Halley’s Comet returns about every 76 years."],["A famous prediction","Edmond Halley predicted its return using Newton’s laws."],["Next visit","It will next pass near the Sun in 2061."]],
l:"Halley’s Comet is the most famous comet in history and the only short-period comet regularly visible to the naked eye from Earth. It returns to the inner solar system about every 76 years, so most people can see it at most once in their lifetime. Records of its appearances go back more than 2,000 years, including Chinese observations from 240 BC and its depiction in the Bayeux Tapestry in 1066. In 1705 the English astronomer Edmond Halley used Isaac Newton’s laws to show that comets seen in 1531, 1607 and 1682 were the same object and predicted its return in 1758. When it reappeared, it was named after him. Its last appearance was in 1986, when spacecraft flew close to its nucleus. It will return in 2061.",
s:[["A dirty snowball","Halley’s nucleus is a potato-shaped lump of ice, dust and rock about 15 kilometers long. It is one of the darkest objects in the solar system, blacker than coal, because of a crust of dark dust. When it nears the Sun, heat turns ice into gas, releasing dust and forming a glowing coma and long tails."],["An oval orbit","Halley’s Comet follows a long, stretched orbit that carries it from inside the orbit of Venus to beyond the orbit of Neptune. It travels around the Sun in the opposite direction to the planets. It reached the farthest point of its orbit in December 2023 and is now heading back toward the Sun."],["Meteor showers","As Halley’s Comet travels, it leaves a trail of dust along its orbit. When Earth passes through this trail twice a year, the dust burns up in the atmosphere, creating two meteor showers: the Eta Aquariids in May and the Orionids in October. These showers can be seen from the Philippines."],["Changing appearances","Some returns of Halley’s Comet have been spectacular, while others were faint, depending on where Earth was when the comet passed the Sun. In 837 it passed very close to Earth and was extremely bright. The 1986 appearance was one of the poorest for viewers on Earth."]],
k:[["Orbital period","About 76 years"],["Last near the Sun","1986"],["Next near the Sun","2061"],["Nucleus size","About 15 km long"],["Related meteor showers","Eta Aquariids and Orionids"]],
d:[["240 BC","Chinese astronomers record the comet"],["1066","The comet appears before the Norman Conquest of England"],["1705","Edmond Halley predicts the comet’s return"],["1758","The comet returns as predicted"],["1910","Earth passes through the comet’s tail"],["1986","The Giotto spacecraft photographs the comet’s nucleus"]],
s2:[["Halley’s prediction","Edmond Halley, a friend of Isaac Newton, studied records of comets and noticed that those of 1531, 1607 and 1682 had similar orbits. He proposed that they were one comet returning roughly every 76 years and predicted it would reappear in late 1758 or early 1759. Halley died in 1742 and did not live to see it, but on Christmas night 1758, a German farmer and amateur astronomer, Johann Georg Palitzsch, spotted it. The prediction was a triumph for Newton’s theory of gravity."],["An omen in history","For centuries, comets were seen as omens of disaster or change. Halley’s Comet appeared in 1066, shortly before William of Normandy conquered England, and it was embroidered into the Bayeux Tapestry with people pointing at it in wonder. Some historians believe the Italian painter Giotto depicted the comet as the Star of Bethlehem in a fresco painted in the early 1300s."],["Comet pills of 1910","In 1910 astronomers announced that Earth would pass through the comet’s tail, which contained a poisonous gas called cyanogen. Although scientists explained that the gas was far too thin to cause harm, some people panicked. Sellers made money from comet pills and gas masks. Nothing happened, and the comet put on a fine show."],["Spacecraft visit","When Halley returned in 1986, an international fleet of spacecraft went to meet it. The European Space Agency’s Giotto, named after the painter, flew within about 600 kilometers of the nucleus in March 1986 and took the first close-up images of a comet’s nucleus. Soviet Vega probes and Japanese probes also studied it."],["Mark Twain and the comet","The American writer Mark Twain was born in 1835, shortly after the comet appeared. He predicted he would die when it returned, saying he had come in with the comet and expected to go out with it. He died in April 1910, a day after the comet passed closest to the Sun."],["The 2061 return","Halley’s Comet will next pass closest to the Sun in July 2061. Astronomers expect it to be better placed for viewing from Earth than in 1986. Many people who are children today may get the chance to see it, perhaps with their own children or grandchildren."]],
sp:["The farmer who saw it first","As the year 1758 drew to a close, astronomers across Europe searched the skies for the comet Edmond Halley had predicted. Some doubted it would appear. Professional astronomers in France had calculated that the gravity of Jupiter and Saturn would delay its arrival, so they expected it in the spring of 1759. On Christmas night 1758, Johann Georg Palitzsch, a farmer near Dresden in Germany who loved astronomy, pointed his telescope at the sky and found a faint, fuzzy patch. It was the comet. It passed closest to the Sun in March 1759, within a month of the calculations. Halley had been dead for 16 years, but his prediction had come true."],
g:[["Comet","An icy object that forms a glowing tail near the Sun"],["Nucleus","The solid center of a comet"],["Coma","The cloud of gas and dust around a comet’s nucleus"],["Orbital period","The time to complete one orbit"]],
f:["Halley’s Comet returns about every 76 years.","It will return in 2061.","It causes two meteor showers each year."],
fun:"Halley’s nucleus is darker than coal, even though comets are often called dirty snowballs.",
tl:"Ang Kometa ni Halley ay bumabalik tuwing humigit-kumulang 76 na taon. Hinulaan ni Edmond Halley ang pagbabalik nito gamit ang mga batas ni Newton. Muli itong lalapit sa Araw sa taong 2061."});

ADDART({id:"betelgeuse",t:"Betelgeuse",c:"Astronomy",i:"🔴",w:"Betelgeuse",r:["stars","supernova","constellations","pulsar","bh"],
p:[["A red giant","Betelgeuse is a huge red supergiant star in Orion."],["A sudden fade","In 2019 and 2020 it faded dramatically."],["Future supernova","One day it will explode as a supernova."]],
l:"Betelgeuse is a red supergiant star in the constellation Orion, marking the hunter’s shoulder. It is one of the brightest stars in the night sky and one of the largest known: if placed at the center of our solar system, it would extend beyond the orbit of Mars, and perhaps toward Jupiter. Betelgeuse is a few hundred light-years from Earth, with estimates ranging from about 500 to 700 light-years. It is a young but massive star near the end of its life, and it is expected to explode as a supernova sometime within the next 100,000 years or so. Its brightness varies, and in late 2019 and early 2020 it dimmed dramatically, which scientists later traced mainly to a cloud of dust it had ejected. In 2025 astronomers reported evidence of a small companion star.",
s:[["How big is it?","Betelgeuse’s radius is roughly 700 to 1,000 times that of the Sun, though it is hard to measure because the star has no sharp edge and its size changes. Its mass is about 15 to 20 times the Sun’s. It is so large and close that it was the first star after the Sun whose size was measured, in 1920."],["Red and cool","Betelgeuse glows reddish-orange because its surface is cooler than the Sun’s, at around 3,500 degrees Celsius. Although cooler, it is enormously bright, giving off tens of thousands of times as much energy as the Sun because of its huge surface."],["A restless star","Betelgeuse’s surface is covered with giant bubbling cells of hot gas, some larger than Earth’s orbit. The star pulsates, changing in size and brightness over months and years. It also loses large amounts of material into space, surrounding itself with clouds of gas and dust."],["The coming explosion","Betelgeuse is running out of fuel in its core. When fusion stops, its core will collapse and it will explode as a supernova, likely leaving behind a neutron star. Astronomers cannot predict exactly when; it could be tomorrow or tens of thousands of years from now."]],
k:[["Constellation","Orion"],["Type","Red supergiant"],["Distance","About 500–700 light-years"],["Size","Hundreds of times the Sun’s radius"],["Fate","Supernova"]],
d:[["1836","John Herschel notes that Betelgeuse varies in brightness"],["1920","Albert Michelson and Francis Pease measure its size"],["1995","Hubble takes the first direct image of the surface of a star other than the Sun"],["2019","Betelgeuse begins its Great Dimming"],["2021","Astronomers conclude a dust cloud caused most of the dimming"],["2025","Astronomers report evidence of a companion star"]],
s2:[["The Great Dimming","Beginning in October 2019, Betelgeuse faded until, by February 2020, it was less than half its usual brightness, noticeably changing the look of Orion. Some people wondered whether it was about to explode. Studies using the Very Large Telescope in Chile and the Hubble Space Telescope showed that the star had ejected a large bubble of gas, which cooled into dust and blocked part of its light, along with a cool patch on its surface. By mid-2020 it had brightened again."],["What would the supernova look like?","When Betelgeuse explodes, it will become far brighter than any star or planet in the night sky, possibly as bright as a half Moon, and visible during the day for weeks. It would then fade over months. Because it is several hundred light-years away, the explosion would not harm life on Earth."],["A companion star","Astronomers had long suspected that a small star orbits Betelgeuse, causing a slow change in its brightness every six years or so. In 2025 a team using the Gemini North telescope in Hawaii reported detecting a faint companion close to the giant. It is thought to be a young star that may eventually be swallowed by Betelgeuse."],["The name","The name Betelgeuse comes from Arabic, through a long chain of copying and mistranslation. The original Arabic name is usually interpreted as the hand of al-Jawzāʾ, a figure later associated with Orion. Over centuries, medieval European scholars misread the Arabic letters, producing the modern name."],["Orion from the Philippines","Orion is easy to see from the Philippines in the evening sky from about December to March. Betelgeuse is the bright reddish star at the upper left of the constellation as it rises in the east, opposite blue-white Rigel. The three stars of Orion’s Belt are known in Filipino Christian tradition as the Tatlong Maria or Tres Marias."],["Betelgeuse in culture","Betelgeuse has appeared in many stories and myths. Aboriginal Australian peoples, Polynesian navigators and other cultures had their own names and stories for the star. In modern popular culture, it gave its name to the comedy film Beetlejuice, whose title character’s name is pronounced like the star."]],
sp:["Watching a giant fade","In late 2019, amateur and professional astronomers noticed something unusual: Betelgeuse, one of the most familiar stars in the sky, was fading. By February 2020, it had dropped from among the ten brightest stars to about the twenty-fifth. Social media buzzed with speculation that the star might be about to explode as a supernova. Astronomers urgently pointed telescopes at it. Images from the Very Large Telescope revealed that the southern half of the star had become much darker. Later analysis showed that Betelgeuse had burped out a bubble of gas that cooled and formed dust, acting like a veil. The star had not exploded, but the event gave scientists a rare view of how dying giant stars lose material."],
g:[["Red supergiant","A huge, cool, bright star near the end of its life"],["Supernova","The explosion of a star"],["Light-year","The distance light travels in a year"],["Variable star","A star whose brightness changes"]],
f:["Betelgeuse is in Orion.","It dimmed dramatically in 2019 and 2020.","It will one day explode as a supernova."],
fun:"If Betelgeuse replaced the Sun, its surface would reach beyond the orbit of Mars.",
tl:"Ang Betelgeuse ay napakalaking pulang bituin sa konstelasyong Orion. Biglang lumabo ito noong 2019 at 2020 dahil sa ulap ng alikabok. Balang araw, sasabog ito bilang isang supernova."});

ADDART({id:"magellanic",t:"The Magellanic Clouds",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Magellanic Clouds",r:["galaxies","milky","magellan","supernova","andromeda"],
p:[["Neighbor galaxies","The Magellanic Clouds are two small galaxies near the Milky Way."],["Southern skies","They are best seen from the Southern Hemisphere."],["A famous supernova","A supernova exploded in the Large Magellanic Cloud in 1987."]],
l:"The Large and Small Magellanic Clouds are two irregular dwarf galaxies close to the Milky Way, visible to the naked eye from the Southern Hemisphere as faint, glowing patches in the night sky. The Large Magellanic Cloud is about 160,000 light-years away and the Small Magellanic Cloud about 200,000 light-years away. They are among the nearest galaxies to our own. Named in Europe after Ferdinand Magellan, whose expedition described them in the 1500s, they had long been known to peoples of the Southern Hemisphere and to Arab and Persian astronomers. The clouds are rich in gas and young stars, and contain the Tarantula Nebula, one of the most active star-forming regions known. In 1987 a supernova exploded in the Large Magellanic Cloud.",
s:[["Two dwarf galaxies","The Large Magellanic Cloud has a mass of perhaps one-tenth or less that of the Milky Way, and the Small Magellanic Cloud is smaller still. Both have irregular shapes, partly because the Milky Way’s gravity and their interactions with each other have pulled and distorted them. The Large Magellanic Cloud shows signs of a central bar and a single spiral arm."],["The Tarantula Nebula","The Tarantula Nebula in the Large Magellanic Cloud is a huge cloud of glowing gas where new stars are forming. It contains some of the most massive stars known, including R136a1, one of the heaviest stars ever found. If the Tarantula were as close as the Orion Nebula, it would cast shadows on Earth."],["The Magellanic Stream","A long trail of gas called the Magellanic Stream stretches across much of the southern sky, pulled out of the clouds by gravity. A bridge of gas and stars also connects the two clouds, showing that they have interacted with each other."],["Visitors or companions?","Astronomers long thought the Magellanic Clouds were satellite galaxies orbiting the Milky Way. Precise measurements of their motions using the Hubble Space Telescope, published from 2006 onward, suggested they may be moving so fast that they are on their first pass near the Milky Way. Many astronomers expect the Large Magellanic Cloud eventually to collide and merge with the Milky Way, billions of years from now."]],
k:[["Type","Irregular dwarf galaxies"],["Distance of the Large Cloud","About 160,000 light-years"],["Distance of the Small Cloud","About 200,000 light-years"],["Famous nebula","Tarantula Nebula"],["Famous supernova","SN 1987A"]],
d:[["964","Persian astronomer al-Sufi describes the Large Magellanic Cloud"],["1519","Magellan’s expedition records the clouds during its voyage"],["1912","Henrietta Leavitt publishes her discovery of a rule for Cepheid stars in the Small Cloud"],["1987","Supernova 1987A explodes in the Large Magellanic Cloud"],["2006","Hubble measurements suggest the clouds may be on their first pass near the Milky Way"],["2022","The James Webb Space Telescope images the Tarantula Nebula in detail"]],
s2:[["Supernova 1987A","In February 1987, astronomers saw a star explode in the Large Magellanic Cloud. It was the closest supernova seen since 1604 and could be seen with the naked eye from the Southern Hemisphere. Detectors in Japan and the United States caught a burst of neutrinos hours before the light arrived, confirming theories of how massive stars collapse. Astronomers continue to study the expanding remains."],["Leavitt’s measuring stick","At Harvard College Observatory in the early 1900s, Henrietta Swan Leavitt studied photographs of the Small Magellanic Cloud. She found that brighter Cepheid variable stars pulsed more slowly. Because all the stars in the cloud were at roughly the same distance, this revealed a rule linking a Cepheid’s true brightness to its pulsing period. Her discovery gave astronomers a way to measure distances to other galaxies, which Edwin Hubble later used."],["Names and history","In Europe, the clouds were named after Ferdinand Magellan because Antonio Pigafetta, the chronicler of Magellan’s expedition, described them during the voyage of 1519 to 1522, which also reached the Philippines. Southern peoples such as Aboriginal Australians, Māori and Polynesian navigators, and Andean cultures had long had their own names and stories for them. Some astronomers have suggested renaming the clouds, citing the violence of Magellan’s expedition and earlier knowledge of the clouds, while others prefer to keep the long-established name; the question remains under discussion."],["Seeing them","The Magellanic Clouds are easy to see from Australia, New Zealand, southern Africa and South America on dark, moonless nights, far from city lights. From the Philippines they lie very low near the southern horizon, and can be glimpsed only from the southern islands under exceptionally clear, dark skies."],["Neighbors in the Local Group","The Magellanic Clouds are part of the Local Group, a family of galaxies that includes the Milky Way, the Andromeda Galaxy and dozens of small galaxies. The Large Magellanic Cloud is the fourth-largest galaxy in the group. It even has its own small satellite galaxies."],["A laboratory for astronomy","Because all the stars in each cloud are at nearly the same distance from Earth and are not hidden by much dust, the Magellanic Clouds are ideal for studying how stars form, evolve and die. Their gas contains fewer heavy elements than the Milky Way, resembling conditions in the early universe."]],
sp:["The night a star exploded","In the early hours of February 24, 1987, Ian Shelton, a Canadian astronomer working at Las Campanas Observatory in Chile, developed a photograph of the Large Magellanic Cloud. He noticed a bright star that had not been there the night before. He went outside, looked up, and could see it with his own eyes. Around the same time, other observers in Chile and New Zealand spotted it too. It was a supernova, the brightest seen since the time of Johannes Kepler in 1604. Meanwhile, neutrino detectors deep underground in Japan and the United States had already recorded a burst of particles from the dying star. For astronomers, it was the event of a lifetime."],
g:[["Dwarf galaxy","A small galaxy with relatively few stars"],["Cepheid variable","A pulsing star used to measure distances"],["Supernova","The explosion of a star"],["Local Group","The group of galaxies that includes the Milky Way"]],
f:["The Magellanic Clouds are galaxies.","Supernova 1987A exploded in the Large Magellanic Cloud.","They are best seen from the Southern Hemisphere."],
fun:"Light from the Large Magellanic Cloud left it about 160,000 years ago, when early modern humans lived in Africa.",
tl:"Ang Magellanic Clouds ay dalawang maliliit na galaxy na malapit sa Milky Way. Pinakamainam itong makita mula sa katimugang bahagi ng mundo. Noong 1987, may bituing sumabog bilang supernova sa Large Magellanic Cloud."});

;
/* data/space12.js */
// Space: white dwarfs, the Hubble Space Telescope, the Space Shuttle, New Horizons and Cassini.
ADDART({id:"whitedwarfs",t:"White Dwarfs",c:"Astronomy",i:"⚪",w:"White dwarf",r:["stars","sun","supernova","pulsar","nebula"],
p:[["Dead stars","A white dwarf is the hot, glowing core left after a star like the Sun dies."],["Incredibly dense","A teaspoon of white dwarf material would weigh tonnes."],["The Sun’s future","Our Sun will one day become a white dwarf."]],
l:"A white dwarf is the dense remnant of a star that has used up its nuclear fuel. Stars up to about eight times the mass of the Sun, including the Sun itself, end their lives this way. After swelling into a red giant and shedding its outer layers as a glowing cloud called a planetary nebula, the star leaves behind its hot core: a white dwarf about the size of Earth but with roughly the mass of the Sun. Its matter is so tightly packed that a teaspoonful would weigh several tonnes. White dwarfs no longer produce energy by fusion; they simply glow from stored heat and slowly cool over billions of years. Most stars in the Milky Way, including the Sun, will become white dwarfs.",
s:[["How they form","When a Sun-like star runs low on hydrogen in its core, it swells into a red giant, burning helium and other fuels. Eventually it puffs its outer layers into space, forming a planetary nebula. The exposed core, made mostly of carbon and oxygen, shrinks into a white dwarf, initially extremely hot."],["What holds them up","A white dwarf is not supported by heat and fusion like a normal star. Instead, its electrons are packed so tightly that a quantum effect called electron degeneracy pressure resists further squeezing. This pressure supports the star against its own gravity."],["The Chandrasekhar limit","In 1930 the young Indian physicist Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar calculated that a white dwarf cannot be more than about 1.4 times the mass of the Sun. Above this limit, electron pressure fails, and the star collapses. This idea helped explain neutron stars and black holes, and Chandrasekhar shared the 1983 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Cooling over time","A white dwarf starts out at temperatures of over 100,000 degrees Celsius and slowly cools and dims. As it cools, its interior gradually crystallizes into a solid. Over enormous spans of time, it would become a cold, dark black dwarf, but the universe is not yet old enough for any to exist."]],
k:[["Typical size","About the size of Earth"],["Typical mass","About 0.6 times the Sun"],["Maximum mass","About 1.4 times the Sun"],["Main makeup","Carbon and oxygen"],["Nearest famous example","Sirius B"]],
d:[["1844","Friedrich Bessel predicts an unseen companion of Sirius"],["1862","Alvan Graham Clark sees Sirius B through a telescope"],["1914","Astronomers find that a dim star, 40 Eridani B, is very hot"],["1922","Willem Luyten uses the term white dwarf"],["1930","Chandrasekhar calculates the maximum mass of a white dwarf"],["2019","Gaia data show white dwarfs crystallizing as they cool"]],
s2:[["Sirius B","Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky, has a faint companion called Sirius B. In 1844 the German astronomer Friedrich Bessel noticed Sirius wobbling and predicted that an unseen companion was tugging on it. In 1862 the American telescope maker Alvan Graham Clark spotted it. Astronomers were later amazed to find that it was hot but very small, meaning it must be extraordinarily dense."],["Exploding white dwarfs","If a white dwarf in a close binary system steals enough gas from a companion star, or merges with another white dwarf, it can approach the Chandrasekhar limit and explode in a type Ia supernova. These explosions have a fairly consistent brightness, so astronomers use them as standard candles to measure distances. Such measurements led to the discovery in 1998 that the expansion of the universe is speeding up."],["Novae","When a white dwarf pulls hydrogen from a companion, the gas can build up on its surface and ignite in a sudden burst of fusion, called a nova. The star brightens dramatically for days or weeks without being destroyed. Some systems produce repeated novae over decades."],["The Sun’s fate","In about five billion years, the Sun will swell into a red giant, possibly swallowing Mercury, Venus and perhaps Earth. It will then shed its outer layers and shrink into a white dwarf about the size of Earth. Its glowing remnant will fade over trillions of years."],["Planets around dead stars","Astronomers have found white dwarfs with rocky debris falling onto their surfaces, the remains of planets or asteroids torn apart by gravity. In 2020 astronomers discovered a giant planet orbiting close to a white dwarf, showing that some planets can survive their star’s death."],["Cosmic clocks","Because white dwarfs cool at predictable rates, astronomers can estimate the ages of star clusters and parts of the Milky Way by finding the coolest, faintest white dwarfs. This method suggests that the oldest parts of our galaxy are around 10 to 13 billion years old."]],
sp:["Chandrasekhar’s voyage","In 1930 Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, a 19-year-old graduate from Madras in India, sailed to England to study at Cambridge. During the long sea voyage, he worked on calculations about white dwarfs, combining Einstein’s relativity with quantum physics. He discovered that a white dwarf above about 1.4 solar masses could not support itself and would collapse. When he presented his findings a few years later, the famous astronomer Arthur Eddington publicly ridiculed the idea, and many scientists sided with Eddington. Chandrasekhar moved to the United States and continued his research. Decades later his conclusion was fully accepted, and in 1983 he received the Nobel Prize in Physics. NASA’s Chandra X-ray Observatory is named after him."],
g:[["White dwarf","The dense remnant of a Sun-like star"],["Planetary nebula","A glowing shell of gas thrown off by a dying star"],["Chandrasekhar limit","The maximum mass of a white dwarf"],["Black dwarf","A white dwarf that has cooled completely"]],
f:["The Sun will become a white dwarf.","White dwarfs are about the size of Earth.","Sirius has a white dwarf companion."],
fun:"A teaspoon of white dwarf material would weigh about as much as an elephant or more.",
tl:"Ang white dwarf ay ang maliit at siksik na labi ng bituing tulad ng Araw. Kasinlaki ito ng Daigdig ngunit halos kasimbigat ng Araw. Balang araw, magiging white dwarf din ang ating Araw."});

ADDART({id:"hubblest",t:"The Hubble Space Telescope",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔭",w:"Hubble Space Telescope",r:["scope","jwst","hubble","galaxies","spaceshuttle"],
p:[["Above the clouds","Hubble orbits above Earth’s atmosphere for clear views."],["A flawed start","Its mirror had a flaw that astronauts later fixed."],["Changing astronomy","Hubble has transformed our view of the universe."]],
l:"The Hubble Space Telescope is a large telescope orbiting Earth, launched by the Space Shuttle Discovery in April 1990. A joint project of NASA and the European Space Agency, it is named after the astronomer Edwin Hubble. Because it orbits about 540 kilometers above the surface, beyond the blurring effects of Earth’s atmosphere, Hubble can take extraordinarily sharp images in visible, ultraviolet and near-infrared light. Soon after launch, scientists found that its main mirror had been ground to the wrong shape, but astronauts installed corrective optics in 1993. Over five servicing missions, astronauts repaired and upgraded it. Hubble has helped measure the age of the universe, discover that its expansion is accelerating, and photograph galaxies billions of light-years away.",
s:[["How Hubble works","Hubble’s 2.4-meter main mirror collects light and reflects it to a smaller secondary mirror, which sends it to scientific instruments, including cameras and spectrographs. It orbits Earth about every 95 minutes. Gyroscopes and other systems keep it pointed precisely at targets for hours at a time."],["The flawed mirror","After launch, Hubble’s first images were disappointingly blurry. Investigators found that the main mirror was too flat at the edges by about 2 micrometers, a fraction of the width of a human hair, because of an error in a testing device. The mistake was embarrassing for NASA, and critics called the telescope a costly failure."],["The rescue","In December 1993, astronauts aboard the Space Shuttle Endeavour carried out a series of spacewalks to install corrective optics, like glasses for the telescope, and a new camera. The repair was a success, and Hubble’s images became sharp. Later missions replaced instruments, gyroscopes, batteries and solar panels."],["Servicing missions","Five shuttle missions serviced Hubble between 1993 and 2009. After the Columbia disaster in 2003, NASA at first canceled the final servicing mission because of safety concerns, but public and scientific support led it to be restored. The last mission, in 2009, extended Hubble’s life for many years."]],
k:[["Launched","April 24, 1990"],["Main mirror","2.4 meters"],["Orbit height","About 540 km"],["Partners","NASA and the European Space Agency"],["Named after","Edwin Hubble"]],
d:[["1946","Lyman Spitzer proposes a large telescope in space"],["1990","Hubble is launched aboard Space Shuttle Discovery"],["1993","Astronauts fix Hubble’s blurry vision"],["1995","Hubble takes the Hubble Deep Field and Pillars of Creation images"],["2009","The final servicing mission upgrades Hubble"],["2024","Hubble switches to operating with one gyroscope to extend its life"]],
s2:[["Deep fields","In 1995 astronomers pointed Hubble at a tiny, seemingly empty patch of sky for ten days. The resulting Hubble Deep Field revealed thousands of galaxies, some seen as they were billions of years ago. Later deep fields looked even farther, showing that the universe contains vastly more galaxies than once thought."],["Famous images","Hubble’s photograph of the Pillars of Creation, towering columns of gas and dust in the Eagle Nebula where stars are forming, became one of the most famous images in science. Its pictures of colorful nebulae, colliding galaxies and planets have appeared in textbooks, posters and stamps around the world, inspiring public interest in astronomy."],["Scientific discoveries","Hubble helped pin down the age of the universe at about 13.8 billion years and measure its expansion rate. Together with ground telescopes, it provided evidence that the expansion is accelerating, driven by dark energy. It showed that supermassive black holes lie at the centers of most large galaxies and observed comet Shoemaker-Levy 9 crashing into Jupiter in 1994."],["Studying other worlds","Hubble was used to make some of the first measurements of the atmospheres of planets orbiting other stars. It has watched seasons change on Mars, storms on Jupiter and Saturn, and plumes of water possibly erupting from Jupiter’s moon Europa. It also discovered several small moons of Pluto."],["Hubble and Webb","The James Webb Space Telescope, launched in 2021, observes mainly in infrared light and has a much larger mirror. Rather than replacing Hubble, it works alongside it: Hubble sees visible and ultraviolet light, while Webb sees farther and through dust. Astronomers often combine data from both."],["The end of Hubble","Without servicing, Hubble’s orbit is slowly decaying because of drag from the thin upper atmosphere. NASA expects the telescope to continue working into the 2030s. Options for its eventual fate include a controlled reentry into the ocean or a boost to a higher orbit, and some companies have proposed private missions to service it."]],
sp:["Ten days staring at nothing","In 1995 Robert Williams, director of the institute that runs Hubble, made a daring decision. He used his own discretionary observing time to point the telescope at a tiny patch of sky near the Big Dipper that seemed to contain nothing at all, about the size of a grain of sand held at arm’s length. Some colleagues worried it would be a waste of precious time. For ten days, Hubble collected faint light from that spot. When the images were combined, they revealed around 3,000 galaxies of all shapes, colors and ages, some among the most distant ever seen. The Hubble Deep Field showed that every small patch of sky is crowded with galaxies, and changed how people imagined the size of the universe."],
g:[["Space telescope","A telescope placed in orbit"],["Servicing mission","A mission to repair or upgrade a spacecraft"],["Deep field","A long exposure of a small area of sky"],["Spectrograph","An instrument that splits light into colors"]],
f:["Hubble was launched in 1990.","Astronauts fixed its flawed mirror in 1993.","Hubble orbits Earth about every 95 minutes."],
fun:"Hubble’s mirror flaw was smaller than a fiftieth of the width of a human hair.",
tl:"Ang Hubble Space Telescope ay teleskopyong umiikot sa Daigdig mula pa noong 1990. Malinaw ang mga larawan nito dahil nasa labas ito ng atmospera. Ipinakita ng Hubble na napakaraming galaxy sa sansinukob."});

ADDART({id:"spaceshuttle",t:"The Space Shuttle",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"Space Shuttle",r:["rocket","iss","astronauts","space","hubblest"],
p:[["A reusable spaceship","The Space Shuttle was the first reusable crewed spacecraft."],["Thirty years of flights","It flew from 1981 to 2011."],["Two tragedies","Challenger and Columbia were lost with their crews."]],
l:"The Space Shuttle was a partly reusable spacecraft operated by NASA from 1981 to 2011. It launched like a rocket, orbited Earth like a spacecraft and landed like a glider on a runway. The system included a winged orbiter carrying astronauts and cargo, a large external fuel tank and two solid rocket boosters. Five orbiters flew in space: Columbia, Challenger, Discovery, Atlantis and Endeavour. Over 135 missions, the shuttles carried hundreds of astronauts from many countries, launched satellites and space probes, deployed and repaired the Hubble Space Telescope, and built much of the International Space Station. Two orbiters were destroyed in accidents, Challenger in 1986 and Columbia in 2003, killing 14 astronauts. The program ended in 2011.",
s:[["How it worked","At launch, the orbiter’s three main engines burned fuel from the external tank, while the two solid rocket boosters provided most of the thrust. The boosters separated about two minutes after launch and parachuted into the ocean to be recovered and reused. The external tank was released just before reaching orbit and burned up in the atmosphere."],["In orbit","The orbiter could carry up to seven astronauts and a large payload in its cargo bay, whose doors opened in space. A robotic arm, built by Canada, moved satellites and astronauts. Missions usually lasted one to two weeks."],["Coming home","To return, the orbiter fired its engines to slow down and reentered the atmosphere, protected by thousands of heat-resistant tiles and panels that withstood temperatures over 1,500 degrees Celsius. It then glided without engines to a landing on a long runway, usually in Florida or California."],["Promises and reality","The shuttle was planned to make spaceflight cheap and routine, with frequent flights. In practice, it required extensive refurbishment between missions, flew far less often than planned and was very expensive. Supporters point to its unique abilities to carry large cargo and crews and to repair satellites, while critics argue that it was too costly and risky."]],
k:[["Operator","NASA"],["Years flown","1981–2011"],["Missions","135"],["Orbiters flown in space","Columbia, Challenger, Discovery, Atlantis, Endeavour"],["Maximum crew","Usually up to 7"]],
d:[["1972","President Richard Nixon approves the Space Shuttle program"],["1981","Columbia makes the first shuttle flight"],["1986","Challenger breaks apart after launch"],["1990","Discovery launches the Hubble Space Telescope"],["2003","Columbia breaks apart during reentry"],["2011","Atlantis makes the final shuttle flight"]],
s2:[["The Challenger disaster","On January 28, 1986, the Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart 73 seconds after launch, killing all seven crew members, including Christa McAuliffe, a schoolteacher chosen to be the first teacher in space. An investigation found that a rubber seal, called an O-ring, in a booster had failed in cold weather. It also criticized NASA’s decision-making, as engineers had warned of the danger."],["The Columbia disaster","On February 1, 2003, Columbia broke apart while returning to Earth, killing its seven astronauts, including Ilan Ramon, Israel’s first astronaut, and Kalpana Chawla, the first woman of Indian origin in space. A piece of foam had broken off the external tank during launch and damaged the wing’s heat shield. The disaster led NASA to decide to retire the shuttle."],["Building the space station","Shuttles carried many of the large modules, trusses and solar panels that make up the International Space Station, and astronauts assembled them on spacewalks. They also carried crews and supplies to the station. After the shuttle’s retirement, the United States relied on Russian Soyuz spacecraft until private American spacecraft began carrying astronauts in 2020."],["Firsts and pioneers","Shuttle flights carried many pioneering astronauts. Sally Ride became the first American woman in space in 1983, and Guion Bluford the first African American in space the same year. Astronauts from Europe, Japan, Canada, Saudi Arabia, Mexico and other countries flew aboard the shuttle. Mae Jemison became the first African American woman in space in 1992."],["Where the shuttles are now","After retirement, the remaining orbiters were placed in museums. Discovery is displayed near Washington, D.C., Atlantis at the Kennedy Space Center in Florida, and Endeavour at the California Science Center in Los Angeles. The test orbiter Enterprise, which never flew in space, is in New York."],["The Soviet shuttle","The Soviet Union built its own shuttle, Buran, which looked similar to the American orbiter. In 1988 it made a single uncrewed flight, orbiting Earth twice and landing automatically. The program was canceled after the collapse of the Soviet Union, and Buran was destroyed in 2002 when a hangar roof collapsed."]],
sp:["Columbia’s first flight","On April 12, 1981, exactly 20 years after Yuri Gagarin became the first human in space, the Space Shuttle Columbia stood on its launch pad in Florida with two astronauts aboard, John Young and Robert Crippen. It was the first time NASA had launched a new spacecraft with a crew on its very first flight, without an uncrewed test. With a roar, the shuttle rose into the sky. After two days in orbit, Columbia reentered the atmosphere and glided to a smooth landing on a dry lakebed in California, watched by huge crowds. A new era of reusable spaceflight had begun, one that would last 30 years."],
g:[["Orbiter","The winged part of the shuttle that carried crew"],["Solid rocket booster","A rocket filled with solid fuel"],["Payload bay","The cargo area of the shuttle"],["Reentry","Returning into Earth’s atmosphere"]],
f:["The shuttle landed like a glider.","Shuttles flew from 1981 to 2011.","Shuttles helped build the International Space Station."],
fun:"The shuttle’s first launch was on the 20th anniversary of the first human spaceflight.",
tl:"Ang Space Shuttle ay sasakyang pangkalawakan ng NASA na maaaring gamitin muli, na lumipad mula 1981 hanggang 2011. Lumilipad ito na parang rocket at lumalapag na parang eroplano. Nawala ang Challenger at Columbia kasama ang kanilang mga tauhan."});

ADDART({id:"newhorizons",t:"New Horizons",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"New Horizons",r:["pluto","kuiper","dwarfplanets","voyager","solar"],
p:[["To Pluto","New Horizons was the first spacecraft to visit Pluto."],["A heart on Pluto","It revealed a giant heart-shaped plain of ice."],["Farther still","It later flew past Arrokoth, the most distant object ever explored."]],
l:"New Horizons is a NASA space probe that made the first close-up exploration of Pluto and its moons in July 2015. Launched in January 2006, it was the fastest spacecraft ever launched from Earth, and it took nine and a half years to reach Pluto. Its images transformed Pluto from a blurry dot into a complex world with mountains of water ice, glaciers of frozen nitrogen, a hazy blue atmosphere and a vast heart-shaped plain. In January 2019 the spacecraft flew past Arrokoth, a small, snowman-shaped object in the Kuiper Belt, the most distant object ever explored up close. New Horizons continues to travel outward through the Kuiper Belt, studying the outer solar system.",
s:[["The journey","New Horizons launched on January 19, 2006, aboard an Atlas V rocket. In 2007 it flew past Jupiter, using the giant planet’s gravity to speed up and shorten its journey. It spent much of its trip in hibernation to save energy, waking periodically for checkups."],["Pluto revealed","On July 14, 2015, New Horizons passed about 12,500 kilometers above Pluto’s surface, taking detailed images and measurements. It found a young, active world with ice mountains several kilometers high, flowing nitrogen glaciers and layers of haze. Data from the flyby took more than a year to send back to Earth."],["The heart of Pluto","The most famous feature it revealed is a bright, heart-shaped region named Tombaugh Regio, after Pluto’s discoverer. Its left lobe, Sputnik Planitia, is a huge basin filled with nitrogen ice that slowly churns in large cells, renewing the surface. The heart made Pluto a favorite with the public."],["Pluto’s moons","New Horizons studied Pluto’s largest moon, Charon, which is half Pluto’s size and has a dark red polar region colored by material escaping from Pluto. It also photographed the small moons Nix and Hydra. Pluto and Charon are sometimes described as a double world."]],
k:[["Launched","January 19, 2006"],["Pluto flyby","July 14, 2015"],["Arrokoth flyby","January 1, 2019"],["Operator","NASA"],["Power source","A nuclear battery"]],
d:[["1930","Clyde Tombaugh discovers Pluto"],["2006","New Horizons is launched"],["2006","Pluto is reclassified as a dwarf planet"],["2007","New Horizons swings past Jupiter"],["2015","New Horizons flies past Pluto"],["2019","New Horizons flies past Arrokoth"]],
s2:[["A dwarf planet after launch","Just months after New Horizons launched, the International Astronomical Union reclassified Pluto as a dwarf planet in August 2006. Some scientists on the mission, including its leader Alan Stern, have argued strongly that Pluto should still be called a planet, while many other astronomers support the reclassification. The debate continues."],["Tombaugh’s ashes","New Horizons carries a small portion of the ashes of Clyde Tombaugh, the American astronomer who discovered Pluto in 1930 and died in 1997. It also carries US state quarter coins and a piece of SpaceShipOne, the first private crewed spacecraft. Tombaugh’s ashes will continue traveling out of the solar system."],["Arrokoth","On January 1, 2019, New Horizons flew past Arrokoth, a Kuiper Belt object about 36 kilometers long, made of two flattened lobes joined together. It is thought to be a pristine building block left over from the formation of the solar system. Its name comes from the Powhatan and Algonquian language and means sky, chosen with the consent of Powhatan elders."],["Nuclear power","Sunlight at Pluto is far too weak for solar panels, so New Horizons uses a radioisotope thermoelectric generator, which turns heat from decaying plutonium into electricity. Some groups protested the launch of nuclear material, while NASA argued the risks were very small. The same type of power has been used by the Voyager and Cassini probes."],["Slow messages","At Pluto’s distance, radio signals traveling at the speed of light took about four and a half hours to reach Earth. Because New Horizons’ transmitter was weak and the distance so great, data trickled back slowly, and the full set of Pluto flyby data took about 16 months to download."],["Still flying","New Horizons continues outward through the Kuiper Belt, measuring dust, charged particles and the faint glow of the universe. Scientists hope it might find another object to fly past. It is expected to eventually leave the solar system’s bubble of solar wind, following the path of the Voyager probes."]],
sp:["The phone-home moment","As New Horizons raced past Pluto on July 14, 2015, it was too busy collecting data to communicate with Earth. Mission controllers in Maryland had to wait about 13 hours, not knowing whether the spacecraft had survived passing through the Pluto system, where an unseen piece of debris could have destroyed it. That evening, a signal finally arrived, carrying the message that the spacecraft was healthy and had stored its data. The control room erupted in cheers. Over the following months, images revealing Pluto’s mountains, glaciers and heart streamed back, and a world that had been a mystery for 85 years came into view."],
g:[["Space probe","An uncrewed spacecraft sent to explore"],["Kuiper Belt","A region of icy objects beyond Neptune"],["Flyby","Passing close to an object without stopping"],["Dwarf planet","A small planet-like body"]],
f:["New Horizons flew past Pluto in 2015.","It found a heart-shaped region on Pluto.","It explored Arrokoth in 2019."],
fun:"New Horizons carries some of the ashes of the man who discovered Pluto.",
tl:"Ang New Horizons ang unang sasakyang pangkalawakan na bumisita sa Pluto noong 2015. Natuklasan nito ang malaking kapatagan ng yelo na hugis-puso. Noong 2019, dinaanan nito ang Arrokoth, ang pinakamalayong bagay na nasuri nang malapitan."});

ADDART({id:"cassini",t:"The Cassini–Huygens Mission",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🪐",w:"Cassini–Huygens",r:["saturn","titan","enceladus","voyager","jupiter"],
p:[["Saturn explorer","Cassini orbited Saturn for 13 years."],["Landing on Titan","Its Huygens probe landed on Saturn’s moon Titan in 2005."],["A grand finale","Cassini ended its mission by diving into Saturn."]],
l:"The Cassini–Huygens mission was a joint project of NASA, the European Space Agency and the Italian Space Agency to study Saturn, its rings and its moons. Launched in October 1997, the spacecraft arrived at Saturn in 2004 and orbited the planet for 13 years. It carried the Huygens probe, built by the European Space Agency, which parachuted onto Saturn’s largest moon, Titan, in January 2005, the most distant landing ever made on another world. Cassini discovered water jets on the moon Enceladus, lakes of liquid methane on Titan, new moons and details of Saturn’s rings and storms. In 2017, running low on fuel, Cassini made a series of daring dives between Saturn and its rings before plunging into the planet’s atmosphere.",
s:[["A long journey","Cassini was one of the largest and most complex interplanetary spacecraft ever built, about the size of a small bus. It took nearly seven years to reach Saturn, swinging past Venus twice, Earth and Jupiter to gain speed. It arrived on July 1, 2004, firing its engine to slip into orbit through a gap in the rings."],["Huygens on Titan","On January 14, 2005, Huygens descended through Titan’s thick, orange haze for about two and a half hours, photographing river channels and hills, and landed on a damp plain scattered with rounded pebbles of ice. It sent data for over an hour from the surface. Its images were the first ever taken from the surface of a world in the outer solar system."],["Discoveries at Enceladus","In 2005 Cassini discovered plumes of water vapor and ice erupting from the south pole of the small moon Enceladus. Later flybys through the plumes revealed salts, organic molecules and evidence of a global ocean, making Enceladus a prime target in the search for life."],["Saturn’s secrets","Cassini studied a giant six-sided jet stream at Saturn’s north pole, called the hexagon, and watched a huge storm encircle the planet in 2010 and 2011. It revealed waves, clumps and small moonlets within the rings and helped measure the length of Saturn’s day."]],
k:[["Partners","NASA, ESA, Italian Space Agency"],["Launched","October 15, 1997"],["Arrived at Saturn","July 1, 2004"],["Huygens landing","January 14, 2005"],["Mission end","September 15, 2017"]],
d:[["1655","Christiaan Huygens discovers Titan"],["1675","Giovanni Cassini discovers a gap in Saturn’s rings"],["1997","Cassini–Huygens is launched"],["2004","Cassini arrives at Saturn"],["2005","Huygens lands on Titan"],["2017","Cassini ends its mission by plunging into Saturn"]],
s2:[["Named for astronomers","The orbiter is named after Giovanni Domenico Cassini, an Italian-French astronomer who discovered four of Saturn’s moons and the large gap in its rings now called the Cassini Division. The probe is named after the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens, who discovered Titan in 1655 and first explained that Saturn is surrounded by a ring."],["Lakes and seas on Titan","Cassini’s radar peered through Titan’s haze and found lakes and seas of liquid methane and ethane near its poles, some larger than the Great Lakes of North America. Titan is the only other world known to have stable liquids on its surface, with a cycle of methane rain, rivers and evaporation resembling Earth’s water cycle."],["The Grand Finale","Running low on fuel in 2017, Cassini began its Grand Finale: 22 dives through the narrow gap between Saturn and its innermost rings, a region never explored before. It measured the mass of the rings and sampled particles raining into Saturn. On September 15, 2017, it plunged into Saturn’s atmosphere, sending data until the end. This was done deliberately to avoid any chance of Cassini crashing into and contaminating Enceladus or Titan, which might harbor life."],["The day the Earth smiled","On July 19, 2013, Cassini turned its cameras toward the Sun while Saturn blocked its glare and photographed Earth as a tiny dot beneath Saturn’s rings, nearly 1.5 billion kilometers away. NASA invited people around the world to look up and wave at the same moment, an event called The Day the Earth Smiled."],["Launch controversy","Before its launch in 1997, some groups protested because Cassini carried plutonium for its nuclear power generators, fearing an accident could spread radioactive material. NASA and supporters said the risks were extremely low and that solar power was impractical so far from the Sun. The launch and an Earth flyby in 1999 went safely."],["Legacy","Cassini’s findings are still being analyzed and have reshaped scientists’ understanding of Saturn and its moons. They inspired NASA’s Dragonfly mission, a drone-like rotorcraft planned to explore Titan in the 2030s, and proposals for future missions to Enceladus."]],
sp:["Diving into Saturn","On the morning of September 15, 2017, scientists and engineers gathered at NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California for Cassini’s final moments. The spacecraft, after 20 years in space and 13 at Saturn, was falling toward the giant planet at more than 100,000 kilometers per hour. As it entered the atmosphere, it kept its antenna pointed at Earth, sending data about Saturn’s gases until its thrusters could no longer hold it steady. Because radio signals took about 83 minutes to reach Earth, by the time the signal disappeared, Cassini had already burned up. In the control room, people embraced, some in tears, as one of the most successful planetary missions in history came to a close."],
g:[["Orbiter","A spacecraft that circles a planet"],["Probe","A spacecraft sent to land or descend"],["Cassini Division","A large gap in Saturn’s rings"],["Grand Finale","Cassini’s last series of dives"]],
f:["Huygens landed on Titan in 2005.","Cassini orbited Saturn for 13 years.","Cassini ended its mission by diving into Saturn."],
fun:"Cassini photographed Earth from Saturn as a tiny pale dot beneath the rings.",
tl:"Ang Cassini–Huygens ay misyon upang pag-aralan ang Saturn, ang mga singsing at buwan nito. Lumapag ang Huygens sa buwang Titan noong 2005. Tinapos ng Cassini ang misyon nito sa pagbagsak sa Saturn noong 2017."});

;
/* data/animals15.js */
// Animal Kingdom: seals, lemurs, hedgehogs, bison and the platypus.
ADDART({id:"seals",t:"Seals, Sea Lions and Walruses",c:"Mammals",i:"🦭",w:"Pinniped",r:["whale","otters","penguin","arctic","antarc"],
p:[["Flipper-footed","Seals, sea lions and walruses are called pinnipeds, meaning fin-footed."],["Deep divers","Elephant seals can dive more than 2,000 meters."],["Back from the brink","Some seal species recovered after near extinction."]],
l:"Pinnipeds are a group of marine mammals that includes true seals, eared seals such as sea lions and fur seals, and the walrus. Their name means fin-footed, because their legs have evolved into flippers. There are about 33 living species, found mostly in cold and temperate waters, from the Arctic and Antarctic to the coasts of California, South Africa and New Zealand. Pinnipeds spend most of their lives in the sea but come ashore or onto ice to rest, breed and give birth. They are excellent swimmers and divers, insulated by thick layers of blubber. Many species were hunted heavily for their fur, oil and ivory, and some were nearly wiped out, though protection has allowed several to recover.",
s:[["True seals","True seals, such as harbor seals and elephant seals, have no external ear flaps. On land they move clumsily on their bellies, but in water they swim powerfully by sweeping their hind flippers side to side. Most live in cold waters, though monk seals live in warm seas."],["Eared seals","Sea lions and fur seals have small external ears and can turn their hind flippers forward to walk on land. They swim mainly with their large front flippers. Sea lions are often trained in aquariums because they are intelligent and playful."],["The walrus","The walrus lives in the Arctic and is the only living species of its family. Both males and females have long tusks, which they use to haul themselves onto ice, defend themselves and display. Walruses feed mainly on clams, which they find by sensing the seafloor with their stiff whiskers and suck out of their shells."],["Diving champions","Pinnipeds slow their heart rates and send blood mainly to the brain and heart when they dive. Elephant seals can dive more than 2,000 meters deep and stay underwater for over an hour, sometimes nearly two. Weddell seals of Antarctica hunt under thick sea ice, keeping breathing holes open with their teeth."]],
k:[["Group","Pinnipeds"],["Living species","About 33"],["Main families","True seals, eared seals, walruses"],["Deepest diver","Elephant seals"],["Only freshwater seal","Baikal seal"]],
d:[["c. 23 million years ago","Early pinniped relatives live in the Arctic"],["1911","An international treaty limits the hunting of northern fur seals"],["1972","The United States passes the Marine Mammal Protection Act"],["1983","Europe bans imports of baby seal pelts"],["2008","The Caribbean monk seal is declared extinct"],["2009","The European Union bans most trade in seal products"]],
s2:[["Hunting and controversy","For centuries, seals were hunted for fur, meat, oil and skins. Commercial seal hunting, especially of young harp seals in Canada, has been one of the most heated debates in animal protection. Animal welfare groups argue that the hunt is cruel and unnecessary, while many Canadian Inuit and coastal communities defend it as a traditional, sustainable source of food and income. The European Union banned most seal products in 2009 but made exceptions for Inuit hunts."],["Elephant seals’ comeback","Northern elephant seals were hunted so intensively for their oil in the 1800s that by the 1890s only a tiny group survived on an island off Mexico. After protection by Mexico and the United States, their numbers grew to well over 100,000. Because they descend from so few survivors, they have very little genetic variety."],["Seals and climate change","Many Arctic seals depend on sea ice to rest, give birth and raise their pups. As warming shrinks sea ice, ringed seals, harp seals and walruses face greater challenges. Walruses have increasingly crowded onto beaches in huge groups when ice is unavailable, where stampedes can kill young animals."],["Monk seals","Monk seals live in warm waters. The Caribbean monk seal, last seen in the 1950s, was declared extinct in 2008, the first seal known to have been lost to human causes. The Hawaiian and Mediterranean monk seals are endangered and are protected through beach monitoring and fishing rules."],["Life on land and sea","Most pinnipeds return to the same beaches or ice floes each year to breed. Male elephant seals and sea lions fight for territories and groups of females. Pups are often fed rich, fatty milk and grow quickly; hooded seal pups are weaned in only about four days, one of the shortest nursing periods of any mammal."],["Seals near Asia","No seals live naturally in the Philippines, but several species live elsewhere in Asian waters, such as spotted seals in the Yellow Sea and the Sea of Japan, and the Baikal seal of Lake Baikal in Siberia, the only seal that lives only in fresh water."]],
sp:["The walrus that sank boats","In the summer of 2022, a young female walrus nicknamed Freya wandered far from the Arctic, appearing in the harbors of Norway, Denmark, the Netherlands and Britain. In Oslo, she climbed onto small boats to rest, sometimes sinking them under her weight of several hundred kilograms. Crowds gathered to see her, and some came dangerously close. Norwegian authorities warned people to keep their distance, but eventually decided she posed a risk to public safety and put her down. The decision caused outrage around the world and debate about how to handle wild animals that wander into cities."],
g:[["Pinniped","A seal, sea lion or walrus"],["Blubber","A thick layer of fat under the skin"],["Haul out","When a seal comes onto land or ice"],["Flipper","A flat limb used for swimming"]],
f:["Elephant seals can dive over 2,000 meters.","Walruses use their tusks to climb onto ice.","The Baikal seal lives in fresh water."],
fun:"Hooded seal pups are fed by their mothers for only about four days.",
tl:"Ang mga seal, sea lion at walrus ay mga mamalyang dagat na tinatawag na pinniped. Mahuhusay silang lumangoy at sumisid. May mga uri na halos maubos dahil sa pangangaso ngunit muling dumami dahil sa proteksiyon."});

ADDART({id:"lemurs",t:"Lemurs",c:"Mammals",i:"🐒",w:"Lemur",r:["madag","monkeys","tarsier","gorilla","conservation"],
p:[["Only in Madagascar","Lemurs live naturally only on the island of Madagascar."],["Female leaders","In many lemur groups, females are in charge."],["Highly threatened","Lemurs are among the most endangered mammals on Earth."]],
l:"Lemurs are primates found naturally only on Madagascar and a few nearby islands off the coast of Africa. They belong to the same order as monkeys, apes and humans, but to a separate branch that also includes lorises. There are around 100 species, ranging from mouse lemurs weighing about 30 grams, the smallest primates in the world, to the indri, which can weigh over 7 kilograms. Lemurs evolved in isolation for tens of millions of years after their ancestors reached Madagascar, producing a remarkable variety of forms and behaviors. Many lemurs live in groups led by females. Because of forest loss, hunting and the pet trade, the vast majority of lemur species are threatened with extinction, making them a top conservation priority.",
s:[["How they reached Madagascar","Scientists think the ancestors of lemurs reached Madagascar from mainland Africa perhaps 50 to 60 million years ago, possibly by drifting on rafts of floating vegetation. With few competitors, they spread into many habitats and evolved into dozens of different species."],["Ring-tailed lemurs","Ring-tailed lemurs, with their black-and-white striped tails, are the best-known lemurs. They live in groups and spend more time on the ground than most lemurs. They sunbathe in the morning, sitting upright with arms spread, and males hold stink fights, waving tails coated in scent at rivals."],["The aye-aye","The aye-aye is one of the strangest primates. It taps on tree bark with a long, thin middle finger, listening for hollow tunnels made by insect larvae, then gnaws a hole and fishes them out. Some people in Madagascar traditionally considered it a bad omen, and it was sometimes killed on sight."],["The indri","The indri is the largest living lemur and has only a stubby tail. Groups of indri sing loud, haunting songs that can be heard several kilometers through the forest, marking their territories. Indri do not survive in captivity, so their survival depends entirely on protecting their forests."]],
k:[["Found in","Madagascar"],["Species","About 100"],["Smallest","Madame Berthe’s mouse lemur"],["Largest living","Indri"],["Main threat","Forest loss"]],
d:[["c. 55 million years ago","Ancestors of lemurs may reach Madagascar"],["c. 2,000 years ago","Humans settle Madagascar"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the genus Lemur"],["1966","The Duke Lemur Center begins in the United States"],["2000","Madame Berthe’s mouse lemur is described"],["2020","Experts report that most lemur species are threatened"]],
s2:[["Giant lemurs of the past","Until about 1,000 to 2,000 years ago, Madagascar was home to giant lemurs, some as large as gorillas. Archaeoindris may have weighed around 160 kilograms. These giants disappeared after humans arrived, probably because of hunting and changes to the landscape, though climate change may also have played a role."],["Females in charge","In many lemur species, females are dominant over males. Females often get first choice of food and can chase males away. Scientists think this may help females meet the high energy demands of pregnancy and nursing in Madagascar’s unpredictable environment, where food can be scarce."],["Why they are endangered","More than 90 percent of Madagascar’s original forests have been lost or degraded, largely because of slash-and-burn farming, logging and charcoal production driven by poverty. Lemurs are also hunted for food and captured as pets. Conservationists stress that protecting lemurs must also improve the lives of local people."],["Conservation efforts","Protected areas such as Ranomafana and Andasibe-Mantadia national parks shelter many lemur species and attract ecotourists, providing jobs as guides and in lodges. Community forest management, tree planting and breeding programs in zoos also help. However, political instability and limited funding have made conservation difficult."],["Lemurs and tarsiers","Lemurs belong to the group of primates called strepsirrhines, along with lorises and galagos, which have moist noses like dogs. The Philippine tarsier, though small and big-eyed like many lemurs, belongs to the other group, haplorhines, which also includes monkeys, apes and humans."],["Name meaning","The name lemur comes from the Latin lemures, meaning ghosts or spirits of the dead. Carl Linnaeus chose it, possibly because of the animals’ nocturnal habits, glowing eyes and quiet movements in the night forest."]],
sp:["The singing forest","Before dawn in the rainforest of Andasibe in eastern Madagascar, a strange sound begins: a long, wailing cry that rises and falls like a siren or a whale song. It is a family of indri, the largest lemurs, singing together from the treetops. Other indri groups reply from across the valley. The songs help families stake out their territories and stay in contact. In Malagasy legend, the indri is related to humans, and in some communities it is taboo, or fady, to harm one. Visitors who come to hear the indri also bring income to local guides, giving people another reason to protect the forest."],
g:[["Lemur","A primate found only in Madagascar"],["Primate","The group of mammals that includes lemurs, monkeys, apes and humans"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"],["Fady","A Malagasy taboo"]],
f:["Lemurs live naturally only in Madagascar.","Mouse lemurs are the smallest primates.","Many lemur groups are led by females."],
fun:"The aye-aye finds food by tapping on wood and listening for hollow tunnels.",
tl:"Ang lemur ay primate na matatagpuan lamang sa Madagascar. Sa maraming grupo ng lemur, ang mga babae ang namumuno. Isa ito sa pinakananganganib na mamalya sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"hedgehogs",t:"Hedgehogs",c:"Mammals",i:"🦔",w:"Hedgehog",r:["rodents","insect","animalbehavior","phbio","mindanao"],
p:[["Spiny coats","Hedgehogs are covered in thousands of sharp spines."],["Rolling up","When threatened, they curl into a prickly ball."],["A Philippine relative","The Mindanao gymnure is a hedgehog relative."]],
l:"Hedgehogs are small, spiny mammals found in Europe, Asia and Africa, and introduced to New Zealand. There are about 17 species. Their backs are covered in thousands of stiff, hollow spines, which are modified hairs. When threatened, a hedgehog rolls into a tight ball, protecting its soft face and belly. Hedgehogs are mainly nocturnal and eat insects, worms, snails and other small animals, which makes them welcome in gardens. In cold climates, they hibernate through the winter. Despite their appearance, hedgehogs are not related to porcupines, which are rodents. Their closest relatives are the gymnures, or moonrats, of Southeast Asia, including the Mindanao gymnure found only in the Philippines. Some hedgehog species are kept as pets.",
s:[["Spines","An adult European hedgehog has around 5,000 to 7,000 spines. They are not barbed and do not detach easily, unlike porcupine quills. Each spine is hollow with internal ridges, making it light but strong. Hedgehogs shed and regrow spines gradually."],["Rolling into a ball","A strong band of muscle running along a hedgehog’s back lets it pull its spiny skin over its head, legs and tail like a drawstring bag. Few predators can get past this defense, though badgers, owls and foxes sometimes manage."],["Diet","Hedgehogs eat beetles, caterpillars, earthworms, slugs, snails and occasionally eggs, frogs and small snakes. They find food mainly by smell and hearing, snuffling and grunting as they forage, which is how they got their name: they root around like hogs in hedges."],["Hibernation","In cold places, hedgehogs build nests of leaves and hibernate from autumn to spring. Their body temperature drops and their heart slows dramatically, allowing them to survive months without eating on stored fat. Disturbing a hibernating hedgehog can be dangerous for it."]],
k:[["Species","About 17"],["Spines on adult","About 5,000–7,000"],["Activity","Mostly nocturnal"],["Closest relatives","Gymnures, or moonrats"],["Philippine relative","Mindanao gymnure"]],
d:[["c. 50 million years ago","Early hedgehog relatives live in North America"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the European hedgehog"],["1905","Beatrix Potter publishes The Tale of Mrs. Tiggy-Winkle"],["1990s","African pygmy hedgehogs become popular pets"],["2020","Hedgehogs are listed as vulnerable on Britain’s mammal red list"],["2024","The European hedgehog is listed as near threatened worldwide"]],
s2:[["The Mindanao gymnure","The Mindanao gymnure, Podogymnura truei, is a small, soft-furred relative of hedgehogs that lives only in the mountain forests of Mindanao, such as on Mount Apo and Mount Kitanglad. It has no spines and looks somewhat like a shrew. Very little is known about its life, and it depends on the survival of the island’s highland forests."],["Self-anointing","Hedgehogs sometimes chew strong-smelling substances and then lick frothy saliva onto their spines, a strange behavior called self-anointing. Scientists are not sure why. Theories include that it helps disguise their scent, adds a defensive coating or is simply a reaction to new smells."],["Decline in Europe","Hedgehog numbers in Britain and parts of Europe have fallen sharply in recent decades. Causes include loss of hedgerows and grassland, pesticides that reduce their insect food, roads and garden fences that block their movements. Many people now cut small holes in fences to create hedgehog highways between gardens."],["Hedgehogs as pets","The African pygmy hedgehog is a popular pet in many countries, including in the Philippines. Pet hedgehogs need warmth, a proper diet and space to exercise, and they can carry germs such as salmonella. Some places restrict keeping them. In the Philippines, importing and selling exotic animals requires permits from wildlife authorities."],["Venom resistance","European hedgehogs are partly resistant to the venom of adders, a European snake, and sometimes kill and eat them. Scientists have found that a protein in hedgehog blood helps neutralize some snake toxins, though a bite can still harm them."],["Hedgehogs in stories","Hedgehogs appear in many folk tales and children’s books, such as Beatrix Potter’s Mrs. Tiggy-Winkle and stories by the Brothers Grimm. In European folklore, people once falsely believed hedgehogs stole milk from cows and collected fruit on their spines."]],
sp:["Hedgehog highways","In Britain, where hedgehogs have declined dramatically, ordinary people have joined a nationwide effort to help them. Because hedgehogs travel up to two kilometers a night searching for food and mates, solid garden fences and walls can trap them in small areas. A campaign called Hedgehog Street encourages neighbors to cut small holes, about 13 centimeters wide, at the bottom of fences to link their gardens. Volunteers have registered many thousands of these hedgehog highways on an online map. Some new housing developments now include them by design. It is a simple example of how small actions by many people can help wildlife."],
g:[["Spine","A stiff, sharp modified hair"],["Hibernation","A deep sleep through winter"],["Nocturnal","Active at night"],["Gymnure","A soft-furred relative of hedgehogs"]],
f:["Hedgehogs are not related to porcupines.","They roll into a ball for protection.","The Mindanao gymnure is a hedgehog relative."],
fun:"Hedgehogs got their name because they snuffle and grunt like hogs while searching hedges for food.",
tl:"Ang hedgehog ay maliit na mamalyang may libu-libong tinik sa likod. Kapag nanganganib, bumibilog ito na parang bola. Ang Mindanao gymnure ay kamag-anak ng hedgehog na matatagpuan lamang sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"bison",t:"Bison",c:"Mammals",i:"🦬",w:"Bison",r:["carabao","grasslands","conservation","tamaraw","wolf"],
p:[["Giants of the plains","Bison are the largest land animals in North America and Europe."],["Nearly wiped out","Hunting reduced American bison from millions to about a thousand."],["Saved and returned","Conservation brought both American and European bison back."]],
l:"Bison are large, shaggy wild cattle with humped shoulders. There are two living species: the American bison of North America, often called buffalo, and the European bison, or wisent. They are the heaviest land animals in North America and Europe, with males weighing up to about 1,000 kilograms. Bison graze on grasses and once roamed in enormous herds across the Great Plains, shaping grassland ecosystems and the lives of many Native American peoples. In the 1800s, mass hunting reduced American bison from tens of millions to only about a thousand. European bison were wiped out in the wild by the 1920s. Conservation efforts saved both species, and bison have since been reintroduced to parks and reserves. They are relatives of cattle, carabao and the tamaraw.",
s:[["American bison","American bison have a massive head, a large shoulder hump of muscle and thick, woolly fur on the front of their bodies that protects them in harsh winters. They use their heads to sweep snow aside to reach grass. Despite their size, they can run at around 55 kilometers per hour and jump fences."],["European bison","The European bison is slightly taller and longer-legged than its American cousin and lives in forests as well as meadows. It is Europe’s heaviest land animal. After being wiped out in the wild, it was rebuilt from just a few animals kept in zoos and parks, and today free-roaming herds live in Poland, Belarus and other countries."],["Shaping the land","Grazing bison keep grasses short, while their wallowing in dust and mud creates depressions that hold water for plants and animals. Their dung fertilizes the soil, and the grasses they trample help seeds spread. Prairie dogs, birds and insects all benefit from bison herds."],["Herds and behavior","Bison live in herds of females and young, while adult males often travel alone or in small groups except during the breeding season. In summer, males fight by charging and butting heads to win mates. Bison can be dangerous, and visitors to parks such as Yellowstone are warned to stay well back."]],
k:[["Species","American bison and European bison"],["Weight of large males","Up to about 1,000 kg"],["Top speed","About 55 km/h"],["American bison low point","About 1,000 in 1889"],["Relatives","Cattle, carabao, tamaraw"]],
d:[["c. 10,000 years ago","Larger ancient bison species die out in North America"],["1870s","Mass commercial hunting of American bison peaks"],["1889","Only about 1,000 American bison are estimated to survive"],["1905","The American Bison Society is founded"],["1927","The last wild European bison is killed"],["1952","European bison are released into the wild in Poland"]],
s2:[["Bison and Native Americans","For many Plains Indian nations, such as the Lakota, Cheyenne and Comanche, bison provided food, clothing, shelter, tools and spiritual meaning. Hunters used nearly every part of the animal. The loss of the herds in the late 1800s devastated these peoples’ ways of life and made them more dependent on the United States government."],["The great slaughter","Between about 1870 and 1885, commercial hunters killed millions of bison for their hides, which were made into leather for industry, and many carcasses were left to rot. Railroads made shipping hides easier. Some US Army officers and politicians welcomed the destruction as a way to force Plains nations onto reservations. Historians debate how much this was official policy, but its effects on Native peoples are widely recognized."],["Saving the bison","A few ranchers, Native Americans, conservationists and zoos protected small herds. The American Bison Society, founded in 1905, worked to establish protected herds, and in 1907 the Bronx Zoo sent bison to a reserve in Oklahoma. Yellowstone National Park is the only place where bison have lived continuously since prehistoric times. In 2016 the bison was named the national mammal of the United States."],["Bison today","Today there are several hundred thousand American bison, though most are raised commercially on ranches for meat. Only a smaller number live in conservation herds that roam more freely. Many Native American tribes manage their own herds, restoring bison to their lands, through groups such as the InterTribal Buffalo Council."],["European bison’s rescue","The last wild European bison in Poland’s Białowieża Forest was killed in 1919 and the last in the Caucasus in 1927. Scientists and zoos traced the few remaining captive animals and began a careful breeding program. In 1952 bison were returned to Białowieża Forest. Their numbers have grown to several thousand, though all descend from about a dozen founders."],["Bison and the carabao","Bison belong to the same family as cattle, goats and antelopes, and to the same tribe as cattle, yaks and Asian water buffalo. The carabao of the Philippines and the tamaraw of Mindoro are their more distant relatives. Like bison, the tamaraw was once far more widespread before hunting and habitat loss reduced its numbers."]],
sp:["The bison of Yellowstone","By 1902, only about two dozen wild bison remained in Yellowstone National Park, hiding in remote valleys and threatened by poachers. Park managers brought in bison from private herds and protected the survivors, slowly rebuilding the population. Today Yellowstone’s bison, numbering several thousand, are among the few genetically pure wild bison left. They are also the subject of disputes. Some bison carry a disease called brucellosis, and ranchers in nearby Montana fear it could spread to cattle, so bison that leave the park have sometimes been captured or culled. Conservationists and tribes argue that bison should be allowed to roam more widely, and the debate continues."],
g:[["Bison","A large wild cattle with a shoulder hump"],["Wisent","The European bison"],["Herd","A group of grazing animals"],["Wallow","A shallow hole where animals roll in dust or mud"]],
f:["Bison are the largest land animals in North America.","European bison were saved from a few zoo animals.","The bison is the US national mammal."],
fun:"Despite weighing up to a tonne, a bison can run about as fast as a horse over short distances.",
tl:"Ang bison ay malalaki at mabalahibong ligaw na baka sa Hilagang Amerika at Europa. Halos maubos sila dahil sa pangangaso noong 1800s. Kamag-anak sila ng kalabaw at tamaraw."});

ADDART({id:"platypus",t:"The Platypus",c:"Mammals",i:"🦆",w:"Platypus",r:["kangaroo","evo","australiacountry","otters","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Egg-laying mammal","The platypus is a mammal that lays eggs."],["Duck-billed","It has a bill like a duck that senses electricity."],["Venomous","Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their back legs."]],
l:"The platypus is a semi-aquatic mammal found only in eastern Australia, including Tasmania. With a duck-like bill, a beaver-like tail, webbed feet and thick fur, it looks so unusual that when the first specimen reached Britain in 1798, some scientists thought it was a hoax. The platypus is one of the monotremes, the only mammals that lay eggs, along with the echidnas. Mothers feed their young milk that seeps through patches of skin, since they have no nipples. The platypus hunts underwater with its eyes, ears and nostrils closed, finding prey by sensing the tiny electric fields produced by their muscles. Males have venomous spurs on their hind legs. The platypus is protected in Australia but is threatened by drought, dams and habitat loss.",
s:[["Monotremes","Monotremes are an ancient branch of mammals that split from other mammals more than 160 million years ago. Only five species survive: the platypus and four species of echidnas, found in Australia and New Guinea. They lay leathery eggs but have fur and produce milk like other mammals."],["The electric bill","The platypus’s soft, rubbery bill is covered with tens of thousands of special receptors that detect electric fields and movements in water. As it swims, it sweeps its head from side to side, locating shrimp, insect larvae and worms hidden in mud, even with its eyes shut."],["Venomous spurs","Males have a sharp spur on each hind ankle connected to a venom gland. The venom is most active in the breeding season, suggesting males use it against rivals. It is not deadly to humans but causes intense, long-lasting pain that ordinary painkillers barely relieve."],["Burrows and eggs","Females dig long burrows in riverbanks, where they lay one to three small eggs and curl around them to keep them warm. The babies, called puggles by some, hatch after about ten days and lap milk from their mother’s skin. They leave the burrow after several months."]],
k:[["Found in","Eastern Australia"],["Group","Monotremes"],["Reproduction","Lays eggs"],["Special sense","Electroreception"],["Defense","Venomous spurs in males"]],
d:[["1798","A platypus specimen is sent to Britain"],["1799","George Shaw describes the platypus scientifically"],["1884","William Caldwell confirms that platypuses lay eggs"],["2008","The platypus genome is first published"],["2016","The platypus is listed as near threatened"],["2020","Scientists report that platypus fur glows under ultraviolet light"]],
s2:[["A suspected hoax","When the British zoologist George Shaw examined the first platypus skin in 1799, he wrote that it was impossible not to suspect some deception. At the time, taxidermists sometimes stitched together parts of different animals to sell as curiosities. Shaw searched for stitches around the bill but found none, and he concluded the animal was real."],["Proving the eggs","For decades, European scientists argued about whether platypuses laid eggs, as Aboriginal Australians reported. In 1884 the Scottish scientist William Caldwell, working in Queensland with Aboriginal guides, found a female with an egg. He sent a famous short telegram to a scientific meeting in Canada announcing that monotremes lay eggs."],["A mosaic of features","The platypus genome, published in 2008, showed a mix of genes found in mammals, birds and reptiles. For example, its venom genes resemble those of reptiles, though they evolved separately. Its unusual features help scientists understand how mammals evolved from ancient ancestors."],["No stomach","The platypus has no true stomach; its food passes from the esophagus straight to the intestine. It stores food in cheek pouches while hunting and grinds it with hard pads, since adults have no teeth. Platypuses eat a large share of their body weight every day."],["Threats","Platypuses need healthy rivers and creeks. Dams, drought, bushfires, land clearing and fishing nets and traps that drown them have caused declines. During the 2019 to 2020 bushfires and droughts, scientists warned that platypus populations were shrinking, and some have called for stronger protection."],["Aboriginal knowledge","Aboriginal Australians have known the platypus for thousands of years and tell stories about it. In one widely shared Dreamtime story, the platypus is the child of a duck and a water rat, explaining its mixture of features. Aboriginal knowledge helped European scientists learn about its behavior."]],
sp:["The telegram from Australia","In 1884 the young Scottish scientist William Caldwell traveled to Queensland determined to settle the question of how platypuses and echidnas reproduce. With the help of Aboriginal hunters, he examined many animals. Finally, he found a female platypus that had laid one egg and had another inside her body. Caldwell sent a brief telegram to the British Association for the Advancement of Science, meeting in Montreal, Canada. It read, in essence, that monotremes were egg-laying and that their eggs were like those of reptiles and birds. The message was read aloud to the meeting and caused a sensation, ending nearly a century of debate."],
g:[["Monotreme","A mammal that lays eggs"],["Electroreception","The ability to sense electric fields"],["Spur","A sharp spike on the leg"],["Burrow","A tunnel dug as a home"]],
f:["The platypus lays eggs.","Its bill senses electricity.","Male platypuses are venomous."],
fun:"Under ultraviolet light, platypus fur glows a blue-green color.",
tl:"Ang platypus ay mamalyang nangingitlog na matatagpuan lamang sa Australia. May tuka ito na parang sa pato na nakakadama ng kuryente. May lason ang mga tinik sa paa ng lalaking platypus."});

;
/* data/animals16.js */
// Animal Kingdom: foxes, deer, the Philippine mouse-deer, cloud rats and the Visayan warty pig.
ADDART({id:"foxes",t:"Foxes",c:"Mammals",i:"🦊",w:"Fox",r:["wolf","dogs","arctic","animalbehavior","conservation"],
p:[["Clever hunters","Foxes are small members of the dog family."],["Found almost everywhere","The red fox lives on more continents than any other wild carnivore."],["From desert to Arctic","Fennec foxes live in deserts and Arctic foxes on the ice."]],
l:"Foxes are small to medium-sized members of the dog family, Canidae, with pointed ears, narrow snouts and bushy tails. The best-known are the true foxes of the genus Vulpes, which include the red fox, the Arctic fox and the tiny fennec fox of the Sahara. The red fox is the most widespread wild carnivore on Earth, living across North America, Europe, Asia and North Africa, and introduced to Australia. Foxes are mostly solitary hunters that eat rodents, rabbits, birds, insects, fruit and scraps, and many have adapted well to farmland and cities. They are known for their cunning in folklore around the world. Foxes are not native to the Philippines, but appear in many stories and fables familiar to Filipino readers.",
s:[["The red fox","Red foxes are adaptable animals with reddish-orange fur, white chests and black legs, though some are silver or dark. They hunt mice by listening carefully, then leaping high and pouncing down on prey hidden under grass or snow. Studies suggest they may use Earth’s magnetic field to aim these pounces."],["The Arctic fox","Arctic foxes survive temperatures far below freezing with thick fur, short ears and furry feet. Many turn white in winter and brown or gray in summer. They follow polar bears to eat leftovers and store food in summer for the long winter."],["The fennec fox","The fennec fox of the Sahara is the smallest fox, weighing around one kilogram, with enormous ears up to about 15 centimeters long. Its ears help it hear insects and small animals underground and release body heat in the desert. Its furry feet protect it from hot sand."],["Family life","Most foxes live alone or in pairs for much of the year but raise their young, called kits or cubs, together in dens. Both parents bring food to the young. Foxes communicate with barks, screams and scent marks, and their eerie nighttime screams are often mistaken for human cries."]],
k:[["Family","Canidae, the dog family"],["Most widespread species","Red fox"],["Smallest species","Fennec fox"],["Young","Kits or cubs"],["Native to the Philippines","No"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the red fox"],["1850s","Red foxes are introduced to Australia for hunting"],["1959","Dmitry Belyaev begins breeding tame foxes in the Soviet Union"],["2002","Hunting foxes with dogs is banned in Scotland"],["2004","Hunting with dogs is banned in England and Wales"],["2019","Scientists report an Arctic fox that walked about 3,500 km from Norway to Canada"]],
s2:[["Foxes in Australia","Red foxes were introduced to Australia in the 1800s so that settlers could hunt them for sport. They spread across much of the continent and, along with feral cats, have contributed to the extinction or decline of many small native mammals and birds. Australian authorities use baiting, fencing and other methods to control foxes and protect wildlife."],["The fox experiment","In 1959 the Soviet scientist Dmitry Belyaev began an experiment in Siberia, breeding silver foxes and selecting only the tamest each generation. After a few decades, many foxes wagged their tails, sought human attention and developed floppy ears and spotted coats. The experiment helped scientists understand how domestication may have changed wolves into dogs."],["Fox hunting debate","Hunting foxes with packs of hounds was a long tradition in Britain. Supporters argued that it controlled foxes that kill farm animals and preserved rural culture and jobs. Opponents called it cruel and unnecessary. After long debate, Scotland banned it in 2002 and England and Wales in 2004, though trail hunting, following a laid scent, continues and remains controversial."],["City foxes","Red foxes have moved into many cities, including London, Tokyo and Toronto, where they eat rubbish, pet food and rodents and raise cubs under sheds and in parks. Some people enjoy seeing them, while others consider them pests. City foxes tend to be bolder than their rural relatives."],["The clever fox in stories","Foxes appear as tricksters in folklore worldwide, from Aesop’s fable of the fox and the grapes to the medieval European tales of Reynard the Fox. In Japan and China, fox spirits called kitsune and huli jing can shape-shift into people. In Filipino schools, Aesop’s fox fables are commonly read, while Philippine folk tales give the trickster role to animals such as the monkey and the mouse-deer."],["The long-distance fox","In 2018, a young female Arctic fox fitted with a tracking collar left the Svalbard islands of Norway and walked across sea ice to Greenland and then to Canada, covering about 3,500 kilometers in 76 days. Scientists were astonished by the journey, which showed how sea ice connects Arctic animal populations and why its loss matters."]],
sp:["Foxes that wagged their tails","When Dmitry Belyaev started his experiment in 1959, his foxes came from fur farms and were fearful or aggressive toward people. Each year, his team tested the young foxes and allowed only the calmest, friendliest ones to breed. Within about ten generations, some foxes began wagging their tails and whimpering for attention like puppies. Over later generations, many developed curly tails, floppy ears and patches of white fur, features also seen in many domesticated animals. The experiment continued for decades after Belyaev’s death in 1985 and became one of the most famous studies of how domestication works."],
g:[["Canid","A member of the dog family"],["Kit","A young fox"],["Den","A shelter where foxes raise young"],["Domestication","Taming and breeding wild animals to live with people"]],
f:["The red fox is the most widespread wild carnivore.","The fennec fox is the smallest fox.","Foxes are not native to the Philippines."],
fun:"Red foxes may use Earth’s magnetic field to aim their pounces on hidden mice.",
tl:"Ang fox ay maliit na kamag-anak ng aso, kilala sa talino sa mga kuwento. Ang red fox ang pinakalaganap na ligaw na mangangaso sa mundo. Walang katutubong fox sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"deer",t:"Deer",c:"Mammals",i:"🦌",w:"Deer",r:["phbio","palawan","conservation","horses","tamaraw"],
p:[["Antlered animals","Most male deer grow antlers that they shed every year."],["Moose to pudú","Deer range from giant moose to tiny pudús."],["Philippine deer","The Philippines has three native deer species."]],
l:"Deer are hoofed mammals of the family Cervidae, found in forests, grasslands, mountains and tundra on every continent except Antarctica and Australia, where some have been introduced. There are about 50 species, from the moose, which can stand over 2 meters tall at the shoulder, to the pudú of South America, barely 40 centimeters tall. Most male deer grow bony antlers each year, shedding them after the breeding season. Deer are plant-eaters that browse on leaves, twigs and grasses. They have been hunted by people for food, hides and antlers since prehistoric times, and reindeer were domesticated. The Philippines has three native species: the Philippine brown deer, the endangered Visayan spotted deer and the Calamian deer of Palawan’s Calamian Islands.",
s:[["Antlers","Antlers are made of bone and grow from the skull each year, covered at first in a soft, furry skin called velvet that supplies blood. Antlers are among the fastest-growing tissues of any animal. When fully grown, the velvet dries and is rubbed off, and males use their antlers to fight rivals. They fall off after the breeding season and regrow the next year."],["Reindeer","Reindeer, called caribou in North America, live in the Arctic and are the only deer in which both males and females grow antlers. They migrate long distances in huge herds. Indigenous peoples such as the Sámi of northern Europe have herded reindeer for centuries, using them for meat, milk, hides and transport."],["Moose","The moose is the largest deer, with males weighing up to about 700 kilograms and carrying broad, flat antlers. Moose live in northern forests of North America, Europe and Asia, where they are called elk in Europe. They are good swimmers and feed on water plants as well as trees."],["Life as prey","Deer are prey for wolves, tigers, leopards, pythons and other predators. Their eyes on the sides of the head give a wide field of view, and their large ears catch faint sounds. Many species raise their white tails as an alarm signal when they flee."]],
k:[["Family","Cervidae"],["Species","About 50"],["Largest","Moose"],["Smallest","Pudú"],["Philippine species","Philippine brown deer, Visayan spotted deer, Calamian deer"]],
d:[["c. 15,000 years ago","Cave paintings in Europe depict deer"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the red deer"],["1967","Chronic wasting disease is first recognized in deer in Colorado"],["1976","The Calauit Island wildlife sanctuary is created in Palawan"],["1990s","A conservation breeding program for the Visayan spotted deer begins"],["2000s","Visayan spotted deer survive mainly on Negros and Panay"]],
s2:[["The Visayan spotted deer","The Visayan spotted deer, known locally as usa, is a small deer with dark brown fur covered in cream-colored spots. It once lived on several Visayan islands but now survives mainly in forests of Negros and Panay, and it is endangered because of hunting and forest loss. Conservation centers in Negros and Panay, working with local universities and foreign zoos, breed the deer and educate communities."],["The Philippine brown deer","The Philippine brown deer, also called the Philippine sambar, lives on Luzon, Mindanao, Mindoro, Samar, Leyte and other islands. It is the most widespread of the country’s deer but has declined because of hunting for meat, which is still sold illegally in some areas. It is listed as vulnerable."],["The Calamian deer","The Calamian deer is found only on a few islands of the Calamian group in northern Palawan, such as Busuanga, Culion and Calauit. It is endangered. Calauit Island, set aside as a wildlife sanctuary in 1976, also became famous for African giraffes and zebras brought there. The sanctuary’s creation required moving residents off the island, and some later returned and claimed rights to the land, making it a subject of long dispute."],["Deer in culture","Deer appear in art and stories worldwide, from prehistoric cave paintings to the story of Bambi. In Nara, Japan, hundreds of sika deer roam freely and bow to visitors who feed them special crackers. In Christianity, the deer is a symbol of the soul longing for God, and in Buddhism, the Buddha gave his first sermon in a deer park."],["Too many deer?","In parts of North America and Europe where wolves and other predators have disappeared, deer numbers have risen sharply. Large herds can eat young trees and plants faster than forests can regrow and cause many traffic accidents. Managers use regulated hunting, fencing and, in some places, the return of predators to control their numbers, though each approach has supporters and critics."],["Antlers and medicine","Deer antler velvet is used in traditional Chinese and Korean medicine, and is sold as a supplement elsewhere. Supporters claim it improves strength and health, but scientific evidence for most of these benefits is weak. Some deer farms in Asia and New Zealand raise deer for velvet."]],
sp:["The spotted deer of Negros","In the 1980s, biologists searching the forests of Negros found that the Visayan spotted deer had become very rare, hunted for meat and losing its habitat as forests were cleared for sugarcane and farms. In the 1990s, a conservation partnership involving the Philippine government, local groups, Silliman University in Dumaguete and zoos in Europe began breeding the deer in captivity. Some deer born in these programs have been sent to zoos abroad to form a backup population, while others are kept in breeding centers in Negros and Panay. Rangers and educators also work with upland communities, explaining that the usa is found nowhere else on Earth."],
g:[["Antler","A bony growth on a deer’s head, shed yearly"],["Velvet","Soft skin covering growing antlers"],["Browse","To feed on leaves and twigs"],["Usa","The Filipino word for deer"]],
f:["Most male deer shed their antlers every year.","Reindeer females also grow antlers.","The Visayan spotted deer is endangered."],
fun:"Deer antlers can grow over two centimeters in a single day.",
tl:"Ang usa ay hayop na may sungay na tinatawag na antler, na nalalaglag at muling tumutubo taon-taon. May tatlong katutubong usa sa Pilipinas, kabilang ang nanganganib na Visayan spotted deer."});

ADDART({id:"mousedeer",t:"The Philippine Mouse-Deer",c:"Mammals",i:"🦌",w:"Philippine mouse-deer",r:["deer","palawan","phbio","phfolk","conservation"],
p:[["Tiny hoofed animal","The pilandok is one of the smallest hoofed mammals."],["Not a true deer","Mouse-deer belong to an ancient family called chevrotains."],["Found only in the Philippines","It lives only on Balabac and nearby islands."]],
l:"The Philippine mouse-deer, known locally as pilandok, is a tiny hoofed mammal found only on Balabac and a few nearby islands off the southern tip of Palawan. Standing only about 40 centimeters tall, it is one of the smallest hoofed animals in the world. Despite its name, it is not a true deer or a mouse but a chevrotain, a member of an ancient family of hoofed mammals that has changed little over millions of years. Mouse-deer have no antlers; instead, males have small, tusk-like upper canine teeth. The pilandok is shy and active mostly at dawn, dusk and night. It is endangered because of hunting, the pet trade and loss of forest, and is protected under Philippine law.",
s:[["Chevrotains","Chevrotains are a family of small hoofed mammals found in the forests of Southeast Asia, India and Africa. They are among the most primitive living ruminants, the group of plant-eaters that chew the cud, which also includes deer, cattle and giraffes. Fossils show that chevrotain-like animals lived more than 30 million years ago."],["Appearance","The pilandok has a rounded body, very thin legs and small hooves. Its fur is dark brown with white stripes on its throat. It weighs only a few kilograms. Males have fang-like canine teeth, which they use in fights with rivals."],["Habitat and food","The Philippine mouse-deer lives in lowland forests and mangroves on Balabac, Bugsuk and Ramos islands. It eats fallen fruits, leaves, shoots and flowers on the forest floor, and is a shy animal that hides in dense undergrowth."],["Threats","Hunting for meat, capture for the pet trade, and clearing of forests for farms and development have reduced its numbers. Its very small range makes it especially vulnerable. It is listed as endangered, and capturing, selling or keeping one without a permit is illegal under the Philippine Wildlife Act."]],
k:[["Local name","Pilandok"],["Scientific name","Tragulus nigricans"],["Height","About 40 cm"],["Range","Balabac and nearby islands, Palawan"],["Status","Endangered"]],
d:[["c. 30 million years ago","Chevrotain-like animals live in Asia and Europe"],["1892","The Philippine mouse-deer is described for science"],["2001","The Philippine Wildlife Act protects threatened species"],["2008","The Philippine mouse-deer is listed as endangered"],["2010s","Rescue centers in Palawan care for confiscated mouse-deer"],["2019","A related mouse-deer species is rediscovered in Vietnam"]],
s2:[["Pilandok the trickster","In Philippine folklore, especially among the Maranao and other peoples of Mindanao and Palawan, Pilandok is a clever trickster who outwits kings, crocodiles and powerful enemies with quick thinking. In some stories Pilandok is a mouse-deer, and in others a clever man. Similar tales of the cunning mouse-deer, called Sang Kancil, are told in Malaysia and Indonesia."],["The crocodile bridge","In a famous Southeast Asian tale, the mouse-deer wants to cross a river full of crocodiles. He tells them the king has ordered him to count them all, so the crocodiles line up across the river. The mouse-deer hops across their backs, counting, and leaps safely onto the far bank, laughing. Versions of this story are told in the Philippines, Malaysia and Indonesia."],["A rediscovered relative","In 2019 scientists announced that camera traps in Vietnam had photographed the silver-backed chevrotain, a mouse-deer species that had not been recorded by scientists for nearly 30 years. The rediscovery gave hope for other little-known chevrotains, including the Philippine mouse-deer."],["Protecting the pilandok","Conservation groups, Palawan authorities and local communities work to stop illegal hunting and pet trade and to protect remaining forests on Balabac. Some confiscated mouse-deer are cared for at rescue centers in Puerto Princesa. Expanding protected areas and developing alternative livelihoods for local people are seen as key to its survival."],["Development pressures","Parts of Balabac and the surrounding islands have been proposed for tourism resorts, plantations and other development. Supporters argue these could bring jobs and income to one of Palawan’s poorer areas, while conservationists warn that clearing forests and mangroves could push the pilandok and other endemic species closer to extinction."],["Tiny hooves, big leaps","Although small and delicate-looking, mouse-deer are quick and agile, darting through undergrowth and leaping away from danger. When alarmed, some chevrotains drum their hooves rapidly on the ground. Mouse-deer can also swim and sometimes escape into water."]],
sp:["Counting the crocodiles","Long ago, the story goes, Pilandok the mouse-deer wanted to reach sweet fruits growing across a wide river. The river was full of hungry crocodiles. Pilandok called out that the sultan had ordered a feast and needed to know how many crocodiles lived in the river, so he had been sent to count them. Proud and greedy, the crocodiles lined up from one bank to the other. Pilandok jumped from back to back, counting aloud, one, two, three, until he reached the far shore. Then he thanked them and skipped away, leaving the crocodiles grumbling. Children across the Philippines and its neighbors still enjoy this tale of brains defeating brawn."],
g:[["Pilandok","The Filipino name for the Philippine mouse-deer"],["Chevrotain","A small, ancient type of hoofed mammal"],["Ruminant","A plant-eater that chews the cud"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"]],
f:["The pilandok is not a true deer.","It lives only on Balabac and nearby islands.","Male mouse-deer have tusk-like teeth instead of antlers."],
fun:"An adult Philippine mouse-deer is about as tall as a house cat.",
tl:"Ang pilandok ay napakaliit na hayop na may kuko na matatagpuan lamang sa Balabac at kalapit na mga isla sa Palawan. Hindi ito tunay na usa kundi isang chevrotain. Kilala rin si Pilandok bilang matalinong tauhan sa mga kuwentong-bayan."});

ADDART({id:"cloudrats",t:"Cloud Rats",c:"Mammals",i:"🐀",w:"Cloud rat",r:["rodents","phbio","banaue","mindanao","conservation"],
p:[["Giant tree rats","Cloud rats are large, furry rodents found only in the Philippines."],["Bushy tails","Some cloud rats have long, fluffy tails."],["A Philippine treasure","Luzon has one of the richest collections of unique mammals on Earth."]],
l:"Cloud rats are large, furry rodents that live only in the Philippines, mostly in the mountain forests of Luzon. The name refers to their home in high, misty cloud forests. They include the giant cloud rats of the genus Phloeomys, which can weigh around two kilograms and are among the largest rats in the world, and the bushy-tailed cloud rats of the genus Crateromys, found on Luzon, Panay, Dinagat and Ilin Island. Cloud rats are mostly nocturnal plant-eaters that climb trees and nest in hollows. They are part of a remarkable group of mammals that evolved only on Philippine islands. Several species are threatened by hunting and forest loss, and one may already be extinct.",
s:[["Giant cloud rats","The northern and southern giant cloud rats of Luzon have long, shaggy fur, often a mix of white, brown and black, small ears and long tails. They live in forests and even forest edges and plantations. They are hunted for meat in some upland areas, and are occasionally kept in zoos and parks."],["Bushy-tailed cloud rats","Bushy-tailed cloud rats have thick, fluffy tails that may help them balance in trees. The Luzon bushy-tailed cloud rat lives in the Cordillera mountains. The Panay cloud rat was described in 1996, the Dinagat bushy-tailed cloud rat was known for decades from a single specimen until it was photographed alive in 2012, and the Ilin Island cloud rat has not been seen since the 1950s."],["Life in the trees","Cloud rats climb with strong claws, feeding on leaves, buds, fruits and bark at night. They spend the day in tree hollows or dense vegetation. Unlike the common rats found in houses and rice fields, which were brought by people, cloud rats are native forest animals that do not invade homes."],["Island evolution","Luzon has been separated from mainland Asia for millions of years. Ancestors of cloud rats arrived long ago and evolved into many new forms. Separate mountain ranges on Luzon, like islands within an island, each developed their own unique rodent species."]],
k:[["Found in","The Philippines only"],["Largest","Giant cloud rats, about 2 kg"],["Main island","Luzon"],["Activity","Mostly nocturnal"],["Food","Leaves, fruits, bark"]],
d:[["1890s","Scientists describe the giant cloud rats of Luzon"],["1953","The only known Ilin Island cloud rat specimen is collected"],["1981","The Dinagat bushy-tailed cloud rat is described"],["1996","The Panay cloud rat is described"],["2012","The Dinagat bushy-tailed cloud rat is photographed alive"],["2016","Scientists report dozens of mammal species found only on Luzon"]],
s2:[["Luzon’s mammal treasures","A long-term survey of Luzon’s mountains led by researchers from the Field Museum in Chicago and the National Museum of the Philippines, including the late Filipino biologist Danilo Balete, found that the vast majority of the island’s native non-flying mammals live nowhere else. Many were new to science, including several species of shrew-rats and forest mice. Scientists have called Luzon one of the richest places on Earth for unique mammals."],["Rediscovery on Dinagat","The Dinagat bushy-tailed cloud rat was first described from a specimen collected in 1975. For decades no scientist saw another, and some feared it was extinct. In 2012 researchers working with local people photographed a living individual on Dinagat Island. The island’s forests are threatened by mining, which some communities depend on for income while others oppose it because of its environmental effects."],["Possibly extinct","The Ilin Island cloud rat is known only from a single specimen bought from local hunters in 1953 on Ilin Island, near Mindoro. Little forest remains on the island, and no further individuals have been found. It may already be extinct, a reminder of how quickly small-island species can disappear."],["Cloud rats and people","In some upland communities, cloud rats are traditionally hunted for food, and they sometimes eat crops near forests. Conservationists work with communities to reduce hunting of rare species and protect forests, noting that large cloud rats reproduce slowly, often having only one young at a time."],["Seeing them","Giant cloud rats are sometimes kept at wildlife centers in the Philippines, where visitors can see them. In the wild, they are hard to observe because they are active at night high in trees. Some birdwatchers and wildlife photographers in Luzon’s mountains have spotted them at night with flashlights."],["Not house rats","The rats commonly seen in Philippine cities and farms are introduced species, such as the black rat and the brown rat, which spread around the world with human trade. They can damage crops and spread disease. Cloud rats and most other native Philippine rodents are shy forest animals that rarely encounter people."]],
sp:["A rat photographed at last","For almost four decades, the Dinagat bushy-tailed cloud rat existed for science only as a single preserved specimen in a museum drawer. Biologists who searched Dinagat’s forests could not find it, and the species was feared lost. In 2012 a team of Filipino and foreign researchers, guided by local people who knew the forest, set up camera traps and searched at night. Finally they found and photographed a living cloud rat, with dark fur and a long, bushy tail, clinging to a tree. The rediscovery showed that the species survived and highlighted the importance of protecting Dinagat’s remaining forests."],
g:[["Cloud rat","A large tree-dwelling rodent of the Philippines"],["Cloud forest","A misty mountain forest"],["Rodent","A mammal with ever-growing front teeth"],["Specimen","A preserved animal kept for study"]],
f:["Cloud rats live only in the Philippines.","Giant cloud rats can weigh about 2 kg.","The Dinagat cloud rat was photographed alive in 2012."],
fun:"A giant cloud rat can weigh about as much as a small house cat.",
tl:"Ang cloud rat ay malalaking dagang-puno na matatagpuan lamang sa Pilipinas, lalo na sa kabundukan ng Luzon. Kumakain sila ng dahon at prutas sa gabi. Hindi sila tulad ng mga dagang-bahay na dinala lamang ng tao."});

ADDART({id:"wartypig",t:"The Visayan Warty Pig",c:"Mammals",i:"🐗",w:"Visayan warty pig",r:["phbio","deer","conservation","deforestation","domestication"],
p:[["A rare wild pig","The Visayan warty pig lives only on Negros and Panay."],["Punk hairstyle","Males grow a spiky mane in the breeding season."],["Critically endangered","It is one of the most threatened pigs in the world."]],
l:"The Visayan warty pig, Sus cebifrons, is a small wild pig found only in the central Philippines. It once lived on Cebu, Negros, Panay, Masbate, Guimaras and other Visayan islands, but today survives mainly in the remaining forests of Negros and Panay, and it is listed as critically endangered. Males have three pairs of fleshy warts on their faces, which may protect them during fights, and in the breeding season they grow a long, spiky mane of hair on their heads and backs, giving them a striking appearance. The pig has declined because of forest loss, hunting and interbreeding with domestic pigs. Conservation breeding programs in the Philippines and in zoos abroad aim to save it. The Philippines has several other native wild pig species.",
s:[["Appearance","Visayan warty pigs are smaller than domestic pigs, with dark gray to black bristly fur. Adult males have three pairs of warts on the face and grow a mane of long hair, sometimes pale, that stands up like a crest when they are excited. Females are smaller and lack the long mane."],["Life in the forest","These pigs live in small family groups in forests and grasslands. They root in the soil for tubers, roots, fallen fruits and earthworms, helping turn over soil and spread seeds. They are mostly active in the early morning and evening."],["Disappearing from islands","Forests on Cebu, Guimaras and other Visayan islands were cleared long ago for farms and towns, and the warty pig vanished from many of them. On Negros and Panay, it survives in mountains such as the Central Panay Mountain Range and Mount Kanlaon, though remaining forests are small and scattered."],["Interbreeding","Where wild and domestic pigs meet, they can breed, producing hybrids. This mixing threatens the genetic identity of the Visayan warty pig. Wild pigs that raid crops are also sometimes killed by farmers."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Sus cebifrons"],["Range today","Negros and Panay"],["Status","Critically endangered"],["Male feature","Three pairs of facial warts"],["Main threats","Forest loss, hunting, interbreeding"]],
d:[["1888","The Visayan warty pig is described for science"],["1990s","Conservation breeding begins in the Philippines and Europe"],["1996","The species is listed as critically endangered"],["2000s","Breeding centers operate in Negros and Panay"],["2019","African swine fever reaches the Philippines, raising concern for wild pigs"],["2020s","Field surveys continue in Panay and Negros mountains"]],
s2:[["Philippine wild pigs","The Philippines has several native wild pigs. The Philippine warty pig lives on Luzon, Mindanao and other islands, and the Mindoro warty pig on Mindoro. The Palawan bearded pig lives on Palawan and nearby islands. All are hunted for meat, and some are threatened by forest loss."],["Saving the species","In the 1990s, Philippine conservation groups, local governments and European zoos began a breeding program for the Visayan warty pig. Pigs are bred at centers in Negros, such as in Bacolod, and in zoos abroad, creating backup populations. Researchers also survey wild populations and work with communities to protect forests."],["African swine fever","African swine fever, a deadly viral disease of pigs, reached the Philippines in 2019, devastating many backyard and commercial pig farms. It does not harm humans, but it can kill wild pigs. Conservationists worry it could spread to the small, rare populations of native wild pigs and have urged careful biosecurity."],["Hunting and culture","Wild pigs have long been hunted in the Philippines, and wild boar meat is valued in some communities. Hunting of threatened species like the Visayan warty pig is illegal under the Wildlife Act, but enforcement in remote mountains is difficult. Conservation groups emphasize education and alternative livelihoods for hunters."],["Forest gardeners","By rooting in the soil and eating fallen fruits, wild pigs mix the soil, bury seeds and help forests regenerate. Losing them can change how forests grow. Scientists consider native wild pigs an important part of healthy Philippine forest ecosystems."],["A pig with a hairstyle","The male warty pig’s breeding-season mane, which can be long and spiky like a punk hairstyle, has made it popular with zoo visitors abroad. Zoos use this attention to teach visitors about Philippine wildlife and to raise funds for conservation in the Visayas."]],
sp:["Searching the Panay mountains","High in the rugged Central Panay Mountain Range, a team of Filipino biologists and forest rangers hikes for days through steep, misty forest. They are looking for signs of the Visayan warty pig: patches of soil turned over by rooting, tracks in the mud and wallows where pigs have rolled. Camera traps strapped to trees capture photos of the pigs at dawn, revealing family groups with striped piglets. These surveys show that the species still survives in the wild, though in small numbers. The team shares their findings with local communities and officials, hoping to strengthen protection of one of the last strongholds of this unique Philippine animal."],
g:[["Warty pig","A wild pig with fleshy warts on its face"],["Hybrid","The offspring of two different species or types"],["Wallow","A muddy hollow where pigs roll"],["Critically endangered","At extremely high risk of extinction"]],
f:["The Visayan warty pig lives only in the Philippines.","Males grow a spiky mane in the breeding season.","It survives mainly on Negros and Panay."],
fun:"Male Visayan warty pigs grow a dramatic mane during the breeding season, like a punk hairstyle.",
tl:"Ang Visayan warty pig ay bihirang baboy-ramo na matatagpuan lamang sa Negros at Panay. Lubhang nanganganib itong maubos. May kulugo sa mukha ang mga lalaki at tumutubo ang mahabang buhok sa ulo tuwing panahon ng pagpaparami."});

;
