// Animal Kingdom: the Philippine cockatoo, swiftlets, kingfishers, pythons and cobras.
ADDART({id:"cockatoo",t:"The Philippine Cockatoo",c:"Birds",i:"🦜",w:"Red-vented cockatoo",r:["parrot","palawan","phbio","conservation","birds"],
p:[["The katala","The Philippine cockatoo is known locally as katala."],["Red underneath","It has bright red feathers under its tail."],["Fighting back","Community conservation has helped it recover on Rasa Island."]],
l:"The Philippine cockatoo, also called the red-vented cockatoo and known locally as katala, is a white cockatoo found only in the Philippines. It is named for the red feathers under its tail, and has a pale yellow crest it raises when excited. Once common across much of the country, it has become critically endangered, mainly because nestlings were taken for the pet trade and its lowland forests and mangroves were cleared. Today it survives mostly in Palawan, including Rasa Island and Dumaran, and in the Sulu archipelago, especially Tawi-Tawi. Conservation efforts involving local communities, former poachers turned wardens and protected areas have helped its numbers grow on some islands, making it a hopeful example for Philippine wildlife.",
s:[["Appearance","The katala is a medium-sized cockatoo, about 30 centimeters long, with white feathers, a short crest tinged yellow, and red and yellow under its tail. Like other cockatoos, it has a strong, curved beak for cracking seeds and nuts."],["Habitat and food","Philippine cockatoos live in lowland forests, mangroves and coastal areas. They feed on fruits, seeds, flowers and buds, flying between islands and the mainland to find food. They nest in hollows of large trees, often near the coast."],["Why it declined","For decades, poachers climbed nesting trees to steal chicks for sale as pets, since cockatoos are intelligent, can mimic speech and fetch high prices. At the same time, lowland forests and mangroves were cut for farms, fishponds and coconut plantations, removing the large trees cockatoos need."],["Cockatoos of the world","Cockatoos are a family of about 21 species of parrots, found mainly in Australia, New Guinea, Indonesia and the Philippines. They are known for their crests, loud calls and long lives; some cockatoos in captivity have lived more than 80 years. The Philippine cockatoo is the only cockatoo native to the Philippines."]],
k:[["Local name","Katala"],["Scientific name","Cacatua haematuropygia"],["Status","Critically endangered"],["Strongholds","Palawan and Tawi-Tawi"],["Main past threat","Nest poaching for pets"]],
d:[["1776","The Philippine cockatoo is described for science"],["1990s","Surveys show the species has disappeared from much of its range"],["1998","A conservation program begins on Rasa Island, Palawan"],["2006","Rasa Island is declared a wildlife sanctuary"],["2010s","Cockatoo numbers rise in protected areas of Palawan"],["2020s","Conservationists work to restore populations in other areas"]],
s2:[["Poachers turned protectors","One of the most successful conservation ideas for the katala was hiring former poachers as wardens. They knew exactly where cockatoos nested and how to climb the trees. Instead of stealing chicks, they now guard nests during the breeding season and are paid for their work. This approach, begun by the Katala Foundation in Palawan, has been praised as a model of community-based conservation."],["Rasa Island","Rasa Island, a small coral island off the town of Narra in southern Palawan, became the most important stronghold of the Philippine cockatoo. After protection began in 1998, the island’s cockatoo population grew several times over. In 2006 it was declared a wildlife sanctuary, and visitors can sometimes see flocks flying between the island and the mainland at dawn and dusk."],["Tawi-Tawi","The Sulu archipelago, especially Tawi-Tawi, still has some cockatoos, but forests there have been heavily cleared and security conditions have made fieldwork difficult. Local groups and the government have worked to survey and protect remaining birds."],["Pets and the law","Keeping, selling or transporting Philippine cockatoos without a permit is illegal under the Philippine Wildlife Act. Conservationists urge people not to buy wild-caught parrots. Some confiscated birds are cared for at rescue centers and may be used for breeding programs."],["A flagship species","The katala has become a flagship species for Palawan conservation, appearing on posters, festivals and school programs. Protecting its nesting trees and mangroves also helps many other species, including fish nurseries along the coast. Conservationists say the cockatoo’s recovery shows that extinction is not inevitable."],["Clever parrots","Cockatoos are highly intelligent. In studies abroad, cockatoos have learned to pick complex locks, use tools and even dance to music. In Australia, wild sulphur-crested cockatoos have learned from each other how to open household rubbish bins."]],
sp:["Dawn on Rasa Island","Just after sunrise, a loud, harsh screeching rises from the mangroves of Rasa Island. Dozens of white birds lift off together and fly across the channel toward mainland Palawan, their red undertail feathers flashing in the light. They are Philippine cockatoos, heading out to feed on fruits and seeds for the day. Watching them, a warden who once climbed these same trees to steal chicks now counts them carefully and records the numbers in a notebook. In the evening, the flock returns to roost on the island. Scenes like this were rare in the 1990s, when the katala seemed close to vanishing; today they offer hope."],
g:[["Cockatoo","A crested parrot"],["Poaching","Illegally taking wild animals"],["Warden","A person who guards wildlife"],["Flagship species","A species used to rally support for conservation"]],
f:["The katala is found only in the Philippines.","It has red feathers under its tail.","Former poachers now guard its nests."],
fun:"Some cockatoos can live for more than 80 years in captivity.",
tl:"Ang katala o Philippine cockatoo ay puting ibong matatagpuan lamang sa Pilipinas, na may pulang balahibo sa ilalim ng buntot. Lubha itong nanganganib. Tumulong ang mga dating poacher na naging tagapagbantay upang dumami ito sa Rasa Island, Palawan."});

ADDART({id:"swiftlets",t:"Swiftlets and Edible Nests",c:"Birds",i:"🐦",w:"Edible bird's nest",r:["birds","palawan","phfood","bat","hummingbird"],
p:[["Nests of saliva","Some swiftlets build nests made almost entirely of their own saliva."],["A costly delicacy","These nests are used to make bird’s nest soup."],["El Nido","El Nido in Palawan is named after swiftlet nests."]],
l:"Swiftlets are small birds of the swift family that live in tropical Asia and the Pacific, including the Philippines. Some species, especially the edible-nest swiftlet, build cup-shaped nests on cave walls made almost entirely of their own hardened saliva. These nests are harvested to make bird’s nest soup, a delicacy in Chinese cuisine for centuries and one of the most expensive animal products in the world. Many swiftlets can navigate in the dark caves where they nest using echolocation, producing clicking sounds and listening for echoes, an ability shared with only a few birds. The town of El Nido in Palawan takes its name from the Spanish word for nest, referring to the swiftlet nests collected from its limestone cliffs.",
s:[["Saliva nests","During the breeding season, edible-nest swiftlets produce large amounts of sticky saliva from glands under their tongues. They lay it down in strands on cave walls, and it hardens into a white, cup-shaped nest. Building one can take about a month or more. Other swiftlets mix their saliva with feathers or plant material, making black or mixed nests of lower value."],["Echolocation","Swiftlets that roost deep in dark caves produce rapid clicking calls and listen to the echoes to avoid walls and find their nests, much like bats. Unlike bats, they use clicks we can hear and do not use echolocation to catch food; they hunt flying insects outside in daylight."],["Master fliers","Swiftlets and swifts spend most of their lives in the air, catching insects on the wing. Their legs are tiny, and they cannot perch on branches like most birds; instead, they cling to vertical surfaces. Some swifts stay airborne for months without landing."],["Bird’s nest soup","Edible bird’s nests are cleaned of feathers, then soaked and cooked into a soft, gelatinous soup, often sweetened. In Chinese tradition, they are believed to benefit health and skin. Scientists have found that the nests contain proteins and other nutrients, but many of the health claims have not been confirmed by strong evidence."]],
k:[["Bird family","Swifts"],["Famous species","Edible-nest swiftlet"],["Nest material","Hardened saliva"],["Navigation in caves","Echolocation"],["Philippine place name","El Nido, Palawan"]],
d:[["1300s–1600s","Bird’s nest soup becomes a luxury food in China"],["1500s","Chinese traders buy swiftlet nests from Southeast Asian islands"],["1990s","Swiftlet houses spread in Malaysia and Indonesia"],["2000s","Palawan regulates the collection of nests in El Nido"],["2016","Scientists report swifts staying airborne for ten months"],["2020s","Southeast Asia supplies most edible nests to China"]],
s2:[["Collectors of El Nido","In the limestone caves of El Nido and other parts of Palawan, licensed collectors climb tall bamboo poles and ladders to reach nests on cave ceilings and walls, a dangerous job passed down in families. Harvesting is allowed only at certain times so that swiftlets can raise their chicks. Local government regulations aim to keep harvesting sustainable."],["Swiftlet houses","In Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand and Vietnam, many people now farm swiftlets in specially built concrete buildings called swiftlet houses. Loudspeakers play swiftlet calls to attract birds, which nest inside, and owners harvest the nests. The industry has grown enormously, though neighbors sometimes complain about the noise, and its spread has raised questions about effects on wild populations."],["Overharvesting","Because the nests are so valuable, some caves have been overharvested, with nests taken before eggs hatched. In places where this happened, swiftlet populations declined sharply. Conservationists support rules that allow harvesting only after young birds have left the nests, and protecting some caves from harvesting entirely."],["Fake nests","The high price of edible nests has led to fraud, including nests bleached, glued or mixed with other materials to look whiter and weigh more. Authorities in several countries have tested nests for additives and banned unsafe products."],["Swifts versus swallows","Swifts and swallows look similar and both catch insects in flight, but they are not closely related. Swifts are closer relatives of hummingbirds. Their similar shapes are an example of convergent evolution, when unrelated animals evolve similar features for a similar way of life."],["Birds of El Nido","El Nido, now one of the Philippines’ most popular tourist destinations, was named for the swiftlet nests by Spanish-era settlers. Its towering limestone cliffs and islands provide nesting sites for swiftlets as well as habitat for other birds and marine life. Tourism and environmental protection have become central issues for the town."]],
sp:["Climbing for nests","In the dim light of a sea cave in northern Palawan, a nest collector checks his bamboo pole, lashed together with rattan, and begins to climb. Far above, near the cave ceiling, rows of small white nests cling to the rock. With a long-handled scraper and a flashlight, he carefully pries the nests loose, dropping them into a basket at his waist. Swiftlets dart past his head, clicking as they navigate in the darkness. The work is dangerous, and a fall can be fatal. The nests he collects will be cleaned, sorted and sold, many eventually ending up in expensive soups in Hong Kong, Taipei or mainland China."],
g:[["Swiftlet","A small bird of the swift family"],["Echolocation","Finding the way using echoes"],["Edible nest","A nest made of saliva used in soup"],["Convergent evolution","Unrelated animals evolving similar features"]],
f:["Edible nests are made of swiftlet saliva.","Swiftlets can echolocate in caves.","El Nido is named after swiftlet nests."],
fun:"A kilogram of the best white edible nests can cost thousands of dollars.",
tl:"Ang swiftlet ay maliit na ibon na gumagawa ng pugad mula sa sarili nitong laway. Ginagamit ang pugad na ito sa mamahaling bird’s nest soup. Ang El Nido sa Palawan ay ipinangalan sa mga pugad na ito."});

ADDART({id:"kingfishers",t:"Kingfishers",c:"Birds",i:"🐦",w:"Kingfisher",r:["birds","fishes","wetlands","phbio","eagle"],
p:[["Diving hunters","Many kingfishers plunge into water to catch fish."],["Jewel colors","Kingfishers are known for bright blue, orange and green feathers."],["Philippine kingfishers","The Philippines has many kingfisher species, some found nowhere else."]],
l:"Kingfishers are a family of brightly colored birds with large heads, long, sharp bills and short legs, found on every continent except Antarctica. There are over 100 species, from the tiny African dwarf kingfisher to the laughing kookaburra of Australia. Many kingfishers hunt fish by perching above water and diving in headfirst, but many others, especially in forests, catch insects, lizards and frogs on land. Kingfishers usually nest in burrows dug into riverbanks or termite mounds, or in tree holes. The Philippines is a hotspot for kingfishers, with numerous species, including several found only in the country, such as the spotted wood kingfisher and the Philippine dwarf kingfisher. The kingfisher’s beak inspired the design of a Japanese bullet train.",
s:[["Diving for fish","A fishing kingfisher perches still above water, judging the depth and position of a fish while adjusting for the way water bends light. It then dives, closing a protective membrane over its eyes, grabs the fish in its bill and flies back to its perch. It often beats the fish against a branch before swallowing it headfirst."],["Forest kingfishers","Many kingfishers rarely fish at all. Forest species, such as the Philippine spotted wood kingfisher, wait quietly in the shade and pounce on insects, worms and small lizards on the ground. The kookaburra of Australia even eats snakes."],["Colors from structure","The brilliant blue of many kingfishers does not come from blue pigment. Instead, tiny structures in their feathers scatter light to produce blue, a phenomenon called structural color. This is why a kingfisher’s color can seem to change with the angle of light."],["Burrow nests","Most kingfishers dig nesting tunnels by repeatedly flying at a bank and striking it with their bills, then scraping out a tunnel with their feet. Some tunnels are a meter long, ending in a chamber where eggs are laid. Others nest in tree cavities or in termite nests."]],
k:[["Family","Alcedinidae"],["Species","Over 100"],["Largest","Laughing kookaburra and giant kingfisher"],["Nest","Burrows or tree holes"],["Philippine endemic example","Spotted wood kingfisher"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the common kingfisher"],["1800s","Kingfisher feathers are used in Chinese jewelry"],["1997","A Japanese bullet train with a kingfisher-inspired nose enters service"],["2000s","DNA studies reveal new kingfisher species in the Philippines"],["2010s","Birdwatching tourism grows in Philippine forests"],["2020s","Conservationists warn of threats to forest kingfishers from logging"]],
s2:[["Kingfishers of the Philippines","The Philippines has a large variety of kingfishers. Common species include the collared kingfisher, often seen in mangroves and coastal areas, and the white-throated kingfisher of open country. Forest endemics include the spotted wood kingfisher of Luzon and Mindanao, the Philippine dwarf kingfisher and other rare island species. DNA studies have shown that some birds once thought to be one species on different islands are actually separate species."],["The bullet train","In the 1990s, Japanese engineers had a problem: when their fast Shinkansen trains exited tunnels, they produced loud booms that disturbed residents. Engineer Eiji Nakatsu, a birdwatcher, noticed that kingfishers dive into water with barely a splash thanks to their long, tapered beaks. A new train nose modeled on the kingfisher’s beak, introduced in 1997, was quieter, faster and used less electricity, a famous example of biomimicry."],["Kingfisher feathers in art","In imperial China, artisans glued iridescent kingfisher feathers onto metal to make jewelry, headdresses and decorations, a technique called tian-tsui. The craft required huge numbers of birds and was eventually abandoned in the 20th century. Today such antiques are prized in museums."],["Kingfishers in myth","In Greek mythology, Alcyone was turned into a kingfisher, and the gods calmed the seas so she could nest; this gave rise to the expression halcyon days, meaning calm, happy times. In some Pacific and Southeast Asian traditions, kingfishers are omens or messengers."],["Threats","Forest kingfishers depend on intact forests and are threatened by logging and land clearing in the Philippines and elsewhere. River kingfishers suffer from water pollution, which reduces fish, and from riverbank changes that destroy nest sites. Healthy kingfisher populations are often a sign of clean waterways."],["Birdwatching","Kingfishers are favorites of birdwatchers and photographers because of their colors and dramatic dives. Birdwatching tours in places such as Mount Kitanglad, Palawan and the Subic forests attract visitors hoping to see rare Philippine kingfishers, providing income for local guides."]],
sp:["The train that copied a bird","In the late 1980s and 1990s, Japan’s high-speed trains had grown so fast that they created a problem. As a train rushed into a narrow tunnel, it pushed a wave of air ahead of it, which burst out of the other end with a boom that could be heard hundreds of meters away. Eiji Nakatsu, an engineer and member of a wild bird society, wondered how a kingfisher could plunge from air into water without a splash. He tested train noses of different shapes and found that one closely resembling a kingfisher’s beak greatly reduced the tunnel boom. The 500 Series Shinkansen, introduced in 1997, used this design and became a celebrated example of engineers learning from nature."],
g:[["Kingfisher","A bird with a large head and sharp bill"],["Structural color","Color produced by tiny structures rather than pigment"],["Biomimicry","Designing technology inspired by nature"],["Halcyon","A word linked to the kingfisher, meaning calm"]],
f:["Not all kingfishers eat fish.","A kingfisher’s beak inspired a bullet train.","Kingfisher blue comes from feather structure."],
fun:"The laughing kookaburra of Australia, famous for its loud call, is a kingfisher.",
tl:"Ang kingfisher ay makukulay na ibon na may mahaba at matalas na tuka. Maraming uri nito ang sumisisid sa tubig upang manghuli ng isda. Marami ang uri nito sa Pilipinas, at ang tuka nito ay ginaya sa disenyo ng tren sa Japan."});

ADDART({id:"pythons",t:"Pythons",c:"Reptiles",i:"🐍",w:"Pythonidae",r:["snake","lizard","croc","phbio","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Giant snakes","Pythons include some of the longest snakes in the world."],["Squeezing hunters","They kill prey by coiling around it and squeezing."],["A Philippine giant","The reticulated python, or sawa, lives in the Philippines."]],
l:"Pythons are a family of large, non-venomous snakes found in Africa, Asia and Australia. There are about 40 species, ranging from small pythons under a meter long to the reticulated python of Southeast Asia, the longest snake in the world, which can exceed 6 meters. Pythons are constrictors: they seize prey with backward-pointing teeth, wrap their coils around it and squeeze, stopping blood flow and killing it quickly. They can swallow animals much wider than their heads, including pigs, deer and occasionally even people, though attacks on humans are rare. Many pythons can sense the body heat of prey using special pits along their lips. The reticulated python, called sawa in the Philippines, is found across the country.",
s:[["How constriction works","When a python strikes, it grips its prey with many sharp, curved teeth, then rapidly wraps coils around the animal. Each time the prey breathes out, the python tightens. Studies in 2015 showed that constriction kills mainly by stopping blood circulation, which happens within minutes."],["Swallowing huge meals","Python jaws are connected by stretchy ligaments, and the two halves of the lower jaw can spread apart. This allows a python to swallow prey much wider than its head. After a big meal, a python may not eat again for weeks or months, and its heart and other organs temporarily grow to help digest."],["Heat-sensing pits","Many pythons have rows of pits along their lips that detect infrared radiation, or heat, from warm-blooded animals. This lets them sense prey in complete darkness. Combined with smell from their flicking tongues, it makes them effective night hunters."],["Mother pythons","Most snakes leave their eggs after laying them, but female pythons coil around their eggs. Some species shiver their muscles to raise the temperature of the eggs, a rare example of a reptile producing heat. They stay with the eggs until they hatch."]],
k:[["Family","Pythonidae"],["Species","About 40"],["Longest species","Reticulated python"],["Venomous","No"],["Filipino name","Sawa"]],
d:[["1801","The reticulated python is described for science"],["1979","A Burmese python is first recorded in Florida’s Everglades"],["2000","Scientists confirm Burmese pythons are breeding in the Everglades"],["2012","The United States bans imports of Burmese pythons"],["2015","Scientists show how constriction kills prey"],["2023","A record-length Burmese python is captured in Florida"]],
s2:[["The sawa","The reticulated python, or sawa, lives in forests, grasslands, farms and even towns across the Philippines and Southeast Asia. Its intricate net-like pattern gives it its name, from the Latin for net. Sawa sometimes enter homes and farms to catch chickens, dogs and rats, and they are often captured and relocated by rescue teams. Reticulated pythons are excellent swimmers and have colonized many islands."],["Pythons and people","Most pythons avoid people, but very large reticulated pythons have occasionally killed and swallowed humans, with a few documented cases in Indonesia in recent years. A study among the Agta people of Luzon, hunter-gatherers who lived in forests, found in the 1970s and 1980s that a significant number had survived python attacks. Such events remain rare."],["Invasion of the Everglades","Burmese pythons, released or escaped from the pet trade, have established a large population in Florida’s Everglades. Studies have linked them to steep declines of raccoons, opossums, rabbits and other mammals there. Florida pays hunters to remove pythons and holds python-catching challenges, but eradicating them has proved extremely difficult."],["The skin trade","Python skins are used to make luxury handbags, shoes and belts, and large numbers are exported from Southeast Asia. Some trade is legal and regulated, while some is illegal or involves cruelty. Fashion companies and conservation groups debate whether the trade can be made sustainable or should be reduced."],["Pythons as pets","Ball pythons and other small pythons are popular pets because they are calm and easy to handle. Experts warn that large species such as reticulated and Burmese pythons can become dangerous and difficult to care for, and that releasing pets into the wild can harm ecosystems, as in Florida."],["Pythons and boas","Pythons are often confused with boas, another family of constricting snakes. Boas mostly live in the Americas and usually give birth to live young, while pythons live in the Old World and lay eggs. The green anaconda of South America, a boa, is the heaviest snake, while the reticulated python is the longest."]],
sp:["The Everglades python hunters","Every summer, people from across the United States travel to the swamps of southern Florida for a contest unlike any other: the Florida Python Challenge. Armed with flashlights, snake hooks and determination, they search levees and roads at night for invasive Burmese pythons. Some catch none; others catch dozens. The pythons are humanely killed, measured and studied. In 2023, hunters and scientists reported a Burmese python measuring about 5.8 meters, a record for the state. Despite the efforts of hunters, trained dogs and researchers, tens of thousands of pythons may still be hiding in the vast wetlands, showing how hard it is to remove an invasive species once it has spread."],
g:[["Constrictor","A snake that kills by squeezing"],["Heat pit","An organ that senses body heat"],["Invasive species","A non-native species that spreads harmfully"],["Sawa","The Filipino word for python"]],
f:["The reticulated python is the longest snake.","Pythons are not venomous.","Female pythons guard their eggs."],
fun:"After a very large meal, a python’s heart can grow noticeably bigger for a while.",
tl:"Ang sawa ay malalaking ahas na walang kamandag at pumapatay sa pamamagitan ng pagpulupot at pagpisil. Ang reticulated python, na matatagpuan sa Pilipinas, ang pinakamahabang ahas sa mundo. Kaya nitong lumunok ng hayop na mas malaki pa sa ulo nito."});

ADDART({id:"cobras",t:"Cobras",c:"Reptiles",i:"🐍",w:"Cobra",r:["snake","seasnakes","firstaid","phbio","egypt"],
p:[["Spreading hoods","Cobras spread a hood when threatened."],["Spitting venom","Some cobras, including the Philippine cobra, spit venom at attackers’ eyes."],["King of snakes","The king cobra is the longest venomous snake."]],
l:"Cobras are venomous snakes known for spreading their necks into a hood when threatened. Most belong to the genus Naja and live in Africa and Asia, including the Philippines. Cobras have short, fixed front fangs and powerful venom that attacks the nervous system and can cause paralysis and death if untreated. Several species, including the Philippine cobra, are spitting cobras that can spray venom into the eyes of a threat several meters away. The king cobra, which belongs to a separate genus, is the longest venomous snake in the world and feeds mostly on other snakes; in 2024 scientists proposed that it is actually four species, including one in Luzon. Cobras play important roles in controlling rodents and are prominent in myths and religions.",
s:[["The hood","When threatened, a cobra raises the front of its body and spreads loose skin over long, movable ribs in its neck, forming a hood. This makes it look larger and warns enemies to keep away. Some cobras have markings on the back of the hood, such as the spectacle pattern of the Indian cobra."],["Venom","Cobra venom mainly contains neurotoxins, which block signals between nerves and muscles, causing drooping eyelids, difficulty swallowing and breathing, and eventually paralysis. Some venoms also destroy tissue around the bite. Antivenom, made from antibodies, can neutralize venom if given quickly."],["Spitting cobras","Spitting cobras have fangs with small openings facing forward. By squeezing their venom glands, they spray venom in a stream toward the eyes of an attacker, sometimes two meters or more. Venom in the eyes causes intense pain and can lead to blindness if not washed out quickly with water."],["King cobras","The king cobra can grow longer than 5 meters. It hunts other snakes, including pythons and venomous species. It is the only snake that builds a nest for its eggs, gathering leaves into a mound and guarding it. Its venom yield is large enough to kill an elephant."]],
k:[["Main genus","Naja"],["Hood support","Elongated neck ribs"],["Venom type","Mainly neurotoxic"],["Philippine species","Philippine cobra, Samar cobra, king cobra"],["Treatment","Antivenom"]],
d:[["c. 1300 BC","Cobras appear on the crowns of Egyptian pharaohs"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the Indian cobra"],["1922","The Philippine cobra is described for science"],["1972","India’s Wildlife Protection Act restricts snake charming"],["2017","The World Health Organization lists snakebite as a neglected tropical disease"],["2024","Scientists propose splitting the king cobra into four species"]],
s2:[["The Philippine cobra","The Philippine cobra, Naja philippinensis, lives in northern islands such as Luzon, Mindoro and Masbate. It is a spitting cobra, and its venom is considered one of the most potent of any cobra. The Samar cobra lives in the southern and eastern islands, including Samar, Leyte and Mindanao. Cobra bites are a medical emergency, and victims should be taken immediately to a hospital that has antivenom."],["The Luzon king cobra","King cobras live from India to Southeast Asia, including the Philippines. In 2024 a study of their DNA and body features proposed dividing them into four species. One of these, Ophiophagus salvatana, lives in Luzon. The research suggests that each king cobra population may need its own conservation planning, though some scientists continue to examine the proposal."],["Snakebite","Snakebites kill tens of thousands of people each year, mostly in rural South Asia, Africa and Southeast Asia, and leave many more with disabilities. Farmers working in fields are at high risk. In 2017 the World Health Organization recognized snakebite as a neglected tropical disease and set goals to halve deaths and disabilities by 2030 by improving access to antivenom and treatment."],["First aid for snakebite","Health experts advise keeping a snakebite victim calm and still, immobilizing the bitten limb and getting to a hospital as quickly as possible. Traditional remedies such as cutting the wound, sucking out venom, applying tight tourniquets or using herbs are not recommended and can cause harm. For venom in the eyes, rinse with plenty of clean water."],["Cobras in culture","In ancient Egypt, the rearing cobra, or uraeus, was a symbol of royalty and protection worn on pharaohs’ crowns. In Hinduism, cobras called nagas are revered, and the festival of Nag Panchami honors them. In Buddhism, a cobra king named Mucalinda is said to have sheltered the Buddha from rain. Snake charmers in South Asia performed with cobras, though this is now restricted by animal protection laws."],["Mongooses and cobras","Mongooses are famous for fighting cobras. They are fast and agile, and have changes in their nerve receptors that make them partly resistant to cobra venom. Rudyard Kipling’s story Rikki-Tikki-Tavi, about a mongoose that defends a family from cobras, made this rivalry famous."]],
sp:["Four kings instead of one","For a long time, scientists thought the king cobra was a single species stretching from India to the Philippines. Researchers led by Indian herpetologist P. Gowri Shankar spent more than a decade examining museum specimens and DNA from snakes across Asia. They noticed differences in the bands on the snakes’ bodies, in their scales and in their genes. In 2024 they published a study proposing four species: one in India’s Western Ghats, one in northern South and Southeast Asia, one in the Sunda region, and one in Luzon. The Luzon king cobra was named Ophiophagus salvatana. The study showed that even famous animals can still hold surprises."],
g:[["Hood","The flared neck of a cobra"],["Neurotoxin","A poison that affects nerves"],["Antivenom","A medicine that neutralizes venom"],["Uraeus","The cobra symbol of Egyptian royalty"]],
f:["Some cobras spit venom.","King cobras eat other snakes.","The Philippine cobra is a spitting cobra."],
fun:"The king cobra is the only snake known to build a nest for its eggs.",
tl:"Ang kobra ay makamandag na ahas na nagbubuka ng leeg na parang talukbong kapag nanganganib. Kayang dumura ng kamandag ng Philippine cobra sa mata ng kalaban. Ang king cobra ang pinakamahabang makamandag na ahas sa mundo."});

;
/* data/animals18.js */
// Animal Kingdom: monitor lizards, squid, tuna, scorpions and wasps.
ADDART({id:"monitorlizards",t:"Monitor Lizards",c:"Reptiles",i:"🦎",w:"Monitor lizard",r:["lizard","komodo","phbio","geckos","croc"],
p:[["Big, smart lizards","Monitor lizards are large, active and intelligent reptiles."],["A common sight","The water monitor, or bayawak, is common in the Philippines."],["Fruit-eating monitors","The Philippines has rare monitors that eat fruit."]],
l:"Monitor lizards are a group of large lizards of the genus Varanus, found in Africa, Asia and Australia. There are around 80 species, ranging from small monitors about 20 centimeters long to the Komodo dragon of Indonesia, the largest living lizard. Monitors have long necks, powerful tails and claws, and forked tongues that they flick constantly to smell their surroundings. Most are active hunters and scavengers, eating insects, crabs, fish, birds, eggs and carrion, and they are considered among the most intelligent reptiles. The Philippines has several monitor species, including the widespread water monitor, known as bayawak, and three remarkable fruit-eating monitors found only in the country, among them the Northern Sierra Madre forest monitor, described in 2010.",
s:[["Forked tongues","Like snakes, monitor lizards flick their long, forked tongues in and out, picking up scent particles and delivering them to a special smell organ in the roof of the mouth. The two tips help them tell which direction a smell comes from, so they can track prey or carrion over long distances."],["Active lifestyle","Monitors have more efficient lungs and hearts than most lizards, allowing them to stay active longer. Many are strong swimmers, climbers or diggers. Water monitors can stay underwater for many minutes and are often seen swimming in rivers, mangroves and even city canals in Southeast Asia."],["Intelligence","In zoos, monitors have learned to recognize their keepers, solve simple puzzles to get food and count small numbers of prey. Some can tell how many snails they have been offered. Their curiosity and problem-solving have surprised scientists who once assumed reptiles were simple creatures."],["Venom glands","Scientists have found venom glands in the mouths of some monitors, including the Komodo dragon. Their saliva may contain substances that interfere with blood clotting. However, how important venom is in their hunting is still debated."]],
k:[["Genus","Varanus"],["Species","About 80"],["Largest","Komodo dragon"],["Filipino name","Bayawak"],["Philippine fruit-eaters","Gray’s monitor, Panay monitor, Sierra Madre monitor"]],
d:[["1845","Gray’s monitor of Luzon is described"],["1912","The Komodo dragon is described for science"],["2001","The fruit-eating Panay monitor is described"],["2010","The Northern Sierra Madre forest monitor is described"],["2010s","Studies reveal several new monitor species in the Philippines"],["2020s","Conservationists work to protect fruit-eating monitors"]],
s2:[["The bayawak","The water monitor and its close Philippine relatives are known as bayawak. They live near rivers, mangroves, farms and towns across the country, feeding on fish, crabs, frogs, birds and chicken eggs, which sometimes makes them unpopular with farmers. In some areas they are hunted for meat and skins, and their meat is eaten in traditional dishes."],["Butaan","Gray’s monitor, called butaan, lives in the forests of southern Luzon and nearby islands. It is unusual because it eats mainly fruit, especially pandanus fruits, as well as snails. It spends much of its time in trees and is rarely seen. Butaan are hunted for food and threatened by forest loss."],["A new giant lizard","In 2010 scientists announced a new species of large monitor lizard from the forests of the Northern Sierra Madre in Luzon, Varanus bitatawa. Growing about 2 meters long, it eats fruit and lives in trees. The Agta people and other local communities had long known and hunted the lizard, but scientists had overlooked it. Its discovery showed how much remains unknown about Philippine wildlife."],["The Panay monitor","The Panay monitor, described in 2001, lives in the remaining forests of Panay. It is dark with a bluish tinge and eats mainly fruit. It is endangered because the island’s forests have been heavily cleared. Its relatives in Luzon suggest that fruit-eating monitors evolved in the Philippines."],["Monitors in Asia","In countries such as Thailand, Malaysia and Indonesia, large water monitors are common in cities, swimming in canals and lounging on riverbanks. In Bangkok’s Lumpini Park, many monitors live among joggers and visitors. They help clean up waste by eating dead animals and leftovers."],["Leather and trade","Monitor lizard skins are used to make leather goods, and large numbers are exported from parts of Asia and Africa, under regulations meant to keep harvests sustainable. Some species are also traded as pets. Conservationists monitor the trade to prevent overexploitation, especially of rare species."]],
sp:["The lizard scientists missed","For years, biologists working in northern Luzon heard local hunters describe a huge, colorful lizard that lived high in the forest trees and ate fruit. In 2009 researchers obtained a specimen from Agta hunters and, after comparing it with other monitors and studying its DNA, realized it was a new species. Announced in 2010, Varanus bitatawa was nearly two meters long, with black and gold patterns and a bright blue tinge. It was surprising that such a large animal had escaped scientific notice, especially on Luzon, one of the most-studied islands in the Philippines. The discovery highlighted the knowledge of indigenous communities and the importance of protecting the Sierra Madre forests."],
g:[["Monitor lizard","A large lizard of the genus Varanus"],["Forked tongue","A tongue split at the tip used for smelling"],["Carrion","The flesh of dead animals"],["Bayawak","The Filipino name for monitor lizards"]],
f:["The Komodo dragon is a monitor lizard.","Some Philippine monitors eat fruit.","A new monitor species was described in 2010."],
fun:"Some monitor lizards in zoos have learned to count how many snails they were given.",
tl:"Ang bayawak ay malalaki at matatalinong butiki na tinatawag na monitor lizard. May mga bihirang bayawak sa Pilipinas na kumakain ng prutas. Isa rito ang Northern Sierra Madre forest monitor na natuklasan ng agham noong 2010."});

ADDART({id:"squid",t:"Squid",c:"Sea Life",i:"🦑",w:"Squid",r:["octopus","mollusc","sea","trench","phfood"],
p:[["Jet-powered","Squid swim by shooting water out of a tube."],["Giants of the deep","The giant and colossal squid are among the largest invertebrates."],["Pusit","Squid, or pusit, is a favorite food in the Philippines."]],
l:"Squid are fast-swimming marine animals related to octopuses and cuttlefish, belonging to a group of mollusks called cephalopods. There are around 300 species, from tiny bobtail squid a few centimeters long to the giant squid and colossal squid of the deep sea, among the largest invertebrates on Earth. Squid have eight arms and two longer feeding tentacles with suckers, large eyes, three hearts and blue blood. They move by jet propulsion, forcing water out of a funnel, and can change color in an instant using special skin cells. Many squeeze out clouds of ink to escape predators. Squid are important prey for whales, seals and fish, and a major seafood worldwide, including in the Philippines, where pusit is cooked in many dishes.",
s:[["Body plan","A squid’s long, streamlined body, called the mantle, contains its organs. Fins on the mantle help steer. Inside is a thin, feather-shaped internal shell called the pen, a remnant of the shells of its ancestors. Its arms surround a sharp, parrot-like beak used to tear prey."],["Jet propulsion","Squid draw water into the mantle and then squeeze it out through a movable funnel, shooting themselves backward or forward. Some squid can move so fast that they leap out of the water and glide through the air for short distances."],["Changing colors","Squid skin contains thousands of color cells called chromatophores, each controlled by muscles and nerves. By expanding or shrinking them, a squid can change color and pattern in a fraction of a second, to camouflage itself, communicate or startle predators."],["Light and ink","Many deep-sea squid produce light through bioluminescence, using it to camouflage their silhouettes or signal. The Hawaiian bobtail squid has a special organ filled with glowing bacteria. Squid also release ink, which can form a dark cloud or a blob shaped like a squid to confuse predators."]],
k:[["Group","Cephalopods"],["Species","About 300"],["Hearts","Three"],["Blood color","Blue"],["Heaviest","Colossal squid"]],
d:[["1857","The giant squid genus Architeuthis is named"],["1952","Studies of squid nerves explain how nerve signals work"],["2004","The first photographs of a live giant squid in the wild are taken"],["2007","A colossal squid of about 495 kg is caught near Antarctica"],["2012","A giant squid is filmed alive in its deep-sea habitat"],["2025","A colossal squid is filmed alive in the deep sea for the first time"]],
s2:[["The giant squid","The giant squid can reach perhaps 12 meters or more including its tentacles, and has the largest eyes of any animal, about the size of dinner plates. For centuries, it was known only from carcasses washed ashore or found in whale stomachs, and it may have inspired legends of the kraken. In 2004 Japanese scientists photographed one alive for the first time, and in 2012 a team filmed one in its deep-sea home."],["The colossal squid","The colossal squid of the Southern Ocean is shorter than the giant squid but much heavier, with hooks as well as suckers on its arms. A specimen caught in 2007 weighed about 495 kilograms. In 2025 scientists aboard an ocean research vessel filmed a young colossal squid alive in the deep sea for the first time."],["Squid and science","Some squid have giant nerve fibers, up to a millimeter thick, that make them escape quickly. In the 1940s and 1950s, British scientists Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley used these giant nerves to discover how electrical signals travel along nerves. They shared the 1963 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Squid remain important animals in neuroscience research."],["Squid in Filipino food","Squid, or pusit, is popular in Filipino cooking, prepared as adobong pusit cooked in its own ink, grilled inihaw na pusit, stuffed squid and fried calamares, a favorite street food. Dried squid is also widely sold. Small squid called lukot and baby squid are eaten in many coastal areas."],["Squid fishing","Many squid are caught at night by boats using bright lights to attract them to the surface, a method used in the Philippines, Japan, Peru and elsewhere. Large lighted fishing fleets can be seen from space. Scientists and governments monitor squid catches because their populations can rise and fall quickly with ocean conditions."],["Humboldt squid","The Humboldt squid of the eastern Pacific grows up to about 2 meters and hunts in large groups. Fishermen call it the red devil because it flashes red and white and can be aggressive. It is one of the most heavily fished squid in the world, especially off Peru and Mexico."]],
sp:["Photographing a legend","For years, Japanese zoologist Tsunemi Kubodera and whale expert Kyoichi Mori searched for a way to see a living giant squid. They knew sperm whales hunted squid near the Ogasawara Islands south of Tokyo, so they lowered a camera and bait on a long line to about 900 meters deep. On September 30, 2004, a giant squid attacked the bait, and the camera snapped hundreds of photographs as the animal struggled for hours before breaking free, leaving one of its tentacles behind. The images, published in 2005, were the first of a giant squid alive in its natural habitat. In 2012 Kubodera and colleagues filmed one from a submersible, revealing a shimmering silver and gold animal."],
g:[["Cephalopod","A mollusk with arms, such as a squid or octopus"],["Mantle","The main body of a squid"],["Chromatophore","A color-changing skin cell"],["Pusit","The Filipino word for squid"]],
f:["Squid have three hearts.","Giant squid have the largest eyes of any animal.","Squid swim by jet propulsion."],
fun:"Some squid can leap out of the water and glide through the air.",
tl:"Ang pusit ay mabilis lumangoy na hayop-dagat na kamag-anak ng pugita. May tatlong puso ito at asul na dugo. Paborito itong pagkain ng mga Pilipino, gaya ng adobong pusit at calamares."});

ADDART({id:"tuna",t:"Tuna",c:"Sea Life",i:"🐟",w:"Tuna",r:["fishes","sea","pacific","phfood","mindanao"],
p:[["Ocean racers","Tuna are among the fastest fish in the sea."],["Warm-blooded fish","Some tuna keep parts of their bodies warmer than the water."],["General Santos","General Santos City is known as the tuna capital of the Philippines."]],
l:"Tuna are large, fast-swimming fish of the mackerel family found in warm and temperate oceans around the world. There are about 15 species, including skipjack, yellowfin, bigeye, albacore and the giant bluefin tunas, which can grow over 3 meters long and weigh more than 600 kilograms. Tuna have torpedo-shaped bodies and can swim at high speeds, crossing entire oceans on migrations. Unlike most fish, some tuna can keep their muscles, eyes and brains warmer than the surrounding water. Tuna are among the most valuable fish in the world, eaten fresh as sashimi and sushi, grilled or canned. The Philippines is an important tuna-fishing country, and General Santos City in Mindanao is famous as its tuna capital.",
s:[["Built for speed","Tuna have smooth, streamlined bodies, stiff crescent-shaped tails and fins that fold into grooves to reduce drag. Small finlets near the tail help control water flow. Some species can reach bursts of around 70 kilometers per hour."],["Warm muscles","Bluefin tuna and some relatives have a system of blood vessels called a countercurrent heat exchanger, which keeps heat produced by their muscles from escaping through the gills. This lets them hunt in cold waters and dive deep while staying active, a rare ability among fish."],["Never stopping","Many tuna must keep swimming to breathe, because water must flow over their gills as they move, a method called ram ventilation. They swim continuously throughout their lives, even while resting."],["Species","Skipjack tuna, the smallest of the commercial species, are the most commonly canned. Yellowfin and bigeye are important for fresh and frozen markets. Atlantic, Pacific and southern bluefin tunas are the largest and most prized for sushi and sashimi."]],
k:[["Family","Mackerels and tunas"],["Species","About 15"],["Largest","Atlantic bluefin tuna"],["Most canned","Skipjack"],["Philippine tuna capital","General Santos City"]],
d:[["1969","An international commission to manage Atlantic tuna is founded"],["1998","The General Santos fish port complex opens"],["2004","A commission to manage western and central Pacific tuna is created"],["2019","A bluefin tuna sells for a record price at a Tokyo auction"],["2021","Some bluefin tuna are downgraded from endangered as stocks recover"],["2020s","General Santos continues to celebrate its yearly Tuna Festival"]],
s2:[["General Santos City","General Santos City on Sarangani Bay in southern Mindanao is home to one of the largest tuna landing ports in Asia. Each day, boats unload yellowfin, skipjack and other tuna, which are sold at auction, processed in canneries or exported fresh to Japan and other countries. Tuna fishing and processing provide many jobs, and the city celebrates its Tuna Festival every September."],["Handline fishing","Many Filipino fishers catch large yellowfin tuna using handlines, a traditional method in which a single baited hook on a long line is lowered from a small boat. Handline fishing is considered more selective and sustainable than large nets. Some Philippine handline-caught tuna has received sustainability certification."],["Overfishing","Demand for tuna has led to heavy fishing pressure. Atlantic and Pacific bluefin tuna were badly overfished in the late 20th century. International commissions set catch limits, and enforcement has improved. In 2021 scientists reported that Atlantic bluefin had recovered enough to be listed as of least concern, although some stocks and other tuna species remain under pressure."],["Record prices","At the first auction of the year at Tokyo’s fish market, restaurant owners compete to buy the best bluefin tuna, sometimes paying enormous sums for publicity. In 2019 a 278-kilogram bluefin sold for about 333 million yen, a record. Such prices are unusual, but they show how valuable bluefin are."],["Tuna and mercury","Large, long-lived tuna can accumulate mercury from their food. Health agencies advise that most people can safely eat tuna as part of a balanced diet, but recommend that pregnant women and young children limit their intake of large tuna species such as bigeye and bluefin, choosing lighter, smaller species such as skipjack instead."],["Dolphin-safe debate","In parts of the Pacific, tuna often swim beneath dolphins, and fishing nets once killed many dolphins. Campaigns in the 1980s and 1990s led to dolphin-safe labels and changes in fishing methods. Debate continues over how well different labels protect dolphins, sharks, turtles and other animals caught accidentally."]],
sp:["Dawn at the fish port","Before dawn at the fish port in General Santos City, boats crowd the docks, their decks loaded with tuna caught days earlier in the waters of Mindanao and beyond. Workers in rubber boots haul huge yellowfin, some taller than a person, onto the concrete floor. Inspectors check each fish, cutting a small sample from the tail to judge its color and fat. Buyers move between rows of fish, bidding for the best ones to export fresh to Japan for sashimi, while others are sent to canneries. By midmorning, the port is washed down, ready for the next catch. For the city, tuna is not only food but the heart of its economy and identity."],
g:[["Tuna","A large, fast ocean fish"],["Ram ventilation","Breathing by swimming with the mouth open"],["Handline","A single line and hook used for fishing"],["Sashimi","Thinly sliced raw fish"]],
f:["Some tuna keep their muscles warm.","General Santos is the Philippine tuna capital.","Skipjack is the most commonly canned tuna."],
fun:"A single bluefin tuna once sold for more than 300 million yen at a Tokyo auction.",
tl:"Ang tuna ay malalaki at mabibilis na isda sa karagatan. Kaya ng ilang tuna na panatilihing mainit ang kanilang kalamnan. Ang General Santos City ay kilala bilang kapitolyo ng tuna sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"scorpions",t:"Scorpions",c:"Insects",i:"🦂",w:"Scorpion",r:["spider","insect","cactus","constellations","firstaid"],
p:[["Ancient arachnids","Scorpions are relatives of spiders that have existed for over 400 million years."],["Glow in the dark","Scorpions glow under ultraviolet light."],["Stinging tails","They have a venomous stinger at the tip of their tails."]],
l:"Scorpions are arachnids, relatives of spiders, ticks and mites, with eight legs, a pair of grasping pincers and a segmented tail that curves over the back and ends in a venomous stinger. There are over 2,500 species, found on every continent except Antarctica, especially in deserts and tropical forests. Scorpions are mostly nocturnal predators of insects, spiders and other small animals. They are among the oldest land animals, with fossils more than 400 million years old. Most scorpion stings are painful but not dangerous to people, though a few dozen species have venom that can be deadly, especially to children. Under ultraviolet light, scorpions glow a vivid blue-green. Mother scorpions give birth to live young and carry them on their backs.",
s:[["Body","A scorpion has a front section with its eyes, mouthparts, pincers and legs, and a long tail made of segments ending in a bulb with venom glands and a stinger. It has two eyes on top and several smaller ones on the sides, but sees poorly and relies mainly on touch and vibrations."],["Hunting","Scorpions wait in burrows or under rocks and sense prey through tiny hairs and comb-like organs underneath their bodies, which detect vibrations and textures. They seize prey with their pincers and use the stinger when needed. Species with large pincers often rely less on venom."],["Glowing scorpions","A substance in the outer layer of a scorpion’s exoskeleton makes it glow blue-green under ultraviolet light, such as moonlight or a black light. Scientists are not sure why. One idea is that it helps scorpions sense light and decide when to hide. Researchers use UV lamps to find scorpions at night."],["Babies on board","Scorpions give birth to live young, which climb onto their mother’s back and ride there until after their first molt. The mother protects them during this vulnerable time. Some scorpions can live for several years, a long time for an invertebrate."]],
k:[["Group","Arachnids"],["Species","Over 2,500"],["Oldest fossils","More than 400 million years old"],["Dangerous species","A few dozen"],["Special feature","Glows under ultraviolet light"]],
d:[["c. 435 million years ago","Early scorpions live in ancient seas and shores"],["Ancient times","Babylonians and Greeks name the constellation Scorpius"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names several scorpion species"],["1993","Scientists isolate chlorotoxin from scorpion venom"],["2010s","Scorpion-based tumor paint is tested to help surgeons see cancer"],["2020s","Scorpion stings rise in parts of Brazil as cities grow"]],
s2:[["Dangerous scorpions","Most deadly scorpions belong to the family Buthidae, such as the deathstalker of North Africa and the Middle East and the Brazilian yellow scorpion. In countries such as Mexico, Brazil, India and parts of North Africa, scorpion stings cause many emergency cases and some deaths each year, mostly among children. Antivenoms and better medical care have reduced deaths."],["Scorpions in the Philippines","The Philippines has several scorpion species, including large dark forest scorpions. Their stings are painful but generally not life-threatening for healthy adults. People who are stung should clean the wound, apply a cold pack and seek medical care, especially for children or anyone with severe symptoms."],["Medicines from venom","Scientists are studying scorpion venom for medical uses. Chlorotoxin, a substance from the deathstalker scorpion, sticks to certain cancer cells. Researchers attached glowing dyes to a version of it to create tumor paint, which helps surgeons see tumors during operations. Other venom compounds are being studied for pain and immune disorders."],["Scorpions in culture","The constellation Scorpius has been seen as a scorpion since ancient times. In Greek myth, a scorpion killed the hunter Orion, and the two were placed on opposite sides of the sky so they never appear together. In ancient Egypt, the goddess Serket was associated with scorpions and protection against venom."],["Desert survivors","Scorpions can survive extreme conditions. Some can go a year without eating by slowing their metabolism. Others survive freezing and thawing. Their waxy exoskeletons reduce water loss in hot deserts. Scientists have even found that some scorpions can survive being submerged in water for many hours."],["Eating scorpions","In parts of China and Southeast Asia, fried scorpions are sold as snacks, and scorpions are used in some traditional medicines. Their stingers are usually removed or the venom destroyed by cooking. Eating insects and arachnids, called entomophagy, is promoted by some as a sustainable source of protein."]],
sp:["Scorpion versus Orion","In Greek mythology, Orion was a giant hunter who boasted that he could kill every animal on Earth. To stop him, the Earth goddess Gaia, or in some versions the goddess Artemis, sent a giant scorpion, which stung Orion and killed him. Zeus placed both in the sky as constellations. Because they were enemies, they were placed on opposite sides of the heavens: when Scorpius rises in the east, Orion sets in the west, as if fleeing. From the Philippines, Scorpius, with its bright red star Antares, can be seen in the evening sky from about May to August, while Orion dominates the sky from December to March."],
g:[["Arachnid","An eight-legged animal such as a spider or scorpion"],["Pincers","Grasping claws"],["Stinger","A sharp organ that injects venom"],["Exoskeleton","A hard outer skeleton"]],
f:["Scorpions glow under ultraviolet light.","Most scorpion stings are not deadly.","Scorpion mothers carry their babies on their backs."],
fun:"Some scorpions can survive a year without eating.",
tl:"Ang alakdan ay kamag-anak ng gagamba na may kamandag sa dulo ng buntot. Kumikinang ito kapag natamaan ng ultraviolet na liwanag. Karamihan sa kagat nito ay masakit ngunit hindi nakamamatay."});

ADDART({id:"wasps",t:"Wasps and Hornets",c:"Insects",i:"🐝",w:"Wasp",r:["bee","ants","insect","flower","agri"],
p:[["Not just stingers","Most wasps are harmless to people and help control pests."],["Paper makers","Paper wasps and hornets build nests from chewed wood."],["Tiny helpers","Many tiny wasps are used to protect crops naturally."]],
l:"Wasps are a huge and varied group of insects related to bees and ants. Scientists have described over 100,000 species, and many more remain undiscovered, making wasps one of the most diverse animal groups on Earth. The familiar social wasps, such as paper wasps, yellowjackets and hornets, live in colonies with a queen and workers and can sting to defend their nests. But most wasps are solitary, and many are parasitoids that lay eggs in or on other insects, eventually killing them. This makes wasps important natural controllers of pests, and farmers use them in biological pest control. Wasps also pollinate many plants, including figs, which depend entirely on tiny fig wasps. In the Philippines, wasps are called putakti.",
s:[["Social wasps","Social wasps live in nests with a queen that lays eggs and workers that build, gather food and defend the colony. In temperate countries, colonies die off in winter, and only new queens survive. In tropical places such as the Philippines, some colonies can last much longer."],["Paper nests","Paper wasps and hornets chew wood fibers from dead trees or fences and mix them with saliva to make a papery material. They build nests of six-sided cells, some hanging under roofs or branches and others hidden in the ground or in hollow trees. In 1719 the French scientist René-Antoine de Réaumur suggested that people could make paper from wood, as wasps do."],["Parasitoid wasps","Most wasp species are parasitoids. A female lays her eggs on or inside a caterpillar, beetle larva, aphid or spider, and the young wasps eat the host from within. Some are so tiny they lay eggs inside the eggs of other insects. Although gruesome, these wasps keep pest populations in check."],["Stings","Only female wasps can sting, because the stinger is a modified egg-laying tube. Unlike honeybees, most wasps can sting more than once. Stings are painful, and for people with severe allergies they can be dangerous. Large numbers of stings from disturbed nests can also be serious."]],
k:[["Order","Hymenoptera, with bees and ants"],["Described species","Over 100,000"],["Most species","Solitary parasitoids"],["Nest material","Chewed wood paper"],["Filipino name","Putakti"]],
d:[["c. 200 million years ago","Early wasps appear"],["1719","Réaumur suggests making paper from wood, inspired by wasps"],["Late 1800s","Natural enemies of pests begin to be used deliberately to protect crops"],["2019","Asian giant hornets are found in North America"],["2020","The first Asian giant hornet nest in the United States is destroyed"],["2024","Asian giant hornets are declared eradicated from the United States"]],
s2:[["Fig wasps","Every species of fig tree depends on its own species of tiny fig wasp for pollination. Female wasps squeeze into a fig, which is actually a hollow cluster of flowers, pollinating it and laying eggs inside. Many fig species are important food for birds, bats and monkeys in tropical forests, including the balete and other figs of the Philippines."],["Protecting crops","Farmers and scientists release parasitoid wasps to control pests such as corn borers, whiteflies and caterpillars instead of, or alongside, chemical pesticides. In the Philippines, the Department of Agriculture has used Trichogramma wasps, which lay eggs inside pest moth eggs, to protect corn and sugarcane. This biological control can reduce pesticide use."],["Asian giant hornets","The Asian giant hornet, the world’s largest hornet, can attack and destroy honeybee hives. In 2019 it was found in British Columbia in Canada and Washington State in the United States, probably accidentally brought by ships. Scientists and volunteers tracked hornets with tiny radio tags to find and destroy their nests, and in 2024 officials declared the species eradicated from the United States."],["Wasps and people","Many people fear wasps because of their stings, especially when wasps gather around food and drinks. Experts advise staying calm, not swatting wasps, covering sweet foods and calling professionals to remove nests near homes. They note that wasps are valuable in gardens because they eat many pest insects."],["Strange wasps","Some wasps have remarkable behaviors. The emerald cockroach wasp stings a cockroach in the brain, then leads it to a burrow like a dog on a leash to feed her young. Tarantula hawk wasps hunt huge spiders, and their sting is considered one of the most painful insect stings."],["Wasps in the Philippines","Putakti and other wasps are common in Philippine gardens, farms and forests. Large hornets and paper wasps sometimes build nests under roofs and in trees. Like elsewhere, many tiny wasps in the Philippines quietly help control crop pests."]],
sp:["Tracking the giant hornets","In late 2020, scientists in Washington State faced a challenge: find the nest of Asian giant hornets before new queens could escape and spread. Entomologists captured live hornets using traps baited with sweet liquids. Then, using dental floss, they carefully tied tiny radio transmitters to the hornets’ waists and released them. Following the signals through forests with antennas, they tracked one hornet to a hollow tree near the town of Blaine. Wearing thick protective suits, they vacuumed out the hornets and removed the nest, which contained hundreds of hornets and developing queens. Similar efforts continued until 2024, when the hornet was declared eradicated from the United States."],
g:[["Parasitoid","An insect that develops inside or on a host, killing it"],["Colony","A group of insects living together"],["Biological control","Using living things to control pests"],["Putakti","The Filipino word for wasp"]],
f:["Only female wasps can sting.","Fig trees depend on fig wasps.","Paper wasps make nests from chewed wood."],
fun:"Some parasitoid wasps are so small they lay their eggs inside the eggs of other insects.",
tl:"Ang putakti ay insektong kamag-anak ng bubuyog at langgam. Karamihan sa mga ito ay hindi nakakasama sa tao at tumutulong pa sa pagkontrol ng mga peste. Ginagamit ng mga magsasaka ang maliliit na putakti upang protektahan ang mga pananim."});

;
/* data/plants3.js */
// Plants: sunflowers, palms, conifers, climbing plants and poisonous plants.
ADDART({id:"sunflowers",t:"Sunflowers",c:"Plants",i:"🌻",w:"Common sunflower",r:["flower","seeds","fib","agri","fruits"],
p:[["Following the Sun","Young sunflowers turn to follow the Sun across the sky."],["Thousands of flowers","A sunflower head is made of many tiny flowers."],["Seeds and oil","Sunflowers are grown for seeds and cooking oil."]],
l:"The common sunflower, Helianthus annuus, is a tall flowering plant native to North America, famous for its large yellow flower heads. It was domesticated by Native Americans thousands of years ago and later spread worldwide. What looks like a single sunflower is actually a head made of hundreds to over a thousand tiny flowers, arranged in spirals that follow a mathematical pattern linked to the Fibonacci sequence. Young sunflowers turn to follow the Sun during the day, while mature ones face east. Sunflowers are grown for their edible seeds and for sunflower oil, one of the world’s most important cooking oils, with Russia and Ukraine as top producers. They have also been used to clean polluted soil and have inspired famous paintings.",
s:[["One head, many flowers","A sunflower is a composite flower. The yellow petals around the edge belong to ray flowers, which attract insects. The center is packed with disk flowers, each able to produce a seed. Bees and other insects pollinate them, moving from the outer disk flowers inward over several days."],["Spirals in the seeds","Sunflower seeds are arranged in two sets of interlocking spirals, one curving clockwise and the other counterclockwise. The numbers of spirals are often consecutive Fibonacci numbers, such as 34 and 55. This arrangement packs the seeds efficiently, leaving almost no gaps."],["Tracking the Sun","Young sunflower plants turn from east to west during the day following the Sun and swing back to face east at night, a behavior called heliotropism. A 2016 study showed this is controlled by an internal clock. When mature, most stop moving and face east, which warms the flowers in the morning and attracts more bees."],["Sunflower farming","Sunflowers grow quickly and tolerate dry conditions. Farmers grow varieties for oil, whose seeds are small and black, and for snacks, whose seeds are larger and striped. Sunflower meal left after pressing oil is fed to livestock. In the Philippines, sunflowers are grown in gardens and on some farms, and sunflower fields have become popular tourist spots."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Helianthus annuus"],["Native to","North America"],["Flower type","Composite of many small flowers"],["Top oil producers","Russia and Ukraine"],["Tallest recorded","Over 9 meters"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Native Americans domesticate sunflowers"],["1500s","Spanish explorers bring sunflowers to Europe"],["1716","An English patent describes pressing oil from sunflower seeds"],["1830s","Large-scale sunflower oil production begins in Russia"],["1888","Vincent van Gogh paints his famous Sunflowers series"],["2016","Scientists explain how sunflowers track the Sun"]],
s2:[["Van Gogh’s sunflowers","In 1888 and 1889, the Dutch painter Vincent van Gogh painted a series of still lifes of sunflowers in vases in Arles, France, to decorate the room of his friend Paul Gauguin. With their bold yellows, they became some of the most famous paintings in the world. One sold in 1987 for a then-record price."],["Ukraine’s flower","The sunflower is a national symbol of Ukraine, where vast fields cover the countryside and sunflower oil is a major export. After Russia’s full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, the sunflower became a worldwide symbol of support for Ukraine. The war disrupted exports and caused sunflower oil prices to rise in many countries."],["Cleaning polluted soil","Sunflowers can absorb some heavy metals and radioactive substances from soil and water through their roots, a process called phytoremediation. After the 1986 Chernobyl nuclear disaster, sunflowers were tested on floating rafts to remove radioactive materials from a pond. Sunflowers were also planted in Japan after the 2011 Fukushima disaster, though their effectiveness there was limited."],["A native American crop","Archaeological evidence shows that Native Americans grew sunflowers in what is now the eastern United States and Mexico thousands of years ago, using the seeds for food, oil, dye and medicine. Sunflowers are one of the few major crops domesticated in North America."],["Sunflowers and bees","Sunflowers produce plenty of nectar and pollen, making them valuable for honeybees and wild bees. Farmers sometimes place beehives near sunflower fields to improve seed production, while beekeepers get sunflower honey, which is golden and crystallizes quickly."],["Giant sunflowers","Some sunflower varieties can grow extremely tall. In 2014 a German gardener grew a sunflower over 9 meters tall, a world record. Sunflower-growing contests are popular in schools and communities, teaching children about plants."]],
sp:["The flower that follows the Sun","Scientists had long noticed that young sunflowers seem to watch the Sun, but they did not know how. In 2016 researchers at the University of California, Davis, studied sunflowers in fields and in growth chambers. They found that during the day, cells on the east side of the stem grow faster, bending the plant westward to follow the Sun. At night, the west side grows faster, swinging the head back to face east. The plants used an internal clock, much like the body clock in humans. The team also found that east-facing mature flowers warm up faster in the morning and receive several times more visits from bees than flowers turned west."],
g:[["Composite flower","A head made of many small flowers"],["Heliotropism","Turning to follow the Sun"],["Phytoremediation","Using plants to clean polluted land"],["Disk flower","A tiny flower in the center of a sunflower"]],
f:["A sunflower head contains many tiny flowers.","Young sunflowers follow the Sun.","Sunflower seeds form Fibonacci spirals."],
fun:"The record sunflower grew taller than a three-story building.",
tl:"Ang sunflower o mirasol ay matangkad na halamang may malaking dilaw na bulaklak. Sinusundan ng mga batang sunflower ang Araw. Ginagamit ang buto nito bilang pagkain at mantika."});

ADDART({id:"palms",t:"Palm Trees",c:"Plants",i:"🌴",w:"Arecaceae",r:["coconut","trees","deforestation","phfood","weaving"],
p:[["Not true trees","Palms are more closely related to grasses than to oaks."],["Useful plants","Palms give us coconuts, dates, palm oil and rattan."],["Philippine palms","Nipa, buri and anahaw are important palms in the Philippines."]],
l:"Palms are flowering plants of the family Arecaceae, with about 2,600 species found mostly in tropical and subtropical regions. Most have a single unbranched trunk topped by a crown of large leaves, either feather-shaped or fan-shaped. Botanically, palms are monocots, related to grasses and lilies, and their trunks lack true wood and growth rings. Palms are among the most useful plants to humans, providing coconuts, dates, palm oil, sago starch, betel nuts, rattan for furniture, and leaves for roofing and weaving. The Philippines has many native palms, including nipa, used for thatch, vinegar and drinks; buri, the country’s largest palm; and anahaw. The rapid spread of oil palm plantations in Southeast Asia has become a major environmental issue.",
s:[["How palms grow","Palms grow from a single bud at the top of the trunk. If this bud is damaged, the palm usually dies, because it cannot branch. Their trunks do not thicken much over time like other trees; instead, they reach their full width before growing tall. Bundles of tough fibers scattered through the trunk give them strength and flexibility in storms."],["Palm leaves","Palm leaves, called fronds, are either pinnate, like feathers, as in coconut and nipa, or palmate, like fans, as in anahaw. Some palm leaves are among the largest of any plant. People weave them into roofs, mats, baskets, hats and decorations."],["Fruits and seeds","Palms produce fruits ranging from dates and coconuts to the giant coco de mer, whose seed is the largest in the plant world. Many palm fruits are eaten by animals, which spread the seeds. Betel nut, from the areca palm, is chewed as a stimulant in parts of Asia, though it is linked to mouth cancer."],["Climbing palms","Rattans are climbing palms with long, thin, flexible stems covered in spines. They climb through forests using hooked whips. Their stems are harvested and made into furniture, baskets and canes. The Philippines, especially Cebu, is known for rattan furniture."]],
k:[["Family","Arecaceae"],["Species","About 2,600"],["Plant group","Monocots"],["Largest Philippine palm","Buri"],["Major crop palms","Coconut, oil palm, date palm"]],
d:[["c. 4000 BC","Date palms are cultivated in Mesopotamia"],["1848","Oil palms from Africa are planted in Java"],["1910s","Commercial oil palm plantations begin in Malaysia"],["2004","The Roundtable on Sustainable Palm Oil is founded"],["2010s","Palm oil becomes the world’s most produced vegetable oil"],["2020s","The European Union adopts rules on deforestation-free products, including palm oil"]],
s2:[["Nipa","Nipa palms grow in brackish water along rivers and mangroves. Their leaves are used for traditional nipa hut roofs, called bahay kubo. Sap from their flower stalks is fermented into tuba, a palm wine, and made into vinegar called sukang nipa, and distilled into a liquor called laksoy. Nipa swamps also protect riverbanks and coasts."],["Buri","The buri palm, also called the talipot or gebang palm, is the largest palm in the Philippines. It grows for decades, then produces one enormous flower cluster with millions of flowers and dies after fruiting. Its leaves are woven into mats, hats and bags, its fibers make raffia and its trunk yields starch. Buri weaving is an important craft in provinces such as Bohol and Quezon."],["Palm oil","Palm oil, from the fruit of the African oil palm, is used in many foods, soaps, cosmetics and biofuels. It is very productive, giving more oil per hectare than other oil crops. But the expansion of plantations in Indonesia and Malaysia has destroyed large areas of rainforest and peatland, threatening orangutans and releasing greenhouse gases. Producers argue that palm oil supports millions of small farmers and that switching to other oils would require even more land. Certification schemes aim to make production more sustainable, though critics say they have weaknesses."],["Palm Sunday","On Palm Sunday, which begins Holy Week, Filipino Catholics bring woven palm leaves, called palaspas, to church to be blessed, recalling the crowds who waved palm branches as Jesus entered Jerusalem. Many palaspas are skillfully woven from young coconut leaves into intricate shapes."],["Dates","The date palm has been cultivated in the Middle East and North Africa for thousands of years and is central to life in desert oases. Dates are rich in sugar and are traditionally eaten to break the fast during Ramadan. A 2,000-year-old date palm seed from Masada has even been sprouted into a living tree."],["Palms in cities","Palms are planted along streets and beaches in many tropical cities, including in the Philippines, for their decorative look. Some urban planners note that palms provide less shade and absorb less carbon than broad-leaved trees, and recommend mixing them with native shade trees."]],
sp:["The palm that flowers once","For 30 to 80 years, a buri palm grows quietly in a Philippine field, its huge fan-shaped leaves spreading over the land. Farmers harvest its leaves for weaving and collect sap. Then, one year, the palm begins its grand finale. From the top of its trunk rises a massive, branching flower cluster several meters tall, one of the largest of any plant, carrying millions of tiny cream-colored flowers. Bees and other insects swarm around it. Over the following months, the flowers become countless round fruits. Having used all its stored energy, the palm then slowly dies, leaving behind seeds that may grow into a new generation."],
g:[["Palm","A plant of the family Arecaceae"],["Frond","A large palm leaf"],["Monocot","A flowering plant group that includes grasses and palms"],["Rattan","A climbing palm used for furniture"]],
f:["Palms are monocots, not true trees.","The buri palm flowers once and dies.","Rattan is a climbing palm."],
fun:"A buri palm may wait decades to flower, then produce millions of flowers at once before dying.",
tl:"Ang palma ay pamilya ng halaman na kinabibilangan ng niyog, nipa, buri at anahaw. Ginagamit ang mga ito sa pagkain, bubong, paghahabi at muwebles. Ang buri ang pinakamalaking palma sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"conifers",t:"Conifers",c:"Plants",i:"🌲",w:"Conifer",r:["trees","giantrees","seeds","bohol","deforestation"],
p:[["Cone bearers","Conifers carry their seeds in cones."],["Needles and scales","Most have needle-like or scale-like leaves."],["City of Pines","Baguio is famous for its Benguet pines."]],
l:"Conifers are cone-bearing seed plants, including pines, spruces, firs, cedars, cypresses, junipers, redwoods and yews. There are about 600 species. Unlike flowering plants, they do not produce flowers or fruits; their seeds develop on the scales of cones. Most conifers are evergreen trees with needle-like or scale-like leaves that stay on the tree year-round. They dominate vast forests across the cold northern parts of North America, Europe and Asia and grow on mountains worldwide. Conifers include the tallest, largest and oldest trees on Earth. They provide most of the world’s timber and paper pulp. In the Philippines, native conifers include the Benguet pine of the Cordillera, which gave Baguio its nickname, the City of Pines, and the almaciga of Palawan.",
s:[["Cones and seeds","Most conifers have separate male and female cones. Small male cones release clouds of pollen carried by the wind. Larger female cones have scales that hold ovules, which become seeds after pollination. Some cones take two years to mature, then open to release winged seeds, while others need fire to open."],["Needles","Conifer needles are narrow, tough and coated in wax, which reduces water loss and helps them survive cold, dry winters and snow. Because needles stay on the tree for several years, conifers can begin making food as soon as spring arrives. A few conifers, such as larches, drop their needles in autumn."],["Resin","Conifers produce sticky resin that seals wounds and defends against insects and fungi. Resin from pines is used to make turpentine and rosin, used by violinists and athletes. Fossilized resin is amber, which sometimes preserves ancient insects."],["The boreal forest","The boreal forest, or taiga, is a huge belt of mostly conifer forest stretching across Canada, Alaska, Scandinavia and Russia. It is the largest land habitat on Earth and stores enormous amounts of carbon in trees and soils. Wildfires and warming temperatures are changing it rapidly."]],
k:[["Group","Gymnosperms"],["Species","About 600"],["Seeds held in","Cones"],["Philippine pine","Benguet pine"],["Tallest conifer","Coast redwood"]],
d:[["c. 300 million years ago","The first conifers appear"],["1600s","European navies prize tall pines for ship masts"],["1909","Baguio, the City of Pines, becomes a chartered city"],["1994","The Wollemi pine is discovered in Australia"],["2020","Firefighters save the wild Wollemi pines from bushfires"],["2020s","Bark beetles and fires damage conifer forests in many countries"]],
s2:[["Benguet pine and Baguio","The Benguet pine grows in the mountains of northern Luzon, especially the Cordillera Central. Its fragrant forests give Baguio City its cool, pine-scented atmosphere and its nickname, City of Pines. Pine cones are sold as souvenirs. The pines are threatened by urban growth, forest fires and pests, and Baguio has run tree-planting and protection programs."],["Almaciga","The almaciga, a native conifer of the genus Agathis, grows in the forests of Palawan and other islands. Indigenous peoples such as the Tagbanua and Batak tap its trunks for a resin known as Manila copal, used in varnishes, paints and incense. Resin collection provides income, though overtapping can harm trees, and communities have worked to manage it sustainably."],["The Wollemi pine","In 1994 a park ranger exploring a remote canyon in the Blue Mountains near Sydney, Australia, discovered a strange conifer known only from fossils. The Wollemi pine had survived as fewer than 100 wild trees. During the 2019 to 2020 bushfires, firefighters were flown in to protect the secret grove with water bombers and sprinklers, saving the trees."],["Christmas trees","In many countries, conifers such as firs, spruces and pines are used as Christmas trees, a custom that spread from Germany. In the Philippines, where real conifers are uncommon in lowlands, artificial trees and native alternatives are popular. Some groups promote reusable or locally made trees to reduce waste."],["Timber and paper","Conifers grow straight and produce softwood timber used for houses, furniture and paper. Pine plantations are grown around the world. Supporters say well-managed plantations reduce pressure on natural forests, while critics note that large monocultures support less wildlife than natural forests."],["Mindoro pine","Another native pine, the Mindoro pine, grows in scattered areas of Mindoro, Zambales and other places. It is adapted to fire and poor soils. Botanists consider the Philippines the meeting point of conifers from northern Asia and the southern hemisphere."]],
sp:["The dinosaur tree","In September 1994, David Noble, a field officer with the New South Wales parks service, was abseiling and exploring a narrow, deep canyon in Wollemi National Park when he noticed some trees with unusual bark that looked like bubbling chocolate. He took a branch to experts, who were astonished: the tree belonged to a family thought to have disappeared from the fossil record millions of years ago. Nicknamed the dinosaur tree, the Wollemi pine became a sensation. To protect the tiny wild population, its location was kept secret, and young trees grown from cuttings were sold worldwide to reduce the temptation to collect wild ones."],
g:[["Conifer","A cone-bearing seed plant"],["Gymnosperm","A plant with seeds not enclosed in fruits"],["Resin","A sticky substance produced by conifers"],["Taiga","The northern conifer forest"]],
f:["Conifers produce cones instead of flowers.","Baguio is called the City of Pines.","Amber is fossilized resin."],
fun:"The Wollemi pine was known only from fossils until it was found alive in 1994.",
tl:"Ang conifer ay mga halamang may cone tulad ng pino. Karaniwang hugis-karayom ang dahon nila at nananatili buong taon. Ang Benguet pine ang dahilan kung bakit tinaguriang City of Pines ang Baguio."});

ADDART({id:"vines",t:"Climbing Plants",c:"Plants",i:"🌿",w:"Vine",r:["trees","amazon","flower","invasiveplants","palms"],
p:[["Reaching for light","Vines climb other plants and structures to reach sunlight."],["Clever climbers","They twine, use tendrils, hooks or sticky roots."],["A turquoise treasure","The turquoise jade vine is native to the Philippines."]],
l:"Climbing plants, often called vines or creepers, are plants that grow upward by attaching to other plants, rocks or structures rather than supporting themselves with thick trunks. This allows them to reach sunlight with less investment in strong stems. They climb by twining around supports, grasping with tendrils, hooking with thorns or clinging with sticky roots or pads. Woody climbers called lianas are common in tropical rainforests, where they can stretch hundreds of meters through the canopy. Many important crops are climbers, including grapes, beans, peas, squash, passion fruit and ampalaya. The Philippines is home to the jade vine, with its striking turquoise flowers, and to many rattans. Some vines, such as kudzu, have become invasive pests.",
s:[["Twiners","Twining plants, such as morning glories, beans and sweet potatoes, wind their stems around a support as they grow. The growing tip slowly circles in the air until it touches something, then wraps around it. Each species usually twines in a particular direction."],["Tendrils","Plants such as peas, grapes, cucumbers and ampalaya produce thin, sensitive tendrils. When a tendril touches a support, it curls around it within minutes to hours, then coils like a spring, pulling the plant closer and absorbing movement in the wind."],["Hooks and roots","Rattans and roses use hooks and thorns to hold onto other plants. Ivy and some figs grow small roots or sticky pads that cling to walls and bark. Boston ivy can climb smooth walls using tiny adhesive discs."],["Lianas","In tropical forests, lianas start on the ground and climb into the canopy, sometimes connecting many trees. They provide food and pathways for animals such as monkeys and squirrels. Lianas compete with trees for light, and in some forests they have become more common as climates change."]],
k:[["Main climbing methods","Twining, tendrils, hooks, roots"],["Woody tropical climbers","Lianas"],["Philippine endemic","Jade vine"],["Famous invasive","Kudzu"],["Crop examples","Grapes, beans, ampalaya"]],
d:[["c. 6000 BC","Grapevines are cultivated in the Caucasus and Middle East"],["1841","A US expedition collects the jade vine in the Philippines"],["1865","Charles Darwin publishes a study of climbing plants"],["1876","Kudzu is introduced to the United States"],["1930s","The US government promotes kudzu to control soil erosion"],["2020s","Scientists study why lianas are increasing in tropical forests"]],
s2:[["The jade vine","The jade vine, Strongylodon macrobotrys, grows in the rainforests of the Philippines and is found nowhere else in the wild. It produces hanging clusters of claw-shaped flowers in an unusual blue-green color. In the wild, the flowers are pollinated by bats, which hang upside down to drink nectar. It is grown in botanical gardens worldwide and is threatened by forest loss in the Philippines."],["Rattan","Rattans are climbing palms with spiny stems that can grow over 100 meters long. The Philippines has many native rattan species. For generations, rattan has been harvested for furniture, baskets and handicrafts, and Cebu became famous for rattan furniture exports. Overharvesting has reduced wild rattan, and the government has regulated its collection and encouraged plantations."],["Kudzu, the vine that ate the South","Kudzu, a fast-growing vine from East Asia, was introduced to the United States in 1876 as an ornamental plant and was later planted widely to control soil erosion. In the warm, humid American South, it spread rapidly, smothering trees, fields and buildings and earning the nickname the vine that ate the South. It is now considered an invasive weed."],["Darwin and climbing plants","Charles Darwin was fascinated by how climbing plants move. He grew them in his greenhouse and watched their tips trace slow circles in the air. In 1865 he published a study describing how plants find and grasp supports. His work showed that plants are capable of complex, purposeful-looking movements."],["Vegetables on vines","Many Filipino vegetables grow on vines trained up bamboo trellises, including ampalaya, upo, patola, sitaw and chayote, known as sayote. Trellising saves space, keeps fruits off the ground and makes harvesting easier, an important practice in backyard gardens and small farms."],["Strangler figs","Some figs start as seeds dropped by birds high in a tree. They send roots down to the ground and gradually wrap around the host tree, sometimes killing it and leaving a hollow lattice of fig roots. In the Philippines, these balete trees are often linked to folklore about spirits and supernatural beings."]],
sp:["The bat-pollinated jewel","Deep in a Philippine rainforest, a vine twists high into the trees. At certain times of the year, it produces long clusters of flowers that hang down like chandeliers, each flower shaped like a claw and colored an astonishing shade of turquoise. Few flowers in the world are this color. At night, a bat arrives, hangs upside down from the flower cluster and pushes its head into a flower to lap the sweet nectar. As it does so, the flower’s structure presses pollen onto the bat’s head. When the bat visits another jade vine, it delivers the pollen. This is the jade vine, one of the Philippines’ most beautiful native plants."],
g:[["Vine","A plant that climbs or trails"],["Tendril","A thin, coiling plant part that grasps supports"],["Liana","A woody climbing plant of tropical forests"],["Trellis","A frame that supports climbing plants"]],
f:["The jade vine is native to the Philippines.","Rattans are climbing palms.","Kudzu is invasive in the United States."],
fun:"A climbing plant’s tendril can curl around a support within minutes of touching it.",
tl:"Ang baging ay mga halamang umaakyat sa ibang halaman o bagay upang maabot ang liwanag. Umaakyat sila sa pamamagitan ng pagpulupot, galamay o kawit. Ang jade vine na may kulay-turkesang bulaklak ay katutubo sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"poisonousplants",t:"Poisonous Plants",c:"Plants",i:"☠️",w:"List of poisonous plants",r:["firstaid","medicinalplants","allergy","flower","agri"],
p:[["Chemical defenses","Many plants make poisons to protect themselves."],["Common dangers","Some poisonous plants grow in homes and gardens."],["Healing in small doses","Some plant poisons are also used as medicines."]],
l:"Poisonous plants contain substances that can harm or kill people or animals if eaten, touched or inhaled. Plants cannot run from animals that eat them, so many produce chemicals that make them bitter, irritating or toxic. Some of the world’s most poisonous plants are common ornamental and garden plants, such as oleander, while others are food crops that must be prepared carefully, such as cassava. In the Philippines, the seeds of the tubang-bakod, or physic nut, are a common cause of accidental poisoning in children. Many plant poisons are also used as medicines in small, controlled doses. Learning to recognize poisonous plants, keeping them away from children and pets, and seeking help quickly when poisoning is suspected can prevent serious harm.",
s:[["Why plants make poisons","Plants produce a huge variety of chemicals that discourage insects, mammals and fungi from eating them. These include alkaloids such as nicotine and atropine, cyanide-producing compounds, sharp crystals and substances that affect the heart. Some animals have evolved resistance and can eat plants that are poisonous to others."],["Household and garden plants","Common ornamental plants can be dangerous. Oleander, widely planted along roads and in gardens, contains heart toxins in all its parts. Dieffenbachia, or dumb cane, contains needle-like crystals that cause painful swelling of the mouth and throat if chewed. Lilies are especially dangerous to cats."],["Food plants","Some foods are safe only when properly prepared. Raw cassava contains compounds that release cyanide and must be peeled, soaked and cooked. Raw kidney beans contain a toxin destroyed by thorough boiling. Potato sprouts and green potato skins contain solanine. Bitter almonds and some fruit seeds also contain cyanide-producing compounds."],["Touching plants","Some plants harm through contact. Poison ivy and its relatives produce an oily substance called urushiol that causes itchy rashes; mango sap contains related chemicals that can irritate sensitive skin. The Australian stinging tree, gympie-gympie, has tiny hairs that cause pain lasting weeks."]],
k:[["Common Philippine danger","Tubang-bakod seeds"],["Heart toxins in","Oleander and foxglove"],["Cyanide risk","Raw cassava"],["Rash-causing substance","Urushiol"],["Famous poison","Hemlock"]],
d:[["399 BC","Socrates is executed with a drink of poison hemlock"],["1785","William Withering describes foxglove as a heart medicine"],["1888","Ricin is identified in castor beans"],["1978","A ricin pellet is used in the assassination of Georgi Markov in London"],["2000s","Physic nut is promoted as a biofuel crop in the Philippines"],["2020s","Poison control centers continue to warn about plant poisonings in children"]],
s2:[["Tubang-bakod","The physic nut, known in the Philippines as tubang-bakod, is a shrub often grown as a living fence and was promoted as a biofuel crop in the 2000s. Its seeds look and taste somewhat like nuts, so children sometimes eat them, causing vomiting, diarrhea and dehydration. Clusters of poisoning cases among schoolchildren have been reported. Doctors advise seeking medical care and keeping the seeds away from children."],["Saga and castor beans","The bright red and black seeds of the rosary pea, called saga in the Philippines, are used in jewelry and crafts but contain abrin, a powerful toxin if the seeds are chewed or broken. Castor beans contain ricin, one of the most toxic natural substances. Castor oil, extracted from the seeds, is safe because the toxin does not pass into the oil."],["Poison and medicine","The difference between a poison and a medicine is often the dose. Digoxin from foxglove treats heart failure, atropine from deadly nightshade is used in surgery and to treat some poisonings, and vincristine from the periwinkle treats cancer. In 1785 the English doctor William Withering described how carefully measured foxglove doses could treat heart problems."],["Poisons in history","Plant poisons have been used for hunting, warfare and executions. In 399 BC the Greek philosopher Socrates was put to death by drinking poison hemlock. Indigenous peoples in South America used curare from plants on blow darts, and peoples of Southeast Asia, including some in the Philippines, used plant poisons on arrows and darts for hunting."],["If poisoning happens","If someone may have eaten a poisonous plant, health experts advise removing any remaining plant material from the mouth, keeping a sample of the plant for identification, and calling a poison control center or going to the nearest hospital immediately. They warn against inducing vomiting unless instructed by a medical professional. In the Philippines, the National Poison Management and Control Center at the Philippine General Hospital provides advice."],["Animals and plants","Pets and livestock can also be poisoned by plants. Dogs and cats can be harmed by lilies, sago palms and some houseplants. Farmers watch for toxic weeds in pastures. Some animals, such as koalas eating eucalyptus and monarch caterpillars eating milkweed, are specially adapted to toxic plants and even use the toxins for defense."]],
sp:["The doctor and the foxglove","In the 1770s, the English doctor William Withering heard of an old folk remedy used by a woman in Shropshire to treat dropsy, a swelling caused by heart failure. The recipe contained about 20 herbs. Withering realized that the active ingredient was likely foxglove, a common but poisonous garden plant. Over nearly ten years, he carefully tested different doses on patients, noting how much helped and how much caused harmful side effects. In 1785 he published his results, one of the earliest careful studies of a medicine’s dose. Medicines derived from foxglove are still used for some heart conditions today."],
g:[["Toxin","A poisonous substance produced by a living thing"],["Alkaloid","A type of plant chemical that often affects the body"],["Urushiol","An oily substance that causes rashes"],["Dose","The amount of a substance taken"]],
f:["Oleander is poisonous in all its parts.","Raw cassava must be cooked to be safe.","Foxglove gave us a heart medicine."],
fun:"Castor oil is safe to use even though castor beans contain one of the most toxic natural substances.",
tl:"May mga halamang may lason na nakasasama kapag kinain o nahawakan. Karaniwang sanhi ng pagkalason ng mga bata sa Pilipinas ang buto ng tubang-bakod. Ang ilang lason ng halaman ay ginagamit ding gamot sa maliit na dosis."});

;
